2 CONT Soldier in King Philip's War
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert doesn't have an original headstone, and the one on his findagrave page is a modern headstone. His burial spot is only speculation.
2 CONT
2 CONT Most Quakers are buried with their families, but some opted to not have a headstone. Other died on mission. Sometimes you will find where the people who they were closest to mark their graves with simple brick type stone. Also, la
2 CONC ter descendants will mark their graves with the elaborate stones they wished to avoid!
2 CONT
2 CONT Historically, Quakers did not have headstones as they wanted the graves to have an equality to them that would be disturbed by the presence of large, ornate headstones (that only few could afford).
2 CONT
2 CONT Quakers, following their tenet of simplicity, historically avoided elaborate headstones or grave markers, believing that such displays could create distinctions and inequality among the deceased. 
2 CONT While early Quaker burial grounds were largely unmarked, the practice evolved to allow for simple, small stones, often with a slight curve at the top, known as "pillowtop" or "Quaker style" headstones. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Content of Markers:
2 CONT These stones typically included only the deceased's name and dates of birth and death, with no ornamentation or epitaphs. Quaker burial practices also extend to using simple, plain burial containers, such as a simple pine box. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Quaker funerals emphasize silent reflection and allow those present to speak from their hearts as they are moved, rather than a structured service. Quakers do not have priests or preachers and share responsibilities within their c
2 CONC ommunity, so a "Friend" of the church, or a close friend or family member, will lead the funeral service. 
