1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =George Sexton Sr.=
2 CONT '''Religious Refugee, Pioneer, Landowner, and Founder of Westfield'''
2 CONT * BIRTH 1632 Yorkshire, England [3,15]
2 CONT * EMEGRATION 1663 to Windsor, Connecuiit from England [14]
2 CONT * DEATH 30 Oct 1690 (aged 71–72) Westfield, Middlesex County, Connecticut, USA [3,15]
2 CONT * BURIAL Burial Details Unknown 
2 CONT * MEMORIAL ID  279255548  
2 CONT
2 CONT Currently we are not aware of which ship George Sexton b.1632 Ireland d. 31 Oct 1689 Westfield, MA sailed upon. When he fled Ireland he was more than just excaping religious problems, he was a "religious fugitive". He was running 
2 CONC from General Ireton, son in law to Oliver Cromwell, who was raging a war against all Protestants. The town of Limerick, Ireland was the hardest hit, estates were confiscated and divided, heavy taxes, soldiers wiped out many towns,
2 CONC  entire families were being transported to the West Indies, in chains, to pick cotton. Those who were not shipped to the Indies as slaves and could not excape Ireland became slaves on their own lands to newly appointed English mas
2 CONC ters. Our George Sexten was lucky to escape alive.
2 CONT
2 CONT George Sexton Sr. stands as the likely emigrant progenitor of the Sexton family in America, even though no definitive record of his arrival exists. Based on the ages of his children, he was born around 1618.  Research by Ray G. Hu
2 CONC lburt shows that a George Sexton, believed to have been a sea captain or trader, firmly established his roots in New England by acquiring significant tracts of land that would form the nucleus of Westfield, Massachusetts.  After e
2 CONC migrating from England between 1635 and 1645, he settled in Windsor, Connecticut, where he quickly became an active participant in land transactions and community affairs. In Windsor, he married Katherine (or Catherine), and toget
2 CONC her they built a family whose legacy would extend across generations.
2 CONT
2 CONT He then moved to Westfield, Massachusetts, where he made a lasting impact. On 10 June 1663, he purchased two parcels of meadow and upland at "Waronoco" (the original name for Westfield) from Thomas Cooper (or Cowper). On period ma
2 CONC ps, his tract appears where "Land" is printed, stretching from just above the "Warrinoke River" (now the Westfield River) to "Brook." He later received additional acreage just below the river, establishing himself as one of Westfi
2 CONC eld's first three settlers.  He acquired this land from a transaction that had its own storied past. Thomas Cooper had secured the property by deed from the local Indian chief, Amoakussen. The deed—recorded as the first in the cou
2 CONC nty records and dated 19 September 1660—declares:
2 CONT
2 CONT "for security of such debt or debts as lye said Amokussen doe own unto the said Thomas Cooper or shall hereafter owe unto him, and that in case I by any providence be taken away, then shall this said Land fall into ye hands & be a
2 CONC t the full enjoymt of the said Thomas Cooper or his heires or assignes forever & without molestation from any: And hereunto have I sett my hand this ninteenth day of September, 1660: I doe acknowledge my selfe indebted unto Thomas
2 CONC  Cooper the summe of 12 pounds..."
2 CONT
2 CONT George actively built the community. Local historian Stephen J. Pitoniakin notes in Western Massachusetts History, the Westfield Area that George Sexton Sr. built the first home in Westfield along the old road from Northampton to 
2 CONC Windsor (today's Little River Street). He positioned his home just south of a prominent Ingersoll homestead on the road to Shakersville. His house not only served as a family residence but also hosted early religious services befo
2 CONC re the community had a dedicated church. Remarkably, nearly a century later, local Separates used the same building for worship.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Westfield genealogist L. M. Dewey recalled that "the house was strongly built and was used as a fort and refuge from Indians," highlighting its vital role during turbulent times.  While he initially settled in Westfield, records 
2 CONC show that he later moved back to Windsor—possibly between 1663 and 1666, based on the recorded birthplaces of his children. In Westfield, on 24 September 1678, he and his wife faced the county court for "abusive words and actions"
2 CONC  toward Samuel Root, the local constable, which reflects the challenges of frontier life. Other documents place him in Hartford on 16 October 1678 and again in Westfield by 25 March 1681.
2 CONT
2 CONT In a later transaction on 25 January 1687/88, George and his wife Catherine conveyed their Westfield property—including their home and surrounding lands—to their sons, Joseph and Benjamin, for £160. Catherine died on 19 September 
2 CONC 1689, and George Sexton Sr. passed away about a year later—sometime before 30 October 1690—leaving an estate valued at £49 and 10 shillings. This arrangement is confirmed by an instrument dated 31 October 1690, where his son recor
2 CONC ds: "signed by George Sexton, Jr., 'sometime of Westfield, but now of Newtown, on Long Island, son and heire to George Sexton of Westfield, lately deceased, consenting to the before mentioned writing made by my honored father'" to
2 CONC  my brothers Joseph and Benjamin, and dated 31 Oct., 1690.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Parents'''
2 CONT James Sexton 1604-1669
2 CONT Joan Ellis Sexton 1608-1640
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Siblings'''
2 CONT Richard Sexton
2 CONT Symon Sexton
2 CONT Patrick Sexton
2 CONT Joseph Sexton
2 CONT Stephen Sexton
2 CONT Joan Sexton
2 CONT John Sexton
2 CONT Francis Sexton
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Spouse'''
2 CONT Catherine/Katherine K. Bird Cowing Sexton 1631–19 SEP 1689 Westfield, Hampden, MA m. 10 JUN 1663 Windsor, Hartford, CT. [3]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Children''' 6x first 3 born in Limerick, Ireland
2 CONT George Sexton Jr. 1658-1689
2 CONT James Sexton Sr. 1660-1741
2 CONT Daniel Sexton 1662-1710 
2 CONT Capt. Joseph Sexton 1664-1742
2 CONT Benjamin Sexton 1667-1754
2 CONT John Sexton 1673-?
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Descendant Details==
2 CONT * George Sexton Jr. b. 1656 Limerick, Ireland 1st married Hannah Spencer, 9 Sept 1679 Hartford, CT and later had a 2nd marriage with Sarah Bearding.  In 1685, George Jr. actively contributed to Westfield's growth by participating 
2 CONC in the construction of a sawmill on the brook on the northeast side of the Westfield River—a key project for local industry and community development.  He died around 1690 in Newtown, Long Island, NY (at sea?)
2 CONT * James Sexton Sr. b. 28 Jan 1661 Limerick, Ireland m.: 1st- 9 Apr 1680 - Hannah Fowler m.: 2nd- Anne (Bancroft) Gilbert in Springfield, CT m.: 3rd- 19 Jan 1734 Mary (Burbank/Miller) MacCranney.  He d. 12 Dec 1741 Westfield, MA 
2 CONT * Daniel Sexton 1662-1710 m. Sarah Bancroft on 28 Dec 1680; later moved to Jamaica, Long Island, had six children.
2 CONT * Capt. Joseph Sexton 3 Feb 1666 in Windsor, CT m. Hannah Wright, 20 Nov 1690; lived in Enfield, CT six children, d. 8 May 1742 Westfield, MA
2 CONT * Benjamin Sexton 10 Dec 1667 Westfield, MA d. 8 Apr 1754 Westfield, m. Mary (Strong) Pixley, 13 Jul 1717, became known as the first white child born in Westfield where he remained, age 88.
2 CONT * John Sexton b. 26 May 1673 in Windsor or Westfield CT. d. ? M. ? lived in Boston, MA (some suggest died early.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Notes==
2 CONT It is often stated that our Sextons came over on the "Blessing". However there is no evidence that the Blessing sailed at the time George arrived. Currently we can not be sure of which ship George Sexten b.1632 Ireland d. 31 Oct 1
2 CONC 689 Westfield, MA sailed upon. When he fled Ireland for England he was more than just excaping religious problems, he was a "religious fugitive". He was running from General Ireton, son in law to Oliver Cromwell, who was raging a 
2 CONC war against all Protestants. The town of Limerick, Ireland was the hardest hit, estates were confiscated and divided, heavy taxes, soldiers wiped out many towns, entire families were being transported to the West Indies, in chains
2 CONC , to pick cotton. Those who were not shipped to the Indies as slaves and could not excape Ireland became slaves on their own lands to newly appointed English masters.
2 CONT
2 CONT Other figures occasionally appear in records whose connections to the Sexton family remain tentative:
2 CONT * Sir George Sexton: Associated with Irish records and noted as secretary to the Lord Deputy; his exact relationship to the American Sextons is not firmly established.
2 CONT * James Sexton: Mentioned in documents from Limerick, Ireland; his connection to George Sexton Sr. is speculative.
2 CONT * Richard Sexton (Saxton): Recorded in early Windsor, Connecticut (as early as 1643); he might have been a cousin or distant relative rather than a direct sibling.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==References==
