1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =George Sexton Jr.=
2 CONT * BIRTH 1658 Limerick, Ireland
2 CONT * DEATH 19 Sep 1689 (aged 30–31) Huntington, Suffolk County, New York, USA
2 CONT * RESIDENCE 1690 Newton, Long Island ?
2 CONT * BURIAL Unknown Lost at Sea
2 CONT * MEMORIAL ID 277101600
2 CONT
2 CONT George Sexton Jr. was born in 1658 in Hartford, Connecticut, the son of George Sexton Sr. and Katherine (Catherine) Cowing. George Jr. married Hannah Spencer 9 Sept 1679 Hartford, CT,  She was born 15 Apr 1653 in Haddam, Middlesex
2 CONC , CT, to Thomas Spencer and Sarah Bearding, and died 1 Apr 1715 in Haddam, Middlesex, CT.   While a widower had a 2nd marriage with Sarah Bearding. In 1685.  Marriage index records indicate George Jr.'s parents were married in 165
2 CONC 8 in CT, thus affirming George Jr. must be born aft. abt. 1658. George Jr.'s uncle appears to be Richard Saxton (Sexton).
2 CONT
2 CONT The ancestry of Ray G. Hulburt notes that a George Sexton, a sea captain or trader with the West Indies Company, owned lands at Westfield, Massachusetts, and was established in various locales during his lifetime. Records show tha
2 CONC t George Sr. resided in Hartford on 16 October 1678 and Westfield on 25 March 1681, later appearing at Newton, Long Island, by 31 October 1690.  He actively participated in the construction of a sawmill on the brook on the northea
2 CONC st side of the Westfield River—a key project for local industry and community development. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Earlier deeds attest to the family's colonial roots: on 10 June 1663, a George Sexton—described as a resident of Windsor—was recorded in the book of deeds at Springfield, Massachusetts. This early entry evidences the Sextons as so
2 CONC me of the first permanent settlers of Westfield between 1656 and 1663. Later, on 25 January 1687/8, lands in Westfield were conveyed by George Sexton (Sr.) to his sons, underscoring the prominence of the family in local affairs.  
2 CONC He is recorded executing a quit claim deed to his brothers on 31 October 1690 after fathers death.
2 CONT
2 CONT George Sexton Jr. himself was noted in records as being of Hartford and Westfield; he later settled at Newton in Long Island. This is confirmed by a deed recorded at Springfield (Vol. II, 73), George and Catherine Sexton convey la
2 CONC nd in Westfield to their sons Joseph and Benjamin, in consideration of the sum of £160, dated 25 Jan., 1687/8, and, by an instrument "signed by George Sexton, Jr., "sometime of Westfield, but now of Newtown, on Long Island, son an
2 CONC d heire to George Sexton of Westfield, lately deceased, consenting to the before mentioned writing made by my honored father " to my brothers Joseph and Benjamin, and dated 31 Oct., 1690, it appears that George Sexton, Sen'r, died
2 CONC  between 1688 and 1690.
2 CONT
2 CONT George Sexton Jr. died on 19 September 1689 in Huntington, Suffolk County, New York. Some say he died at sea as a whaler trading with the West Indies Company.  Following his death, his widow Hannah Spencer eventually returned to H
2 CONC artford; she remarried on 29 November 1698 to Daniel Brainerd of Haddam, Connecticut, as his third wife. Hannah should not be confused with Daniel's first wife, also named Hannah Spencer, who was Gerrard Spencer's daughter. Thus, 
2 CONC the two women named Hannah Spencer should not be confused.
2 CONT
2 CONT The ancestry of the SEXTON FAMILY* is fully set forth in Stiles' "Ancient Windsor." The first of the name who came to live in Dutchess county was the late Samuel J. M. Sexton, of Hyde Park. His line of descent from the first Georg
2 CONC e Sexton, of Windsor, Conn., and Catharine, is: Capt. Joseph (1666- 1742) and Hannah Wright; Joseph, Jr. (1694- 17—), and Sarah Parsons; Deacon Joseph (1726-1819) and Rebecca Chapin; Joseph ( I 753-I 823) and Hannah Cadwell, of Wi
2 CONC lbraham and Monsoon, Mass., whose eldest son was Francis Sexton, born at Wilbraham, November 22, 1779; a merchant in New York, where he died August 7, 1839; he lived many years at No. 28 Dey street, where all his children were bor
2 CONC n. He married, December 14, 1809, Sarah Mills, born March 10, 1792, died September 21, 1862, daughter of William Ross and Johanna, his wife, daughter of Capt. Alexander Leslie (who was lost at sea) and Sarah Tufts (who married, se
2 CONC cond, John Mills, of New York).
2 CONT
2 CONT *The arms of Sexton, called "of London," by Burke, are: "Argent, three chaplets in bend gules, between two bendlets of the last: Crest, out of a ducal coronet or, a dexter arm in armour embowed proper, garnished of the first, hold
2 CONC ing in the gauntlet an anchor sable, fluke and cable or." An ancient seal engraved with the above crest has come down, as an heirloom, to the present S. B. Sexton, of " Torham; " and it furnished an appropriate design for his priv
2 CONC ate yacht signal. The British Herald; Or, Cabinet of Armorial Bearings of the Nobility, By Thomas Robson, describes: Sexton [London] ar. three chaplets, n bend, gu. betw. two bendlets of the last - Crest, out of a cucal coronet or
2 CONC , a dexter arm, in armour, embowed, ppr. garnished of the first, holding in the cauntlet an anchor sa. flue and cable or.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Parents'''
2 CONT George Sexton 1618–1690
2 CONT Catherine Sexton unknown–1689
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Siblings'''
2 CONT Joseph Sexton, b. 1660.
2 CONT Benjamin Sexton
2 CONT Daniel Sexton, m. 9 Dec, 1680, Sarah Bancroft
2 CONT John Sexton, b. 26 May. 1678
2 CONT James Sexton, m. 1st Hannah Fowler, m. 2nd Aunah Gilbert.
2 CONT Gershom Sexton ?
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Spouse'''
2 CONT Hannah Spencer Sexton 1653–1715 m. 9 Sept 1679 Hartford, CT
2 CONT Sarah Bearding Sexton m. 1685
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Children'''
2 CONT Charles Sexton 1680–1752
2 CONT
2 CONT ==References==
2 CONT 1 Pioneers of Old Hopewell, By Ralph Ege, 1908
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/277101600/george-sexton
