1 OCCU Farmer & Surveyor - laid out plan of Springfield, MA
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Captain John Pease, first settler of Enfield. Ancestor of Texas Gov. Elisha M. Pease.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Birth:	Feb 11 1632 - Great Baddow, Chelmsford Dist., Essex, England
2 CONT * Death:	July 8 1689 - Enfield, Hartford Co., Connecticut, New England
2 CONT * Parents:	Robert Pease, Lydia
2 CONT * Wife:	Mary Goodell, Ann Cummings
2 CONT
2 CONT John immigrated to America in 1639 with his Grandmother to join his father, Robert Pease, who had immigrated in 1634. He was one of the founders of Enfield, CT. The first notice of him is in the court records of Salem, Mass. in th
2 CONC e settlement of his father's estate, Aug. 27, 1644.  From that time forward, there is no problem tracing him.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a favorite of his grandmother, Margaret Pease, as shown by her will which was brought into court soon after action was taken in the settlement of his father's estate. It seems probable that he may have been adopted by his g
2 CONC randmother after his father's death. She doubtless has full authority for placing him in the care of Thomas Watson, to dispose of him as her own child, rendering it unnecessary for the court to take any action in relation to his c
2 CONC ustody, as in the case of his older brother.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married first Mary Goddell, daugther of Robert Goddell of Salem, who died January 5, 1669, three days after the birth of her fifth child. He married second, Anne Cummings, daughter of Isaac Cummings of Topsfield, Mass. Dec. 8, 
2 CONC 1669, and who died at Enfield June 29, 1689. He settled as a "yeoman" in that part of Enfield called "Northfields" which is now within the city limits.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is found frequently in the Salem town records, as grantor or grantee, a witness in deeds, overseer of wills, constable, etc. He was made a freeman April 29, 1668, by the general court, and took the oath before the County Court 
2 CONC June 30, following. "October 6, 1681, on sacrament day, John Pease and wife had a letter of recommendation granted to the church at Springfield." Not far from this last date, it is supposed he with his family, his two eldest sons'
2 CONC   families  and numerous neighbors, removed to Fresh Water Brook, then a part of Springfield, where he and his two oldest sons had had land granted them July 23, of the previous year. We find him back at Salem in 1682 when his sol
2 CONC d his house, barn, and out buildings and parcels of land and acknowledged the deed before a Salem justice of the peace.He says in the deed "he was late of Salem, now Enfield." He was there again in Feb. 26, 1683, to give evidence 
2 CONC in a lawsuit involving a boundary line of land once owned and laid out by "father-in-law" Goddell. He then stated he was aged about 53 years old. In September 1684, he was appointed administrator on the estate of Lot Killam, a tow
2 CONC nman of his in Salem, it is said, died "at the new settlement."
2 CONT
2 CONT His relation to the church on his removal to Enfield was an active one, and he stood foremost to assist in maintaining the worship of God on the Sabbath, in the town of Enfield, after it became incorporated. * See Appendix G : htt
2 CONC ps://archive.org/details/genealogicalhist00peas/page/n31
2 CONT
2 CONT He sickened in the midst of making preparations for building, and died "suddenly" July 8, 1689. Death made sad havoc in this family for a short time; taking first the mother, ten days after the father, and one day after that a dau
2 CONC ghter, Abigail.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''His children were:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT # John b. at Salem, May 30, 1654
2 CONT # Robert b. at Salem, May 14, 1656
2 CONT # Mary b. at Salem, Oct. 8, 1658
2 CONT # Abraham, b. at Salem, June 5, 1662
2 CONT # Johnathan, b. at Salem, January 2, 1669
2 CONT
2 CONT '''By his second wife:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT # James, b. at Salem, Dec. 23, 1670
2 CONT # Isaac, b. at Salem, July 15, 1672
2 CONT # Abigail, b. at Salem, Dec. 15, 1675. Died at Enfield July 9, 1689.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Family==
2 CONT
2 CONT With 1st wife Mary Goodale (married 1653) had 5 children, all born at Salem:
2 CONT
2 CONT # John.
2 CONT # Robert. 
2 CONT # Mary.  Married Hugh Pasco.
2 CONT # Abraham.
2 CONT # Jonathan.
2 CONT
2 CONT With 2nd wife Ann Cummings (married 8 Oct 1669) had 3 children, all born at Salem:
2 CONT #  James. b: 23 Dec 1670 in Salem, Essex Co., MA c: Dec 1670 in Salem, MA.  Married Hannah Harmon.
2 CONT #  Isaac. b: 15 Jul 1672 in Salem, MA.  Married Mindwell Osborn.
2 CONT #  Abigail PEASE b: Birth: 15 DEC 1675 in Salem, MA.  
2 CONT
2 CONT # James Pease
2 CONT # Isaac Pease
2 CONT # Abigail Pease
2 CONT # Ann Pease
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Biography==
2 CONT
2 CONT The progenitor of the Enfield Peases. He evidently came to Salem with his mother and grandmother Margaret circa 1639. The first court record of John was in the settlement of his father's estate under the date of 27 Aug 1644. He ap
2 CONC pears to have been a favorite of his grandmother as shown by her will which was brought into court soon after action was taken in the settlement of his father's estate. It seems that he was adopted by his grandmother before his fa
2 CONC ther's decease and she had full authority for placing him in the care of Thomas Watson to raise as his own child. John settled as a farmer in that part of Salem called Northfields, a short distance from the farm of William Pynchon
2 CONC . John's name is found several times in the Essex and Salem records as grantee, grantor, witness, and etc. He was made a freeman 29 Apr 1668 by the General court. He joined the first church of Salem on 4 Jul 1667. On 23 Jul 1680 J
2 CONC ohn was granted land in Enfield. On 6 Oct 1681 John and his wife had a letter of recommendation granted to the church at Springfield. John was back in Salem on Nov 1682 to sell his property in Salem and again on Feb 1683 to give e
2 CONC vidence in a trial involving  boundary line on land once owned by his father-in-law Goodale. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Capt. John Pease, Sen. of Salem died in Enfield. 
2 CONT
2 CONT * This is on a plaque in front of the Old Town Hall of Enfield 1773. Enfield was settled 1680.  Established as the township of Enfield, 1683, this area was part of the Springfield Plantation granted to William Pynchon and others b
2 CONC y the Mass. General Court. Springfield was settleed in 1636, but no effectual grants were made here until after King Philip's War of 1675-1676. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes:
2 CONT
2 CONT * JOHN, Salem, came in the Francis from Ipswich, Co. Suff. 1634, aged 27, with ch. Robert, 3, and two serv. in co. with his br. Robert. They were prob. from Great Baddow, near Chelmsford in Co. Essex. He perhaps. had gr. of ld. 16
2 CONC 37; was not, we may safely judge, one of the first four sett. of Martha's Vineyard, as by doubtf. Tradit. report. d. a. 1639. His wid. Margaret wh. unit. with the ch. 1639, had come with his s. John prob. in a later sh. d. 1644, a
2 CONC nd in her will of 1 Sept. in that yr. ment. br. Robert, and s. Robert. JOHN, Salem, s. of the preced. by w. Mary had John, b. 30 May 1654; Robert, 14 May 1656; Mary, 8 Oct. 1658; Abraham, 5 June 1662; and Jonathan, 2 Jan.1668. his
2 CONC  w. d. 3 days aft. and he m. 8 Dec. 1669, Ann Cummings, prob. d. of Isaac the first of Ipswich, had James, 23 Dec. 1670; Isaac, 15 July 1672; and Abigail, 15 Dec. 1675. He was of ar. co. 1661, freem. 1668, was a capt. and with man
2 CONC y neighb. obt. gr. of ld. at Springfield a. 1681, that part wh. bec. Enfield 1683; there his w. d. 29 June 1689, and he d. 8 July fol. and his d. Abigail, d. the next day. All the six s. outliv. him, and descent. are very num. Ano
2 CONC . John, and Lucy, perhaps his w. are ment. by Felt, II. 578, as favarers of Gorton in 1644. To this John belongs the earlier Distinct. for a worse heresy, as the Court's orde in Nov. 1636, proves, "that he shall be whipt, and boun
2 CONC d to his good behav. for strik, his mo. Mrs. Weston, and deriding of her, and other ill carriage." See Col. Rec. I. 155.197, v3p380
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT # NEHGR VOL: 3 'The Pease Family'  Frederick S Pease
2 CONT #  Isaac Cummings, of Topsfield, Mass., and Some of His Descendants.  Topsfield Historical Society 1899. Page 6
2 CONT
2 CONT Links:
2 CONT
2 CONT * http://wc.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=asorofman&id=I630
2 CONT * http://genforum.genealogy.com/pease/messages/2703.html
2 CONT * [http://photos.geni.com/p13/cf/57/d3/d0/5344483bde91b30d/three_mary_peases_original.pdf "Three Mary Peases of Salem, Massachusetts"] by Ian Watson.  TAG: 70 (1995) 205-208
2 CONT * https://minerdescent.com/2010/08/25/john-pease/
2 CONT * Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/94090214/john-pease Find-A-Grave] Immigrated to America in 1639 with his Grandmother to join his father, Robert Pease, who had immigrated in 1634. Was one of the founders of Enfiel
2 CONC d, CT.
2 CONT
