1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [http://www.lds.org/churchhistory/library/pioneerdetails/1,15791,4018-1-46433,00.html  	Mormon Pioneer Overland Travel, 1847–1868 James Pace Company (1850)]  Age 22
2 CONT
2 CONT Departure: 11 June 1850 Arrival: 20-23 September 1850
2 CONT
2 CONT Company Information: 100 wagons were in the company when it began its journey from the outfitting post at Kanesville, Iowa (present day Council Bluffs). 
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=31038720 Find a Grave]
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth: 	Jan. 30, 1828 Wayne County New York, USA
2 CONT
2 CONT Death: 	Nov. 17, 1920 Payson Utah County Utah, USA
2 CONT
2 CONT John Courtland Searle was born in Butler, Wayne, New York. His parents were Breed Searle and Rebecca Ann Saxton. He married Jerusha Morrison Hill 20 Mar 1850 in Salt Lake City, Salt Lake, Utah. They were the parents of twelve chil
2 CONC dren.
2 CONT
2 CONT (From PETEETNEET TOWN, A History of Payson, Utah, by Madoline Cloward Dixon) "The first settlers of the new colony at Peteetneet were sixteen in number. They included James Pace, his wife, Lucinda Gibson, and their children: Willi
2 CONC am Byron, who at age 15 had served with his father in the Mormon Battalion, Mary Ann, Warren Sidney, Martha Elmina, John E. and Amanda L. Pace. A second family was composed of Andrew Jackson Stewart, his wife, Eunice Haws, and the
2 CONC ir children, Sarah, Catherine and Andrew Jackson Jr. A young couple, John Courtland Searle and his wife, Jerusha Morrison Hill were there too. Their baby would be the first white child to be born in the new settlement (and also th
2 CONC e first to die). Also in the group were two youths, Allison Hill (14), who was a brother-in-law of John C. Searle, and Nathaniel Haws (18), brother of Eunice Haws Stewart."
2 CONT
2 CONT View online death certificate
2 CONT
