1 NOTE {geni:about_me} According to the Descendants of the Founders of Ancient Windsor, Inc., Richard Sexton was a founder of Windsor. He may have been the son of James Sexton of Limerick, Ireland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Sexton, age 14, was found on the roll of the vessel, BLESSING, leaving London July 1635 arriving in Mass. Bay.
2 CONT
2 CONT On April 16, 1646, he married Sarah Cooke in Windsor.
2 CONT
2 CONT A Digest of the Early Conn. Probate Records, Vol. 1, 150, P. 167 of Probate Records: Sexton, Richard Sen., Windsor, Invt. 107-14-00 was taken June 3, 1662 by Benjamin Newberry & John Moore. Also due to his estate, 15-10-00 in addi
2 CONC tion to 110 lbs. of Hops and Fourty Barrels.
2 CONT
2 CONT Court Record, p. 179 on Sept. 11, 1662. Court of Assistants at Windsor: Inventory exhibited. Administration of estate to the relict, and John Macord was desired to assist her. This Court having seen ye order sent to Deac. John Moo
2 CONC re by good wife Lynsley, do order that 10 pounds be paid out of Richard Sexton's Estate to make up what is due upon a Mortgage to the said Sarah Lyndsley 
2 CONT
2 CONT Resided:
2 CONT
2 CONT "as early as 1643" according to Hinman The road to Poquonnoc above the old mill at just about the place where the present road from the bridge near the 1st Congregational Meeting house comes in was anciently intersected at right a
2 CONC ngles by a highway running about southwest from the Rivulet On this highway we find the residences of...
2 CONT
2 CONT ...South of the highway was the lot of HUMPHREY HYDE Sold to Simon Mills he to William Filly and he to Richard Saxton who lived there .
2 CONT
2 CONT ...There goeth out of the Palisado towards north west a highway two rods wide when past the house plots it is larger Next south of this road on the west side of the Palisado and on the property now occupied by Dr William S Pierson
2 CONC  was the home lot of MICHAEL TRY who sold it to Richard Saxton and he to Thomas Parsons who lived therein 1654...
2 CONT
2 CONT The history of ancient Windsor, Connecticut: including East Windsor, South ... By Henry Reed Stiles https://books.google.com/books?id=Qg0WAAAAYAAJ&dq=francis%20saxton%20windsor&ie=ISO-8859-1&pg=PA140#v=onepage&q=francis%20saxton%2
2 CONC 0windsor&f=false
2 CONT
2 CONT http://jerrymilojohnson.com/ancestryproject/genealogy/getperson.php?personID=I21697&tree=mytree
2 CONT
2 CONT https://archive.org/details/variousancestral01star - pg 133 According to  the Descendants of the Founders of Ancient Windsor, Inc., Richard Sexton was a founder of Windsor.  He may have been the son of James Sexton of Limerick, Ir
2 CONC eland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Sexton, age 14, was found on the roll of the vessel, BLESSING, leaving London July 1635 arriving in Mass. Bay.
2 CONT
2 CONT On April 16, 1646, he married Sarah Cooke in Windsor.
2 CONT
2 CONT A Digest of the Early Conn. Probate Records, Vol. 1, 150, P. 167 of Probate Records: Sexton, Richard Sen., Windsor, Invt. 107-14-00 was taken June 3, 1662 by Benjamin Newberry & John Moore. Also due to his estate, 15-10-00 in addi
2 CONC tion to 110 lbs. of Hops and Fourty Barrels.
2 CONT
2 CONT Court Record, p. 179 on Sept. 11, 1662. Court of Assistants at Windsor:  Inventory exhibited.  Administration of estate to the relict, and John Macord was desired to assist her.  This Court having seen ye order sent to Deac. John 
2 CONC Moore by good wife Lynsley, do order that 10 pounds be paid out of Richard Sexton's Estate to make up what is due upon a Mortgage to the said Sarah Lyndsley .* Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/54024248/richard-sexto
2 CONC n Find A Grave Memorial] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Sep 3 2019, 20:32:24 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT "Emigrated from England to New England on board the ship "Blessing" 13 Jul. 1635 at the age of 14 years.  Had six daughters, thus this male line was extinct."
