1 NOTE {geni:about_me} A Patriot of the American Revolution for CONNECTICUT with the rank of PRIVATE.  [http://services.dar.org/public/dar_research/search_adb/?action=full&p_id=A033758  DAR Ancestor #  A033758]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT  DOUGLAS, JOHN DAR Ancestor #: A033758 Service:  CONNECTICUT    Rank: PRIVATE Birth:  4-18-1724    NEW LONDON NEW LONDON CO CONNECTICUT Death:  12-13-1777     COLCHESTER HARTFORD CO CONNECTICUT Service Source:  JOHNSTON, CT MEN IN
2 CONC  THE REV, P 611 Service Description:  1) CAPT ELIPHALET HOLMES 
2 CONT
2 CONT (f/g) Capt John Douglas Birth: 	Apr. 8, 1724 New London New London County Connecticut, USA Death: 	Dec. 13, 1777 New London County Connecticut, USA
2 CONT
2 CONT Capt. John DOUGLAS, born in New London, Conn, April 8, 1724; married, May 11, 1746, Esther, daughter of Clement LEACH, of New London. They at first lived in the village, but in 1760 removed to the Douglas farm on the Colchester ro
2 CONC ad, four miles west of the town plot. They are said to have been "pious and respectable people." Their children were remarkable for their tallness, nearly all of them being six feet or more in height. The following particulars are
2 CONC  taken from a MS account of the family written in 1805 by one of Capt. John's children, Daniel DOUGLAS, then in his 38th year.
2 CONT
2 CONT "John's father dying when he was a youth, he was apprenticed to Esquire LEE to learn blacksmithing in his native town. His boss dying, he was again bound to Samuel CHIPMAN, of Groton, who had been a fellow-apprentice, and went to 
2 CONC Salsbury, a frontier town, but soon after removed to New London. He there married Esther, oldest daughter of Clement LEACH. She was born at New London in 1727. He sold the inherited property to his brother Robert, build a house an
2 CONC d shop, and carried on blacksmithing on a large scale. He lived here till about 1758, when he sold, and bought a farm adjoining that of his brother Robert. He farmed and blacksmithed and kept a tavern many years. He commanded a co
2 CONC mpany of militia. He was often employed to decide differences; was clerk in the church, and lined the hymns, as the custom was. He died here on the 13th of December, 1777. His widow moved with her son David, in 1803, to Whitestown
2 CONC , NY, where she died, Nov. 6, 1806, aged 76 years.
2 CONT
2 CONT from: "A collection of family records: with biographical sketches, and other memoranda of various families and individuals bearing the name DOUGLAS or allied families of that name." page 76 By Charles Henry James Douglas © 1879 
2 CONT
2 CONT Family links: 
2 CONT  Children:
2 CONT   John Douglass (1749 - 1810)
2 CONT   David Douglass (1758 - 1851)
2 CONT   Joshua Douglass (1760 - 1837)
2 CONT   Daniel Douglass (1771 - 1832)
2 CONT
