1 NOTE {geni:about_me} I
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/54009877/augustus_horton_pease
2 CONT
2 CONT Augustus was the 3rd of 10 children (3rd son) born of Emery Pease (1727-1776) and his wife Mary Horton (1738-1812). Augustus's siblings were: David (b. 1755), Emery (b. 1757), Sylvanus (1761-1817), Mary (b. 1764), Elizabeth (b. 17
2 CONC 66), Gaius (1768-1846), Margaret (b. 1772), Matilda (b. 1774) and Independence (b. 1776).
2 CONT
2 CONT On June 30, 1781, Augustus and 4 other Privates from and for Somers were drafted from their respective Town Militia Companys and joined Captain Samuel Granger's Company of Brigadier-General David Waterbury, Jr's. Brigade of 2 Batt
2 CONC alions of Connecticut State Troops. This Brigade was raised for the defense of the Post at Horseneck and places adjucant, and also all the guards raised for the defense of the sea coasts from Horseneck to New Haven inclusive. In J
2 CONC uly, 1781, the Waterbury's Brigade was joined to General George Washington Army while he was encamped at Phillipsburg, New Jersey and for some time after was under Major-General William Heath's orders on the Westchester line.
2 CONT
2 CONT Augustus married, about 1782, Tirzah Hall (1760-1813) of Somers, Tolland County, Connecticut, by whom he had 10 children (6 sons and 4 daughters): Lucina (b. 1783), Asenath (b. 1785), Augustus (b. 1786), Elizabeth (b. 1788), Abiel
2 CONC  (b. 1790), Calvin (b. 1792), Polly (Mary) (b. 1795), Ethan H. (b. 1796), David (b. 1798) and Alpheus (b. 1800). After his 1st wife died, he married Rebecca David in 1814. There was no issue from this 2nd marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT Soon after Augustus married, he moved from Somers to the West Town part of Andover, Windsor County, Vermont. The part of Andover which was set off on October 26, 1799 and incorporated as the Town of Weston where he lived and died 
2 CONC of a cancer on his face.
2 CONT
2 CONT Augustus was elected one of Weston's first three Selectman and its Treasurer on March 3, 1800 at the first Town Meeting held for the town after it had been set off from the Town of Andover. He was a deacon in the Congregational Ch
2 CONC urch at Weston, a Justice of the Peace, and he was familiarly known as "Capt. Pease," having in his younger days been Captain of a Militia Company.
2 CONT
2 CONT References:
2 CONT
2 CONT (1) "A Genealogical and Historical Record of the Descendants of John Pease, Sen., Last of Enfield, Conn." by David & Austin S. Pease, 1869, pages 27, 69 and 70
2 CONT
2 CONT (2) "The Record of Connecticut Men in the Military and Naval Service During the War of the Revolution 1775-1783." by Henry P. Johnston, 1889, pages 564 and 565
2 CONT
2 CONT (3) "Gazetteer and Business Directory of Windsor County, VT., for 1883-84" by Hamilton Child, 1884, pages 250 and 252
2 CONT Inscription
2 CONT Capt. Augustus
2 CONT Pease died Nov. 15,
2 CONT 1831 in the 73 year.
