1 NOTE increase hild, second child of Ephraim and Mary Lyon Child, was born
2 CONT in Woodstock, Connenticut on 13 Dec. 1740. From papers furnished by
2 CONT one of the descendants of Increase Child we obtained items of his
2 CONT history which reveal a somewhat eventful life, showing manliness,
2 CONT patriotism and personal virtues. Captain Increase as he came to our
2 CONT notice is a lusty, burly youth of an adventurous disposition, not
2 CONT content with the monotony of a home devoid of excitement. Bent upon
2 CONT knowing and seeing what was going on in the world. At scarcely 16
2 CONT years of age, Increase in response to the call for volunteers in the
2 CONT French war, was among the first to be enrolled and served through the
2 CONT seven years of this war. Returning to the old homestead at the close
2 CONT of the war, he tarried but a short time, when he left and went to
2 CONT Dutchess, County, New York and engaged in school teaching. After
2 CONT spending a few years teaching he returned to Woodstock, Connecticut
2 CONT and on 3 Nov. 1762 married Olive Pease of Somers, Connecticut born 10
2 CONT Mar. 1738. They made Woodstock their home for a number of years, where
2 CONT four of their nine children were born. Increase Child took his eldest
2 CONT son Salmon, then a lad, on horseback behind him to Dutchess County,
2 CONT New York where he provided a home and brought his family and settled
2 CONT there. When the revalotionary war broke out he enlisted under General
2 CONT Schuyler as a Captain. He served through the war and obtained an
2 CONT Honorable discharge. He died in Greenfield, Saratoga County, New York
2 CONT on 10 June 1810.
