1 NOTE {geni:about_me} A Patriot of the American Revolution for NEW JERSEY with the rank of CAPTAIN.  [https://services.dar.org/Public/DAR_Research/search_adb/?action=full&p_id=A049891  DAR Ancestor # A049891]
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth:  about 1744 New Jersey, USA  Death:  Aug. 11, 1810 Morris County New Jersey, USA 
2 CONT
2 CONT Josiah Hall was the son of Tobias Hall and his first wife. He was one of the eldest of a large family. When his step-mother died and left the youngest son in need of care, Josiah stepped in and took over that role. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Josiah and Abigail Johnson were married 23 Sep 1764, at Morris Town, NJ, by Rev. Timothy Johnes of 1st Presbyterian Church of Morris town, NJ.
2 CONT
2 CONT Together they had 9 children, 8 of whom were named in his pension record:
2 CONT
2 CONT Sarah (m. Thomas Dickerson, son of Daniel) Elizabeth (m. Stephen Price) Mary (m. John Norris) Ann (m. Mr. Miller) Eunice(m. Aaron Dickerson, son of Daniel) Kesiah (m. Mr. More or Mores) Noah Seth ____(?)
2 CONT
2 CONT At the time of the Revolutionary War, Morris County, New Jersey had a young but committed leader in Josiah Hall. By the fall of 1775, Josiah was a lieutenant, under Capt Robert Gaston, of Sixth Company. By 1777, he had been promot
2 CONC ed to Captain of the militia, Eastern batallion (of Denville).
2 CONT ------------------------------------
2 CONT [From the originator if this memorial "CmC #46739848:"
2 CONT
2 CONT "He served during the Revolutionary War from 1776 until the end. He was a Captain of the Morris County (NJ) militia, Eastern Battalion, under Colonels Jacob Ford, Jr. and Slyvanus Seeley. He served at the Battles of Acquackanonk, 
2 CONC Hackensack and Springfield (all in New Jersey).
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of Tobias Hall, and married Abigail Johnson in 1764."
2 CONT ------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT The written words of his wife, Abigail, in Josiah's military pension papers:
2 CONT
2 CONT ...my husband, the said Josiah Hall, died at his residence, where I now live, on the 11th August, 1810, and I have remained a widow from that period to the present day. ...My husband and I lived in this township & county when the 
2 CONC war began and lived here during his whole life time, except the 12 years before stated, when we lived in the township of Pequanok [sp?], in this county. Sworn...5th May 1837
2 CONT -------------------------------------
