2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT The following stories are taken from a manuscript entitled Stories of Auburn by Jean Davis Fugman, Donald May and Raymond Andrews.     Mrs. Fugman, still an active Auburn resident, very kindly allowed the Board of Trustees to phot
2 CONC ocopy her manuscript. Mrs. Fugman, a local treasure, maintains her keen interest in Auburn and its history and is often present at meetings of the Board of Trustees quietly observing the proceedings.    We are pleased to share som
2 CONC e of her stories with the citizens of Auburn Township.      --------------------------------------------------------------------------------    AUBURN HISTORY      Dear reader, try to visualize in your minds eye the area you know 
2 CONC now as nothing but unbroken forest for miles and miles. Trees reaching to the zenith and so thick the sun only knew a patch here and there. That is what the pioneers found here. Imagine the work to clear a patch of ground to lay u
2 CONC p a cabin and after you move in, discover that a bear has moved in also! How about a few Indians? These Indians wanted food, whisky, guns and the oldest boy and girl to drag over the hill and maybe never hear from again.          
2 CONC   Auburn was not settled until 1815. There were no roads or bridges and then there were the Auburn woods, which were another problem for the pioneers. Travelers were terrified of the woods which harbored wolves, bears, snakes and 
2 CONC goodness knows what else.    Bildad Bradley was born in Massachusetts in 1780 and came to Auburn from South Newbury in 1814, started a house on the north line of the township and later discovered he had the house on the wrong lot 
2 CONC and in 1815, he took it down and moved it further west. This would be somewhere near the bend in Messenger Road. He married Emily Veasy and they had four children. Two died young, one being the first buried in the South Newbury Ce
2 CONC metery. Mr. Bradley helped lay out the State Road and carried the front end of the chain.    John Jackson came to Auburn in 1815 and built a house south of the present I.M. Cavanagh home. In 1816 he built the first frame barn in A
2 CONC uburn.    Zadock Reuwee married Lury Snow in 1814. Lury was a daughter of Oliver Snow, and they came to Auburn in 1815. At this time they had one child named Lorin. Mr. Reuwee built a log house east of the State Road and commended
2 CONC  to clear up his farm. On the 16th of December, 1817, he left home on business, and in his absence, his wife went to the neighbors on an errand leaving the little one in the house asleep. She was gone but a short time, but upon re
2 CONC turning discovered the house was on fire. She reached the house as quickly as possible and some neighbors also arrived but they were too late to rescue the child. The bones were gathered up and buried at the center of the township
2 CONC . Elder Seward of Aurora preached the sermon which was the first funeral in Auburn. Mr. Reuwee was also the first to file a suit from Auburn in the county court.    William Crafts walked to Auburn from Gorham, Ontario County, New 
2 CONC York in 1815, bought the whole Ely tract of 1,176 acres then walked back to Gorham. He was married in January 1816 and they returned to Auburn in February 1816. Their son Jeremiah was the first child born in Auburn. Mr. Crafts lat
2 CONC er built the largest house in the township at that time. This is now the home of the Maxwell family south of Auburn Corners on Route 44.    David Smith Sr. came to Auburn in 1816. He was a soldier in the American Revolution and hi
2 CONC s was the fifth family in Auburn. He was appointed postmaster and served 15 years. He then resigned. The first little school was held in his house in the summer of 1817 and was taught by Mrs. Betsy Orton Wilcox.    Others coming t
2 CONC o Auburn in 1816 were Morgan Orton, Austin Richards, Benjamin Woods and Pardon Wilbur. George Antisdale in 1818, Joseph Bartholomew and Elijah Canfield in 1819, Henry Canfield and J. Palmer Bartholomew in 1821, and Oliver Snow in 
2 CONC 1822. Some stories of these early settlers are very interesting, a few of which follow      *********************************  Additional Notes from Bartholomew Family History  Enlisted at the age of 15 as a drummer boy in the War
2 CONC  of 1812 and was present at the termination of the battle of Sackett's harbor.  He re-moved in 1822 to Auburn, Ohio, where he erected the first blacksmith shop in the county.  The following stories are taken from a manuscript enti
2 CONC tled Stories of Auburn by Jean Davis Fugman, Donald May and Raymond Andrews.     Mrs. Fugman, still an active Auburn resident, very kindly allowed the Board of Trustees to photocopy her manuscript. Mrs. Fugman, a local treasure, m
2 CONC aintains her keen interest in Auburn and its history and is often present at meetings of the Board of Trustees quietly observing the proceedings.    We are pleased to share some of her stories with the citizens of Auburn Township.
2 CONC       --------------------------------------------------------------------------------    AUBURN HISTORY      Dear reader, try to visualize in your minds eye the area you know now as nothing but unbroken forest for miles and miles
2 CONC . Trees reaching to the zenith and so thick the sun only knew a patch here and there. That is what the pioneers found here. Imagine the work to clear a patch of ground to lay up a cabin and after you move in, discover that a bear 
2 CONC has moved in also! How about a few Indians? These Indians wanted food, whisky, guns and the oldest boy and girl to drag over the hill and maybe never hear from again.        Auburn was not settled until 1815. There were no roads o
2 CONC r bridges and then there were the Auburn woods, which were another problem for the pioneers. Travelers were terrified of the woods which harbored wolves, bears, snakes and goodness knows what else.    Bildad Bradley was born in Ma
2 CONC ssachusetts in 1780 and came to Auburn from South Newbury in 1814, started a house on the north line of the township and later discovered he had the house on the wrong lot and in 1815, he took it down and moved it further west. Th
2 CONC is would be somewhere near the bend in Messenger Road. He married Emily Veasy and they had four children. Two died young, one being the first buried in the South Newbury Cemetery. Mr. Bradley helped lay out the State Road and carr
2 CONC ied the front end of the chain.    John Jackson came to Auburn in 1815 and built a house south of the present I.M. Cavanagh home. In 1816 he built the first frame barn in Auburn.    Zadock Reuwee married Lury Snow in 1814. Lury wa
2 CONC s a daughter of Oliver Snow, and they came to Auburn in 1815. At this time they had one child named Lorin. Mr. Reuwee built a log house east of the State Road and commended to clear up his farm. On the 16th of December, 1817, he l
2 CONC eft home on business, and in his absence, his wife went to the neighbors on an errand leaving the little one in the house asleep. She was gone but a short time, but upon returning discovered the house was on fire. She reached the 
2 CONC house as quickly as possible and some neighbors also arrived but they were too late to rescue the child. The bones were gathered up and buried at the center of the township. Elder Seward of Aurora preached the sermon which was the
2 CONC  first funeral in Auburn. Mr. Reuwee was also the first to file a suit from Auburn in the county court.    William Crafts walked to Auburn from Gorham, Ontario County, New York in 1815, bought the whole Ely tract of 1,176 acres th
2 CONC en walked back to Gorham. He was married in January 1816 and they returned to Auburn in February 1816. Their son Jeremiah was the first child born in Auburn. Mr. Crafts later built the largest house in the township at that time. T
2 CONC his is now the home of the Maxwell family south of Auburn Corners on Route 44.    David Smith Sr. came to Auburn in 1816. He was a soldier in the American Revolution and his was the fifth family in Auburn. He was appointed postmas
2 CONC ter and served 15 years. He then resigned. The first little school was held in his house in the summer of 1817 and was taught by Mrs. Betsy Orton Wilcox.    Others coming to Auburn in 1816 were Morgan Orton, Austin Richards, Benja
2 CONC min Woods and Pardon Wilbur. George Antisdale in 1818, Joseph Bartholomew and Elijah Canfield in 1819, Henry Canfield and J. Palmer Bartholomew in 1821, and Oliver Snow in 1822. Some stories of these early settlers are very intere
2 CONC sting, a few of which follow      *********************************  Additional Notes from Bartholomew Family History  Enlisted at the age of 15 as a drummer boy in the War of 1812 and was present at the termination of the battle 
2 CONC of Sackett's harbor.  He re-moved in 1822 to Auburn, Ohio, where he erected the first blacksmith shop in the county.  
