2 CONT '''Notes'''
2 CONT     "History of the Town of Hamden, Connecticut":
2 CONT At a General Assembly of the Governor and Company of His Majesty's English Colony of Connecticut in New England, in America, holden at Hartford, in said colony, on the second Thursday of May, being the twelfth day of said month, i
2 CONC n 1757.
2 CONT     "Upon the memorial of Daniel Bradley and others, inhabtants of the north part of the First Society in New Haven, showing that they live at a great distance from the public worship in said society; praying to have a committee a
2 CONC ppointed to view the circumstances of the memorialists, etc., and if they shall think it meet and best make them a distinct ecclesiastical society, etc., as by the memorial on file may more fully appear : "Resolved by this Assembl
2 CONC y that Benjamin Hall, Samuel Bassett and Nathaniel Harrison, Esqrs., be a committee and they are hereby impowered and directed as a committee, at the cost of the memorialists, to repair to said district and view the circumstances 
2 CONC of the memorialists, notifying said first society, etc., and having heard all parties concerned, make their report to this Assembly in October next what they shall think best and most suitable to be done in the premises."
2 CONT
2 CONT     Scott's Note: There is more than one man named Benjamin Hall in my database who may qualify as the man cited above in "History of the Town of Hamden, Connecticut". So one of these may apply:
2 CONT     Benjamin Hall, born 1725 in Wallingford, son of Jonathan Hall and Dinah Andrews; married 1st Mary Ives in 1752 in Wallingford and 2nd Phebe Hall in 1757 in Wallingford; died 1808 and buried in Wallingford
2 CONT     Benjamin Hall, born 1728 in Wallingford, son of John Hall and Elizabeth Royce; married 1st Susanna Peck in 1754 in Wallingford and 2nd Mary Atwater after 1779; died 1806 in Wallingford
2 CONT     Benjamin Hall married Damaris Doolittle (born 1735 in Wallingford); no other data
2 CONT
