1 NOTE {geni:about_me} # '''Atwater''' https://archive.org/details/Vol1AtwaterHistoryAndGenealogy Full Text: "Atwater History and Genealogy VOL. I" Compiled by Francis Atwater 1901
2 CONT #:'''Atwater''' https://archive.org/stream/atwaterhistoryge02atwa#page/n5/mode/2up II  Full Text:"Atwater History and Genealogy VOL. II " Compiled by Francis Atwater 1907
2 CONT # '''Atwater''': https://archive.org/stream/atwaterhistoryge03atwa#page/n5/mode/2up  Full Text: "Atwater History and Genealogy VOL. III" Compiled by Francis Atwater 1918
2 CONT # '''Atwater''': https://archive.org/stream/atwaterhistoryge04atwa#page/n5/mode/2up  Full Text: "Atwater History and Genealogy VOL. IV" Compiled by Francis Atwater 1927
2 CONT # '''Atwater''': https://archive.org/stream/atwaterhistoryge05atwa#page/n287/mode/2up  Full Text: "Atwater History and Genealogy VOL. V" by Charles Hobart Atwater  1956 p. 272
2 CONT # '''Atwater''': : https://archive.org/stream/atwaterhistoryge04atwa#page/10/mode/2up/search/Jonathan Atwater history and genealogy : comprising the results of seventy-seven years research by Rev. E.E. Atwater and the compilerby A
2 CONC twater, Francis, b. 1858; Atwater, Edward E. (Edward Elias), 1816-1887 (1927)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Volume I, Pages 97 and 98l No. 7 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''7 . Jonathan, son of David''', lived in New Haven; m. June 1, 1681, '''Ruth. dau. of Rev. Jeremiah Peck'''. She d. June 3, 1726. He is described in a deed as a merchant. Her will was probated Oct. 5, 1741- The Rev. Jeremiah Pec
2 CONC k, father of Ruth, was a son of William Peck, Deacon, one of the original settlers of New Haven, also a signer of the “ Fundamental Agreement of Quinnipiack/’ He was born in England about 1623. In 1656 he kept a school in Guilford
2 CONC , Conn., where he remained until 1660. That year he was established, according to an act of the court, in the Hopkins Collegiate Grammar School of New Haven, and there “taught the languages of sciences.” In 1661 he was a minister 
2 CONC at Saybrook. In 1677, unwilling to abide by the union of the New Haven Colony with the “lax and broader principaled" Colony of Connecticut, he, with a few others, departed for Newark, N. J., to form a colony more congenial to thei
2 CONC r views. He there resided in 1674. He then removed to Elizabethtown, N. J., thence to Greenwich, Conn., where he was minister to 1689; thence to Waterbary, Conn., where he was the first minister, and there d. June 7, 1699, aged 76
2 CONC . He married, Nov. 12, 1656, Joanna Ketchel, dau. of “Mr." Robert Kctchcl and Margaret Sheaffe. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''30. Joshua''', b. Feb. 29, 1682 d. March 16, 1682. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''31. David''', b. Aug. 5, 1683. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''32. Jeremiah''', b. Jan. 31, 1685. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''33. Mary.'''' b. Dec. 31, 1686; m. Isaac Dickerman. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''34. Ruth''', b. Dec. 31, 1688; m. Samuel Ives. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''35. Jonathan''', b. Nov. 4, 1690. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''36. Lydia''', b. April aS, 1693; d. Aug. 2, 1694. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''37. Joseph''', b. Dec. 9, 1694. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''38. Stephen''', b. Dec. 4. 1696; d. Oct. 23, 1704. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''39. Damaris''', b. Oct. 9, 1698; m. Caleb Hall. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''40. Lydia''', b. July 31, 1701; d. March 30, 1708. 
2 CONT
2 CONT “One of the earliest of New Haven’s successful merchants was '''Jonathan Atwater''', and his profits from trade were such as might well have satisfied the commercial ambition of the planters. The inventory of his estate, as found 
2 CONC in the Probate Records, covers over eight closely written pages and gives a variety of objects, great and small, suggestive not only of wealth but of the processes of its acquisition. There are several farms, with tracts of land a
2 CONC mounting to some four thousand acres; province bills and interest-bearing notes; a stock of merchandise for a retail store, indicating barter In local produce and in manufactures from beyond the seas, such as fine cutlery, silks, 
2 CONC broadcloth, Turkish wrought cushions; a wardrobe containing a number of coats with silver buttons; a gold seal ring; an ivory-headed cane and a watch that had belonged to Mr. Diswell, the regicide ; and finally, three negroes, a f
2 CONC orm of property less common in Connecticut than farther South. The whole estate was appraised at £ 15.323, which was nearly half as much as the official valuation of all estates in New Haven."* 
2 CONT
2 CONT “The inventory of Jonathan Atwater’s estate is full of suggestions concerning the life of those early times. Its list of articles tells of energy, industry and thrift There are the simple tools for every kind of work that filled t
2 CONC he women's hands within doors and employed the men outside, from knitting needle to spinning wheel and loom, from shoemaker’s awl to ax and scythe and lumber. 
2 CONT
2 CONT But quite as suggestive as anything we find is the absence of objects that are familiar to us. There are no musical instruments, not a flute or harp or violin in the house or in the store. Neither do we find a painting mentioned o
2 CONC r any picture, though the sideboard is adorned with pieces of heavy wrought silver, and some of the furniture is.elegant, as well as the wardrobe. 
2 CONT
2 CONT “The stock of books is meagre, the appraisal of the whole being less • than eight pounds. Among them are named two Bibles, two Psalm books, two Psalters and fifteen dozen Catechisms, with a number of books whose titles are not giv
2 CONC en.f This is hardly indicative of literary habits, either in the home or in the community. Happily Yale College was established about this time, and the beginnings were made of a new order of things for New Haven.” J  (NOTE: Inven
2 CONC tory and Legal information may be found in Volume I, from page 99)
2 CONT
2 CONT * Reference-Full Text: https://archive.org/stream/atwaterhistoryge04atwa#page/10/mode/2up/search/Jonathan Atwater history and genealogy : comprising the results of seventy-seven years research by Rev. E.E. Atwater and the compiler
2 CONC by Atwater, Francis, b. 1858; Atwater, Edward E. (Edward Elias), 1816-1887 (1927)
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Page 10
2 CONT
2 CONT The fourth son was Jonathan. He married Ruth, daughter of Rev. Jeremiah Peck. He had eleven children. He was the merchant prince of New Haven, and his profits must have satisfied his ambition. The inventory of his estate found in 
2 CONC the probate records, covers over eight closely written pages, and gives a variety of objects, great and small, suggestive not only of wealth but of the processes of its acquisition. There were several farms with tracts of land amo
2 CONC unting to four thousand acres; province bills and interest bearing notes, a stock of merchandise for a retail store, indicating barter in local produce and in manufactures from beyond the seas, such as fine cutlery, broadcloth, Tu
2 CONC rkish wrought cushions; a wardrobe containing a number of coats with silver buttons; a gold seal ring, an ivory-headed cane and a gold watch, and finally three negroes. I lis whole estate was appraised at 15,323 pounds, which was 
2 CONC nearly half as much as the official valuation of all estates in New Haven. This inventory is full of suggestions concerning the life of those early times. [ts lisl tells of energy, industry and thrift. He lived to the ripe old age
2 CONC  of ninety-four years.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Families of Ancient New Haven by Donald Lines Jacobus, pg.  61, 537, 1384
2 CONT
2 CONT Buried at Center Church on the Green Churchyard in New Haven, CT
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: buried in the New Haven Green; no tombstone remaining
2 CONT --------------------
