1 NOTE {geni:about_me} PATRICK MACGREGORIE, born in Scotland; served in the English Army, in France, under Charles II. On his return he took part in the religious controversy in 1681-2.
2 CONT
2 CONT David Toshach and Patrick MacGregorie were named in a document prepared by Robert Barclay, giving instructions to his Deputy, Gawn Lawrie, dated August 1, 1684.  The document named David Toshach a partner with "James Earl of Perth
2 CONC  and Sir George McKinzie of Tarbutt."  Patrick "Magriger" was named proxy. In the papers of King Charles II (volume 25, 1683) is a document dated August 10, 1683 at Windsor Castle.  It is a "Warrant for a letter of approbation, ex
2 CONC oneration, indemnity, and full security in favour of John, Earl of Braed'alban and Holland, and of his kindred, friends and followers hereafter mentioned....."  Both David Toshach and Patrick MacGregorie are mentioned, along with 
2 CONC four men with the surname of Campbell.
2 CONT
2 CONT This document exonerates the men named from any criminal charges stemming from a battle between the Earl of Braed'alban and supporters and the Earl of Caithness and supporters.  The Earl of Braed'alban was a cousin of David Toshac
2 CONC h through his mother, Katherine Campbell.  The four Campbell men may also have been relatives.  The dispute apparently took place three years earlier.
2 CONT
2 CONT Patrick was chosen as leader of a number of Presbyterians emigrants. Patrick led two ships to the colonies in 1684, along with 25 Presbyterian Covenanter families, David Toschach, the brother of James Drummond, Earl of Perth, who 
2 CONC financed the trip. The party landed in Maryland or Virginia (due to poor winds) and trecked north to New Jersey, their original destination. 
2 CONT
2 CONT They proceeded north to Perth Amboy, in New Jersey.  At the suggestion of Governor Dongan in 1685, he settled instead at Plum Point [New York], just above the Hudson Highlands, where he built a log house and engaged in the Indian 
2 CONC trade.
2 CONT
2 CONT He settled in New York colony and became somewhat famous as the first non-French white man in the Great Lakes region.A settlement had been made upon the borders of Cornwall at Plum Point and on territory now included in the town o
2 CONC f New Windsor.  This was by Col. Patrick MacGregorie, and was the first settlement within the limits of the present county of Orange.
2 CONT
2 CONT MacGregorie was muster general of militia under Gov. Dongan. Learning native languages, he entered the Indian trade. With  Dongan's permission, in 1687 he led a party to trade with the Ottawas at Michilimakinac. That spring, the a
2 CONC dventurers were captured by the French and Indians and Mc Gregory was sent as a prisoner to Montreal. Freed by the French governor as a goodwill gesture, a year later he served Governor Edmund Andros in command of a company of sol
2 CONC diers against the Indians in Maine.
2 CONT
2 CONT Returning, he took part in the Leister revolution and was killed tyring to recapture the fort for the British at Fort George, 19 March, 1691. 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Conflicting information possible arrival in US 1684 or 1697. Alternate birth 1654 Orange, NC
