1 NAME Elisha M. Pease, Governor
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC Oakwood Cemetery
2 ADDR
3 CITY Austin
3 STAE Texas
3 CTRY United States
1 BIRT
2 DATE 3 JAN 1812
1 DEAT
2 DATE 26 AUG 1883
1 TITL Gov.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Pease, Elisha Marshall (1812–1883)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Print
2 CONT By: Roger A. Griffin
2 CONT
2 CONT Type: Biography
2 CONT Published: 1976
2 CONT
2 CONT Updated: February 28, 2020
2 CONT
2 CONT Portrait of Elisha Marshall Pease
2 CONT Portrait of Elisha Marshall Pease. Image courtesy of the Texas State Library and Archives Commission. Image available on the Internet and included in accordance with Title 17 U.S.C. Section 107.
2 CONT PEASE, ELISHA MARSHALL (1812–1883).Elisha Marshall Pease, governor of Texas, son of Lorrain Thompson and Sarah (Marshall) Pease, was born on January 3, 1812, at Enfield, Connecticut. After study at Westfield Academy in Massachuset
2 CONC ts he held several minor positions, including a clerkship in the post office at Hartford, Connecticut. In 1834 he sought new opportunity in the West. By early 1835 he had made his way to Texas, where he settled in the Municipality
2 CONC  of Mina and continued the law studies he had begun in Connecticut. Almost immediately Pease became embroiled in the developing Texas Revolution. In the spring of 1835 he became secretary of a committee of safety at Mina. Though a
2 CONC t first he hoped for conciliation with Mexico, Pease soon changed his position and fought in the battle of Gonzales, the first battle of the revolution, on October 2. He then served as secretary to the General Council of the Provi
2 CONC sional Government and, as a member of that body, attended the convention that met at Washington-on-the-Brazos in March 1836. At that meeting he wrote part of the Constitution of the Republic of Texas. He then served the ad interim
2 CONC  government as chief clerk, successively, of the navy and treasury departments. During the early months of the republic Pease served as clerk to the judiciary committee of the House of Representatives and took a major part in writ
2 CONC ing the new nation's criminal code. In the fall of 1836 he served as acting secretary of the treasury but declined President Sam Houston's offer of the postmaster generalship in order to return to Brazoria to continue his legal st
2 CONC udies. After his admittance to the bar in April 1837 Pease became the republic's first comptroller of public accounts. He then took up the practice of law at Brazoria and soon became successful and respected in his profession.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lucadia Pease, hand-painted tintype portrait (circa 1860s)
2 CONT Lucadia Pease, hand-painted tintype portrait (circa 1860s). Courtesy of the Austin History Center. Image available on the Internet and included in accordance with Title 17 U.S.C. Section 107.
2 CONT After annexation Pease represented Brazoria County in the first three legislatures and authored the Probate Code of 1846. In 1851 he made an unsuccessful run for the governorship. Two years later he won the office and was reelecte
2 CONC d in 1855. Pease was an outstanding governor. Among his important achievements was his pioneering effort to persuade the legislature to establish a system of public education and a state university. Though this effort proved large
2 CONC ly premature, Pease's administration did establish the permanent school fund, and his vision laid the groundwork for future achievement. He also worked to encourage railroad construction in Texas, to put the state penitentiary on 
2 CONC a self-supporting basis, and to establish reservations to civilize and educate the state's Indian population. In addition, he supervised the building campaign that led to the completion of the Governor's Mansion, the General Land 
2 CONC Office building, the State Orphan's Home (now the Corsicana State Home), and a new Capitol. Perhaps his most significant accomplishment was the settlement of the public debt of the state, by which he made available funds for the e
2 CONC stablishment of a hospital for the mentally ill and schools for the deaf and blind (see AUSTIN STATE HOSPITAL, TEXAS SCHOOL FOR THE DEAF, TEXAS SCHOOL FOR THE BLIND), all of which he had recommended to the legislature. Upon his re
2 CONC tirement from office in 1857, the state was in excellent financial condition.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1859 Pease aligned himself with the Unionist faction in Texas politics. He remained active in this movement into the early months of the Civil War, after which he quietly maintained his loyalty to the Union until the end of the
2 CONC  conflict. In 1866 he lost a bid to become governor again in the first election of the Reconstruction era. Early in 1867 he helped organize the Republican party in Texas. Later that year Gen. Philip H. Sheridan removed Governor Ja
2 CONC mes W. Throckmorton from office and appointed Pease in his place. Pease's subsequent efforts to reorganize the state government and bring accountability to its actions were met by conflict within the Republican ranks and bitternes
2 CONC s toward the chief executive by the former Confederate majority in the state. Pease resigned from the governorship in 1869 because of differences with Gen. Joseph J. Reynolds over Reconstruction policies that Pease considered radi
2 CONC cal and despotic. Throughout the remainder of his life Pease remained actively interested in political affairs in the state. He was president of the non-partisan Tax-payers' Convention of 1871, which opposed many of the measures o
2 CONC f Governor Edmund J. Davis's administration. In 1872 he was chairman of the Texas delegation to the national Liberal Republican convention. In 1879 President Rutherford B. Hayes appointed Pease to the collectorship of customs at G
2 CONC alveston. In the closing years of his life Pease practiced law in Austin, engaged in various business ventures, and lived a quiet life with his wife, the former Lucadia Christiana Niles, of Poquonock, Connecticut, whom he had marr
2 CONC ied in 1850, and their two daughters. A third daughter had died in childhood. Pease died on August 26, 1883, after an attack of apoplexy and was buried in Austin.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisha Marshall Pease (January 3, 1812 – August 26, 1883) was a Texas politician. He served as the fifth and thirteenth governor of Texas.
2 CONT
2 CONT A native of Enfield, Connecticut, Pease moved to Mexican Texas in 1835. He soon became active in the Texas independence movement and after the Texas Revolution began, Pease became the secretary of the provisional government and co
2 CONC -wrote the new Texas Constitution. After independence had been won, Pease was named the comptroller of public accounts in the government of the new Republic of Texas.
2 CONT
2 CONT Following the annexation of Texas to the United States, Pease was elected to the Texas House of Representatives in 1845 and reelected in 1847. In 1849, he ran for the Texas Senate from District 11 (Brazoria and Galveston counties)
2 CONC  but lost to John B. Jones who was sworn in on November 5, 1849. Pease contested the election, was declared the winner, and was sworn in 4 days later on November 9, 1849.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pease first ran for governor in 1851 but withdrew from the race two weeks before the election. He was elected in each of the next two elections, 1853 and 1855. As governor, he paid off the state debt and established the financial 
2 CONC foundation that the state would later use to finance its schools and colleges. Some would even say that Pease was the "Savior of the Texas economy."
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1856, surveyor Jacob de Córdova of the Galveston, Houston, and Henderson Railroad Company named a newly discovered river in West Texas the "Pease River" after the governor.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the American Civil War, Pease sided with the Union. After the war, he became a leader in the state Republican Party and was appointed as the civilian governor of Texas in 1867 by General Philip H. Sheridan who was the milit
2 CONC ary head of the Reconstruction government. Pease's policies as governor alienated both ex-Unionists and ex-Confederates and he resigned in 1869.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pease died of apoplexy in Lampasas, Texas. He was buried in Oakwood Cemetery in Austin, Texas.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sources''':
2 CONT Pease, Elisha Marshall. [Correspondence from E.M. Pease to Lorrain T. Pease, his father], letter, January 8, 1837; (https://texashistory.unt.edu/ark:/67531/metapth712849/: accessed October 5, 2022), University of North Texas Libra
2 CONC ries, The Portal to Texas History, https://texashistory.unt.edu; crediting Austin History Center, Austin Public Library.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/online/articles/fpe08
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elisha_M._Pease
2 CONT
