2 CONT Funeral services were held Tuesday at 1 p.m. for Mrs. Vilate Merrell Roberts, wife of George Roberts in the Glines chapel.
2 CONT
2 CONT -Vernal Express, 2nd Glines section, December 28, 1949
2 CONT
2 CONT ACTIVE LDS WORKER DIES AT 70 
2 CONT
2 CONT Funeral services were conducted Tuesday for Mrs. Helen Vilate Merrell Roberts, 70; Glines long-time worker for the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-Day Saints, who died Friday at 5 a.m. at her residence of causes incident to age.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Roberts spent a great part of her time in active LDS work. She was a Relief Society teacher for more than 54 years and for several years was president of the Primary Association at Cedarview Ward in Roosevelt.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was also a first counselor in the Young Women's Mutual Improvement Association and had spent much time in genealogy work. Her friends knew her for the collection of many choice religious volumes and books which she read extens
2 CONC ively.
2 CONT
2 CONT A natice of Paradise, Cache County, she was born March 12, 1879, a daughter of Silas Jerome and Lydia Merrell. She came to the Ashley Valley when very young and resided with her parents during her young womanhood at the Dry Fork s
2 CONC ettlement.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was married to George H. Roberts March 17, 1897, in Vernal. The couple moved to Duchesne County in 1906, when the reservation first opened up.
2 CONT
2 CONT Survivors include her husband; two sons and a daughter, Melvin H. Roberts, Wayne Roberts and Ruby Gray, Vernal; 15 grandchildren and 16 great-grandchildren.
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial was in the Vernal City Cemetery. Glines Ward bishop, Ernest Johnson, officiated at the services which were held in the ward chapel.
2 CONT
2 CONT  -Vernal Express, Glines 2nd section, December 28, 1949, transcribed by Rhonda Holton
2 CONT -----------------
2 CONT Helen Vilate Merrell married George Henry Roberts on March 17, 1897 at Vernal. The marriage was later solemnized in the Salt Lake LDS Temple.
2 CONT
2 CONT To them were born four children, Melvin Henry, Ernest Wayne, Ruby Mae (Mrs. Al Gray) and George Eldon. Eldon died as a baby. The others are all living in Vernal at the present time.
2 CONT
2 CONT Soon after their marriage Mr. and Mrs. Roberts moved to Dry Fork and lived there until 1906 when they to Cedarview, Utah.
2 CONT
2 CONT The family was one of the first to homestead in this section of the Indian Reservation. Her husband became a prominent farmer and sheepman for a period of thirty years.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her husband being a civic minded man, held many positions of importance in the growth and development of Duchesne County. He was a director of the Dry Gulch Irrigation Company and a member of the Duchesne County School Board when 
2 CONC it was first organized, which position he held for a nember of years. He was also a member of the Fourth Judicial District Court and a trustee of the Roosevelt High School.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1936 Mr. and Mrs. Roberts moved back to Vernal and resided in Maeser. Mr. Roberts was chosen as grazier for the Bureau of Land Management, District Grazing Services, which position he held from 1936 to 1940.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1947 they moved to Glines and resided in a new home they had just completed near the ward chapel and also close to the home of their daughter, Ruby Gray. Here they lived until Mrs. Roberts death which occured December 23, 1949.
2 CONT
2 CONT -From her husband's obituary
2 CONT
2 CONT Parents: Merrell, Lydia Merrell and Silas Jerome Merrell 
2 CONT
2 CONT Married George Henry Roberts March 17, 1897 in Vernal, Utah.* Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/42083289/helen-vilate-roberts Find A Grave Memorial] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Nov 
2 CONC 27 2023, 17:48:01 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT
