1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://trees.ancestry.com/tree/60194031/person/32054280496
2 CONT
2 CONT John and Jane Bancroft
2 CONT
2 CONT John BANCROFT - b. about 1596, England; d. about 1647, Lynn, MA. Said to descend from "Thomas (3), Ralph (2), and John (1) of Chellaston, England." To America in the 'James' Apr. 12, 1632. Married about 1622 in England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jane - Arrived with husband and children in the 'James.' Jane married second about 1640 Thomas BARBER.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of John and Jane Bancroft
2 CONT
2 CONT *Thomas - b. 1622, England; d. 1691, MA. Married first Mar. 31, 1647 at Dedham, MA Alice BACON (d. 1648) and second Sep. 15, 1648 Elizabeth METCALF (1626-1711). Note: One source names his father as Thomas BANCROFT of Swarkston, De
2 CONC rbyshire, England. Children of Lt. Thomas and Elizabeth: Elizabeth; Ens. John married Elizabeth EATON; and Thomas married Sarah POOLE.
2 CONT *John - b. England; d. Aug. 6, 1662, Windsor, CT. Married Dec. 3, 1650, Windsor, CT Hannah DUPER, who married second John LONDON. Children: John married Elizabeth EATON; Nathaniel married Hannah WILLIAMS; Ephraim married Sarah STI
2 CONC LES; Hannah; and Sarah.
2 CONT *Anna - b. 1627, England. Married John GRIFFIN.
2 CONT *Ebenezer - http://kinnexions.com/ancestries/griffin.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 166
2 CONT
2 CONT Name: John Bancroft
2 CONT
2 CONT Location: Died 6 August, 1662 (W. R.) Invt. œ110-14-00. Taken 22 August, 1662, by Matthew Grant.
2 CONT
2 CONT The children: John Bancroft, 12 3/4 years of age: Nathaniel, 9 years: Ephraim, 8; Hannah, 3 & 5 months, and Sarah 3 months (W. R.)Court Record, Page 178--10 September, 1662: Order to Dist. the Estate: œ s d To the Widow, 37-10-00 
2 CONC To the Eldest son, 14-11-00 To each of the others, 12-00-00To be paid them at lawfull age. (This Dist. on Page 166. Probate Side.) A DIGEST OF THE EARLY CONNECTICUT PROBATE RECORDS.1650 to 1663. http://trees.ancestry.com/tree/6835
2 CONC 093/person/1674458916/storyx/e1277ff6-add7-4b32-96a5-9b6495cdf3fe?src=search
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT *Note: this is a direct copy and paste from Gerard Pratt Bancroft, Deacon's Geni Profile Overview Tab [http://www.geni.com/people/Gerard-Pratt-Bancroft/383544001440012388 Gerard Pratt Bancroft, Deacon's Geni Profile] and any and a
2 CONC ll research credit goes to, whoever may have placed this information there. I simply put 2+2 together. Gerard is my 5th cousin 7x removed through his maternal lineage. I am not certain that Hannah was his wife, though... though th
2 CONC e description below, relationship to Gerard Bancroft,  would imply, he is the great great grandfather, as well as, John being the father of Nathaniel Bancroft, who married Ruth.  ~Theresa Renée
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT FAMILY RECOLLECTIONS BY HUBERT HOWE BANCROFT:
2 CONT
2 CONT My father was born in Massachusetts; his father's great-great-grandfather, John Bancroft, came from London in the ship "James" in 1632. My father's great-great-grandparents were Nathaniel and Ruth Bancroft, whose son Samuel was bo
2 CONC rn July 8, 1711, and died July 6, 1788. Sarah White was Samuel's wife; and their son Samuel, my father's grandfather, was born at West Springfield, Massachusetts, April 22, 1737.
2 CONT
2 CONT His father, Azariah Bancroft, the eldest of nine children, was born in Granville, Massachusetts, April 13, 1768; and on the 25th of January, 1799, my father was born in Granville, the fourth in a family of eleven. His great-grandp
2 CONC arents removed to Granville, Massachusetts, in 1738, when Samuel Bancroft was a year old, with the first settlers coming to Granville the year he was born.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the book entitled "A Golden Wedding", my father says: "My recollections of my grandfather are vivid and pleasant. He was a tall, thin, voluble old gentleman, fond of company, jokes, and anecdotes. He served in the French and In
2 CONC dian war, and afterward in the Revolutionary war with the rank of lieutenant. He was paid off in continental money, receiving it in sheets, which he never cut apart. He was very fond of relating incidents of the war, and was never
2 CONC  happier than when surrounded by old comrades and neighbors, talking over different campaigns, with a mug of cider warming before the fire. "Slim-legs" he was called by the soldiers. He married Elizabeth Spelman, and died January 
2 CONC 2, 1820.
2 CONT
2 CONT From my grandfather, Azariah Bancroft, who married Tabitha, daughter of Gerard Pratt, and from the wife of the latter, sometime called Dorcas Ashley, my father derived his name Azariah Ashley.
2 CONT
2 CONT This Gerard Pratt was quite a character, and displayed enough peculiarities, which were not affected, to entitle his name to be placed on the roll of great men or men of genius.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gerard Pratt loved sermons, analyzed them, was a avid reader in his free time, and he kept a diary commenting on the content of the sermons.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pratt was an active abolitionist. He personally built the accommodations in his brother's barn that housed slaves escaping in the Underground into Ohio. He helped provided slaves transport and an escort in his wagon and helped his
2 CONC  brother Ashley provide food and take care of slaves as they were escaping the south.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gerard, in season and out of season wore his Puritan hat, a broad-brimmed quakerish-looking affair, although he was no quaker. His hat was the last article of apparel to be removed at night, when he placed it on the bedpost, the f
2 CONC irst to be put on in the morning when he arose, and it was removed during the day only when he asked the blessing at table, which was done standing, our of respect, and during that time he held it in his hand, replacing it before 
2 CONC beginning to eat.
2 CONT
2 CONT Half a mile from the old town of Granville, Massachusetts, lived these great-grand-parents of mine, on two acres of good garden land, with a small orchard in which were six famous seek-no-farther apple-trees, reserved from the old
2 CONC  family farm, afterward owned by their son-in-law, James Barlow. They were aged and infirm when my father, then a small boy, came every year to help his...
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Gerard Pratt Bancroft was the grandson of Gerard Pratt (and first cousin of Hubert Howe Bancroft who wrote the memoir of their grandfather, Gerard Pratt.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Gerard Pratt Bancroft apprenticed under his grandfather Gerard Pratt as a house builder who was important in building the New England style homes of Granville Massachusetts and and then Granville Ohio; and in semi-retired life, he
2 CONC  occupied himself as a cabinet and furniture maker. The Pratt men learned and passed this craft, from father to son, since the first Pratt arrive in Plymouth Colony.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gerard Pratt Bancroft served as a deacon of the church and kept a diary, which is now owned by the Granville Historical Society. The little leather volume is 3 1/2 by 6 inches. It is dated for the year 1873 when he was then 81 yea
2 CONC rs of age and still actively engaged in the carpentry trade.
2 CONT
2 CONT Entries in his diary were written in violet ink with a clear firm hand. He began each entry with an inspirational verse from the Bible or a stanza from a favorite hymn. At the top of each page he notes the weather conditions and a
2 CONC t the bottom of each page he recorded the number of hours he worked. Each Sunday he mentions the topic of the sermon given by Reverend Dudley of the Presbyterian Church with his own commentary response to the sermon. On June 19, h
2 CONC e mentioned the long and beautiful tolling of all of the town's church bells on the occasion of the death of the President of Denison College, Samson Talbot. (He only lived a block or two from the town's two biggest churches.) Ger
2 CONC ard mentions his visits to and from family and records letters written or received.
2 CONT
2 CONT In early June 1873, Gerard Pratt and his wife Jane Bancroft travelled to Springfield Ohio. They travelled in their own wagon to the new Union Station south of Granville, and then by train with a change in Columbus. The whole fare 
2 CONC for both of them was $9.40. The cost of this trip is listed with their income and expenditures for each month in the back of his diary.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gerard Pratt learned the trade of builder from his Pratt grandfather and father Azariah Bancroft who was a business partner as an investor and builder with the Pratt family of his wife. His wife, Jane Pratt was a descendant of Phi
2 CONC neas Pratt who was one of the first Puritan inhabitants of Cambridge Massachusetts, who sailed on the Sparrow to Plymouth Colony. Phineas Pratt of Plymouth Colony was also a "joiner" from England, which meant he was skilled in the
2 CONC  craft of design and cutting, shaping and joining wood together to build houses, which was an essential profession for the colonists. Pratt owned property and built houses in Plymouth and Cambridge Massachusetts. He trained his hi
2 CONC red workers, and son, the craft of how to build houses too. When Phineas was old, and could no longer build houses or manage a work team, the legislature of Massachusetts awarded him an old age pension of 300 acres for being an "e
2 CONC arly comer" who helped build the Plymouth and Massachusetts Colonies.
2 CONT
2 CONT Phineas Pratt's descendants, such as this Gerard Pratt Bancroft, were founders of towns in Springfield and Granville Massachusetts, and then when Ohio settlement opened, both the Bancroft and Pratt families were among the first me
2 CONC n who founded Granville Ohio and were early builders of the town's first houses. The Bancroft family were awarded land in Ohio in recognition for the Revolutionary War service of Samuel Bancroft.
2 CONT
2 CONT In those days, the profession of a son was learned from his father and a joiner enjoyed similar respect as a builder today, if not more, as they were considered respected professionals necessary to the community and were affection
2 CONC ately remembered for the design and building of a family home, a shop, a church, or crafting furniture. The difference was that the joiner actually did the work when they were young to learn the trade and then directed a team of w
2 CONC orkers during much of their working career, who would then also thus learn the trade. Today a builder is just the person who invests money in a project, but has no skill to build the house.
2 CONT
2 CONT Being one of the founders of the town of Granville Ohio, like his father Azariah, Gerard also built many of the early homes of Granville Ohio, including his own brick home on Equality Street (now Elm Street) behind Main Street. Ha
2 CONC ving given up on building homes in old age, at age 81, he describes in his diary work done for customers and the machinery employed, which included making tables, bedsteads, stair bannisters, picture frames, repairing chairs, and 
2 CONC making stretchers for chairs and tables. However, by age 81 the most frequent request was the sharpening of saws. Some days Mr. Pratt says that he stood at the turning lathe all day and notes that each time, it gave him a snifflin
2 CONC g "cold". The steam engine that powers his tools he recorded as needing frequent attention and repair.
2 CONT
2 CONT ABOLITIONISTS: The Bancrofts and the people of Granville MA and Granville Ohio as a whole had always been antislavery. The entire town was traditionally abolitionist since their their Puritan roots due to English legal principles 
2 CONC and Christian principles of kindness. Historian James Stewart (1976) explains the abolitionists' deep beliefs: "All people were equal in God's sight; the souls of black folks were as valuable as those of whites; for one of God's c
2 CONC hildren to enslave another was a violation of the Higher Law, even if it was sanctioned by the Constitution."
2 CONT
2 CONT Slavery was seen as un-Christian, un-English and inhumane.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Granville MA the townspeople hired the first negro preacher in America, Rev. Lemuel Haynes, for his refined intellect and Christian preaching. Rev. Haynes did not posit himself as a negro, but as a Christian. Lemeul Haynes was 
2 CONC the preacher for the Pratt and Bancroft families for 30 years.
2 CONT
2 CONT They saw Christianity as non-tribal, as a break from tribal Judaism, in that Christianity was an inclusive religion rather than exclusive, teaching love, forgiveness and charity. They valued the English Magna Carta, which placed k
2 CONC ings under the law, equal to any common man, and led England to establish the first Parliamentary democratic system in human history. The Protestant Reformation won for Englishmen the "freedom of conscience" to allow the freedom t
2 CONC o judge for themselves what their religion and government could be. This freedom of conscience, born of Protestantism, directly led to the principle of the freedom of speech, the Age of Reason and The Enlightenment, which led to t
2 CONC he Scientific Revolution and Industrial Revolution. The Enlightenment principles taught that men were created equal under God (nature). The Enlightenment equated God with the Providence of the powers of nature itself, which was a 
2 CONC classical European ideal.
2 CONT
2 CONT ABOLITIONISTS: The English-derived Americans, had many principles to draw upon that prompted them to be anti-slavery. Only 1.4% of white Americans ever owned slaves.
2 CONT
2 CONT Antislavery was inevitable. The problem was that once the economy of the south was dependent on slaves, how could it be changed? What right did strangers in the north have over the business of private individuals far away in he so
2 CONC uth? None, except by vote and enforcement, or war.
2 CONT
2 CONT When the entire town of Granville MA moved to Ohio to found Granville Ohio, the Ashley Bancroft house was a stop on the Underground Railroad where the Bancroft family offered refuge to escaping slaves, coming over the Ohio border.
2 CONC  The town worked for decades to end slavery and even had a riot in the 1830s over how to end slavery. Some advocated immediate emancipation, while others suggested purchasing their freedom, an idea rejected by Reverend Lemuel Hayn
2 CONC es because the cost would exceed the entire wealth of the U.S. government. The British had already abolished slavery in 1801 and the town of Granville dedicated much debate as to how to best free the salves of America.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gerard Pratt Bancroft was a member of The Abolitionist Society in 1836 along with his brothers Azariah Ashley Pratt Jr and Dr. William Wilson Pratt. They proposed an immediate end to slavery by emancipation without plans in place 
2 CONC for a new system.
2 CONT
2 CONT Whereas, their uncle, Judge Samuel Pratt was a member of the Colonization Society, who thought the freed slaves should be given the option to return to Africa. They recognized that black field labor was facing a reduced future bec
2 CONC ause the new farming machines such as the cotton gin, would make large slave-labour plantations obsolete in any case, and soon slaves would be less in demand and the townspeople were concerned about the welfare of homeless negroes
2 CONC  and sought a solution. Like Abraham Lincoln originally planned, but did not implement, the Colonization Society raised money to support ending slavery by gradually purchasing the freedom of each slave to permit a gradual introduc
2 CONC tion into a free labor market. However, this did not solve the problem that the cotton gin would put most of them out of work. So, the slaves were to be given the option of repatriation to Africa for those negroes who so wished to
2 CONC  return to their place of origin, which would allow the negro man choice, and the southern economy and peoples, both black and white, a gradual means of re-adjustment to the vast change coming to the entire socio-economic structur
2 CONC e due to machines.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.granvillehistory.org/storage/historicaltimes/HistTimes198901.pdf
