2 TITL Dorothy Ingersoll Phelp Root marries Japhet Chapin
1 NOTE Henry Chapin, son of Samuel and Cisily, was m. Dec. 5, 1664,
2 CONT to Bethia Cooley, daughter of Benjamin and Sarah Cooley of Longmeadow.
2 CONT He d. Aug. 15, 1718. Mrs. Bethia Chapin d. Dec. 11, 1711.
2 CONT It appears that Henry Chapin did not reside in Springfield in the
2 CONT early part of his manhood, but took up his residence there about the
2 CONT year 1659. He, as well as his father, was a prominent man in town
2 CONT affairs, as appears by the ancient records of the town, and was a
2 CONT Representative to the General Court in the year 1689. Tradition
2 CONT says of Henry, that he was impressed on board a British man of war
2 CONT and served seven years, during which time he was in a severe
2 CONT engagement with the Dutch. He afterwards commanded a merchant
2 CONT ship and made several voyages between London and Boston,
2 CONT but at length, tired of a seafaring life, took up his residence in
2 CONT Boston, and afterwards in Springfield, where his father and family
2 CONT resided. Henry came to the northerly part of Springfield (now
2 CONT Chicopee) to reside, built him a house south side of Chicopee River,
2 CONT in what is now the village of Chicopee, on Ferry street, facing south
2 CONT on West street, near the large elm tree and a few feet east of the
2 CONT house formerly owned and occupied by William Chapin, one of his
2 CONT descendants. The house took fire and was burned, 1762. He also
2 CONT made a contract with John Pyncheon for 200 acres of land on the
2 CONT north side of Chicopee River. The following is said to be a copy
2 CONT of the contract.
2 CONT March 9th, 1659. Sold to Henry Chapin 200 acres of land on
2 CONT ye north side of Chickkuppy River to run fro ye hills on ye east
2 CONT side, to the Great river on ve West, and on the south it is to be
2 CONT boucded by, and to join to Chickkuppy river, onley one 25 acres or
2 CONT thirty being by Chickkuppy river about the place which shall be
2 CONT judged best for a warehouse is to be taken out and excepted, out of
2 CONT the parcel yet so as ye 200 acres is to be made up there together.
2 CONT Also Henry is to have half of ye upper Island which is to be as
2 CONT equally divided as it can be. and also he is to have five acres of
2 CONT mowable meadow at the lower end of the muxy meadow. For all
2 CONT which he is to pay and allow me the sum of twenty pounds in Wheat
2 CONT at current prices at four several payments, viz., five pounds by the
2 CONT first of March next, which will be anno 1660, and five pounds by
2 CONT the first of March 1661, and another five pounds in March 1662, and
2 CONT ye last five pounds ye first of March 1663. All the payments to be
2 CONT b SECOND GENERATION.
2 CONT in Wheat at price current at the several times of payment, this is
2 CONT the joint agreement betwixt us this 9th day of March 1659 as witness
2 CONT our hands.
2 CONT Signed Henry Chapin.
2 CONT John Pyncheon.
2 CONT Memorandum. I promised Henry that if I did part with the 25
2 CONT acres or 30 acres or with the Islands, he should have the offer of
2 CONT them. (Said premises have been, and tlie greater part are still in
2 CONT the possession of the descendants of said Henry.)
1 NOTE One souce listed his marriage as taking place in Berry, Pomeroy, Devon Co., England
2 CONT Henry Chapin at an early age entered on a seafaring life; was impressed on board a British sh ip and served seven years, and was in an engagment with the Dutch; afterwards, he commande d a merchant ship sailing between London and 
2 CONC Boston.  Tired of the sea, he came to Springfiel d in 1659, and shared with Japhet his father's gift of lands in Chicopee.  There he built hi s house.
2 CONT Chapin Link:  <http://worldconnect.rootsweb.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?db=deannedriscoll>
