1 TITL Deacon
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Benjamin Chapin, son of Henry and Bethia, b. Feb. 2, 1682, was m. Nov. 9, 1704, to Hannah Colton, daughter of Isaac and Mary Colton of Longmeadow. Mrs. Hannah Chapin d. March 5, 1739. Mr. Benjamin Chapin m. (2) Wid
2 CONC ow Joanna Warriner who had been the wife of Ebenezer Warriner. Deacon Benjamin Chapin d. March 22, 1756. Joanna, (2) wife, d. Oct. 13, 1764. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Benjamin Chapin was one of the first Deacons of the church in Chicopee, he having been elected to that office in 1752. His place of residence was on Chicopee street, near the present residence of Dea. Giles S. Chapin. 
2 CONT
2 CONT # Hannah, b. Oct. 3, 1706 ; m. Benjamin Sikes.  
2 CONT # Benjamin, b. July 17, 1708; m. Anna Howard.  
2 CONT # Isaac, b. Aug. 18. 1710.  
2 CONT # Abner, b. Oct. 16, 1713 ; d. Dec. 16, 1713. .  
2 CONT # Jacob, b. April 18, 1716; d. 1717.  
2 CONT # Bethia, b. June 25, 1718 ; m. Feb. 1, 1739, No. 68, Phineas Chapin.  
2 CONT # Sarah, b. Oct. 13, 1720 ; m. Oct. 31, 1741, Ebenezer Warriner.  
2 CONT # George, b. Dec. 13, 1722; d. Dec. 10, 1782.  
2 CONT # Abigail, b. May 26, 1724.  
2 CONT # Ephraim, b. Oct. 29, 1729; d. Oct. 12, 1805.  
2 CONT # Mary, b. Aug. 18, 1727; m. May 28, 1748, Stephen Morgan of Brimfield.  
2 CONT # Eunice, b. Oct. 28, 1732 ; m. pub. Oct. 24, 1751, to Aaron Ferry of Springfield. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://archive.org/stream/chapingenealogyc1862chap/chapingenealogyc1862chap_djvu.txt THE CHAPIN GENEALOGY, CONTAINING A VERY LARGE PROPORTION OF THE DESCENDANTS OF DEA. SAMUEL CHAPIN, WHO SETTLED IN SPRINGFIELD, MASS. IN 1642, C
2 CONC ollected and Compiled by ORANGE CHAPIN]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT From Lineage Book of the National Society of Daughters of Founders and Patriots of America, Vol. 6, 1916 [https://www.myheritage.com/research/record-90100-77951036/lineage-book-of-the-national-society-of-daughters-of-founders?s=19
2 CONC 8651101 MyHeritage]
2 CONT
2 CONT 29 Lucie Chapin (Mrs. Thomas F. Hayward). Born in White Post, Clarke Co., Virginia. Descendant of Samuel Chapin, of Massachusetts, through the Revolutionary ancestor, Benjamin Chapin, of Connecticut and Virginia, as follows: 
2 CONT *2. William Taylor Chapin (Feb. 23, 1846-June 21, 1878) and Martha Agnes Kerfoot (July 26, 1846-living 1907).
2 CONT * 3. Charles Chapin (Nov. 14, 1795-Sept. 14, 1863) and Mary Ann Martha Wise (Nov. 26, 1809-1872). 
2 CONT *4. Gurden Chapin (Nov. 5, 1765-June 26, 1811) and Margaret R. Reeder (Mch. 6, 1774-Nov. 22, 1843). 
2 CONT *5. Benjamin Chapin (May 24, 1736-1778) and Margaret Colton (Oct. 24, 1738-m. Mch. 4, 1760). 
2 CONT *6. Benjamin Chapin (July 17, 1708-Mch. 8, 1762) and Anna Howard (bef. 1720-aft. 1752). 
2 CONT *7. Benjamin Chapin (Feb. 2, 1682-1756) and Hannah Colton (m. Nov. 9, 1704.-Mch. 5, 1739), his first wife. 
2 CONT *8. Henry Chapin (....-Aug. 15, 1718) and Bethia Cooley (m. Dec. 5, 1664-Dec. 11, 1711). 
2 CONT *9. Samuel Chapin (b. Wales-Nov. 11, 1675) and Cicely Chapin (....-Feb. 8, 1683). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel Chapin (Wales-1675) came to New England, about 1635. He was probably first in Roxbury, but took the freemans oath in Boston, June 2, 1641. In 1642 he removed to Springfield, Mass., where in 1652 he was appointed magistrate,
2 CONC  an office he held for many years. He was commissioner and selectman, was much employed in public business, a useful and highly esteemed man, and a deacon in the church. A statue, The Puritan, by St. Gaudens, has been erected to h
2 CONC is memory in Springfield, Mass
2 CONT --------------------
