1 OCCU Amer.Rev, deacon, Brother to Hannah Chapin - See parents, Samuel *Deacon* Chapin & Cicelly Penney. 2nd Spouse-Dorothy Root
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Deacon '''Japhet Chapin''', son of Samuel & Cicely, lived for a time in Milford, CT. He fought in the 18 May 1676 Battle of Turner's Falls, where the Indians were defeated folling their burning of Springfield in Oc
2 CONC t 1675, King Philip's War See:Winthrop McKins..
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT "Japhet begat ten children, of whom eight were boys and two girls - and these eight boys begat eighty-seven children" Springfield, MA historian Mason D. Green (NEB genealogy)
2 CONT
2 CONT Japhet, the fourth son and sixth child, was born in Roxbury, Mass., 15 Oct. 1642, the year in which his parents removed to Springfield, where he spent his boyhood; at the age of 22 we find him located in Milford, Conn., where he w
2 CONC as married 22 July 1664 to Abilena Coley, the daughter of Samuel Coley and granddaughter of James Prudon - both of whom were among the original planters of Milford in 1639. Whatever the incentive that took him to Connecticut, he a
2 CONC pparently made his home there for some years; for on 16 Nov. 1669 he accepted a deed here from Capt. John Pynchon of Springfield. On Mar. 9, 1666, John Pynchon deeded to Deacon Samuel the greater part of the land adjoining Springf
2 CONC ield in the valley between the Chicopee River and Williamsett Brook. This land Deacon Chapin deeded to his son Japhet 16 Apr. 1673, and here he built the home where he spent the remainder of his life - and with him his parents mad
2 CONC e their home, and here Deacon Chapin died at the beginning of King Phillip's war. 
2 CONT
2 CONT History records that Japhet was engaged in the Great Fight at Turner's Falls in May, 1675, and a Chapin Genealogy published in 1862 contains the following: "On the outside of an old account book belonging to said Japhet and suppos
2 CONC ed to be his handwriting we find: 'I went out volunteare against the ingens the 17th of 
2 CONT
2 CONT May 1676, and we engaged batel the 19th of May in the morning before sunrise, and made great Spoil upon the enemy with the Los of 37 men and the Captain Turner, and came home the 20th of May'." (Incidentally, we might add that His
2 CONC tory reports that "in the conflict the Indians lost in killed and drowned 300 warriors".) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Because of Japhet's service in the war, his son Thomas was granted a large portion of the land given the soldiers and their descendants, by the General Court of Massachusetts. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Like his father, Japhet is said to have been a man of great piety, a bulwark in the Puritan faith. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His wife Abilena, died at Chicopee 17 Nov. 1710, and the following spring 31 May 1711 he married Dorothy Root of Enfield, Conn., and died 20 Feb. 1712, and she remarried. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Of the ten children of Japhet, three were daughters, Sarah and two Hannahs - the second Hannah born 1689, the next year after the death of the first. Of her it is recorded that she was "taken captive by the Indians and kept in cap
2 CONC tivity for two years". One wonders when and how she was returned to her home. (NEB Genealogy)
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Baptism: 15 OCT 1642 Roxbury, Suffolk, MA 
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial: Springfield Cemetery, Springfield, Hampden, MA 2 
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: 
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the book "First Congregational Church" of Chicopee, MA (printed in 1976), the sons of Deacon Samuel Chapin, Japhet and Henry, were living in homes of their own in the "Old Parish of Springfield", as Chicopee was then 
2 CONC known in 1675. For years they, their children, their children's children and neighbors attended First Church, now at Court Square in Springfield. The traveling was very difficult, so forty-nine men, twenty-four of them Chapin's, p
2 CONC etitioned First Church to be set off as a Fifth Parish of Springfield and they planned to build a church of their own. When their petition was denied, they took their case to the Massachusetts General Court which granted them the 
2 CONC right to build. On January 3, 1751 Edward Chapin, one of the grandchildren of Japhet writes that they began cutting timber for a Meeting House. The next day forty men advanced into the woods to cut the timber. All volunteers built
2 CONC  the Meeting House by June 5, 1751.
2 CONT
2 CONT Japhet was prominent in town affairs of Springfield, MA and was chosen Selectman 8 times. Japhet was engaged in King Phillip's War and battled with the Indians at Turners Falls in 1676. A deacon in a Church in Springfield MA. John
2 CONC  Pynchon deeded land in Springfield to him 11/16/1669. Japhet built a house at the upper end of Chicopee Street.
2 CONT
2 CONT Daughter Hannah was married 12/03/1703 to John Sheldon of Deerfield, MA. The town of Deerfield was attacked by the Indians about three months after her marriage, and she was taken captive with many others, and marched to Canada. A
2 CONC fter about two years, many of the captives were redeemed and returned home. It is thought that Hannah's husband went to Canada and obtained her release before the release of the other prisoners.
2 CONT
2 CONT The early Chapin's were laid to rest not far from their church in the parish "Old Burying Ground" on Chicopee Street , Chicopee which was opened in 1741, where more than 700 friends, relatives and ancestors rest in peace as cars s
2 CONC peed by on Chicopee Street. The "Old Burying Ground" is graced today by the Tiffany-built Chapin Mausoleum.
2 CONT
