2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Massachusetts state seal
2 CONT Japhet Chapin was an early settler in Massachusetts.
2 CONT Japhet (also spelled Japhat), the son of Samuel, was christened on 15 Oct 1642 at Roxbury, Suffolk, Massachusetts.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT His father, Samuel, came from England to reside in Springfield, Massachusetts in 1642. "Japhett Chapin Joined in marriage with Abelenah Coley the two and twentieth day of July [1664)."[2][3] Japhet married Abilena Coley on 22 July
2 CONC  1662 at Milford, Connecticut, or Springfield, Massachusetts.[4] She died on 17 Nov 1711.[5] They had at least the following children:
2 CONT
2 CONT "Samuell, s. Japhett and Abilene Chapin, July 4, 1665." [6][7]
2 CONT "Sarah, d. Japhet and Abilene, at Milford, Mar. 15, 1668."[8][9]
2 CONT "Thomas, s. Japhet and Abilene, May 20, 1671 at Milford.HR"[10][11]
2 CONT "John, s. Japhet and Abilene, May 14, 1674."[12][13]
2 CONT "Ebenezer, s. Japhet and Abilene, June 26, 1677."[14][15]
2 CONT "Hannah, d. Japhet and Abilena, June 21, 1679.[16][17]d.y. [18]
2 CONT Hannah, d. Japhet and Abilene, July 18, 1680."[19][20]
2 CONT "David, s. Japhet and Abilene, Nov. 16, 1682."[21][22]
2 CONT "Jonathan, s. Japhet and Abilene, Feb. 20, 1685."[23][24]d.y.[25]
2 CONT Jonathan, s. Japhet and Abilene, Sept. 23, 1688."[26][27]
2 CONT On May 19, 1676 Japhet Chapin was in the "Great Falls Fight" at Turner's Falls, Massachusetts.[28] It is claimed that because the English attack was a surprise, only one English soldier was killed while 300 Indian warriors were ki
2 CONC lled and drowned.[28]
2 CONT
2 CONT "Japhet and Dorithy Root of Enfeild, [married] May 31, 1711." citing Book 1, page 112[29]
2 CONT
2 CONT "Japhet Chapin the husband of Dority Chapen was sick and died on feb 20, 1712."[30] and is buried at the Springfield Cemetery, Springfield, Hampden County, Massachusetts, USA. Plot: Willow Avenue 1-10.[31] His gravestone shows tha
2 CONC t he was 70 years old. You can see a picture of his gravestone at Find A Grave: Memorial #36605785.
2 CONT
2 CONT He passed away in 1712.
2 CONT
2 CONT An excerpt from the Chapin Geneaology:
2 CONT
2 CONT Japhet Chapin, of Chicopee, son of Dea. Samuel Chapin, b. 1642; m. (1) July 22, 1664, Abileuah Cooley. Mrs. Abilenah Chapin d. Nov. 17, 1710. And a small stone in the old burying ground in Springfield marks the spot where she was 
2 CONC buried, m. (2) May 31, 1711, Dorothy Root of Eifield, Ct. He d. Feb. 20, 1712, and was buried by the side of his first wife Abilenah. Their remains and the stones which mark their resting places have (probably) been removed to the
2 CONC  new cemetery in Springfield.
2 CONT Japhet probably resided for a time in Milford, Connecticut- As the Worshipful Capt. John Pyncheon of Springfield conveyed to Japhet Chapin of Milford, in Connecticut Colony, a small strip of land near Connecticut River in Springfi
2 CONC eld, Bounds east on Deacon Chapin's land. Deed dated 16th of November, 1669. By Deed bearing date March 9th, 1666, John Pyncheon conveyed to Samuel Chapin the greater part of the land lying in the Valley between Chicopee River and
2 CONC  Willimansett Brook. And by Deed bearing date April 16th, 1673, Samuel conveyed the same premises to his son Japhet Chapin. The said Japhet built him a house at the upper end of Chicopee street, north-westerly of where the dwellin
2 CONC g house of Henry Sherman now (1862) stands.
2 CONT Japhet was at the great fight at Turner's fulls, May iSth, 1676, and on the outside leaf of an old account book belonging to said Japhet, I find the following, supposed to be in his hand-writing. 
2 CONT " I went out Volenteare against ingens the 17th of May, 1676 and we ingaged batel the 19th of May in the moaning before sunrise and made great Spoil upon the enemy and came off the same day with the, Los of 37 men and the Captin T
2 CONC urner, and came home the 20th of May." 
2 CONT Thomas Chapin, son of Japhet, was one of the original grantees of the large tract of land which was granted to the officers and soldiers and their descendants in the Falls fight. And on another leaf of the same book, I find the fo
2 CONC llowing,
2 CONT "my father was taken out of this troubelsom world the 11 day of November about eleven of the clock in the eve, 1675." 
2 CONT After the death of Japhet Cbapin, the Rev. Mr. Williams of Deerfield wrote a lengthy letter to his children, instructing them concerning the improvement which they should make of his death, and speaking of him as having been a man
2 CONC  of great piety. This letter is now (1859) probably in the care of Mr. Dormer Chapin.[32]
2 CONT
