1 NOTE From Ancestral File (TM), data as of 5 JAN 1998.
1 NOTE  Name may be Elaphalet
2 CONT  Some sources say born Roxbury, Suffolk County, MA.  Some have 15 Aug 1642.
2 CONT  Some sources say married Milford, New Haven County, CT
1 NOTE  Name may be Elaphalet
2 CONT  Some sources say born Roxbury, Suffolk County, MA.  Some have 15 Aug 1642.
2 CONT  Some sources say married Milford, New Haven County, CT
1 NOTE SECOND GENERATION.
2 CONT (2)
2 CONT II. Japhet Chapin, of Chicopee, son of Dea. Samuel Chapin,
2 CONT b. 1642; m. (1) July 22, 1664, Abileuah Cooley. Mrs. Abilenah
2 CONT Chapin d. Nov. 17, 1710. And a small stone in the old burying
2 CONT ground in Springfield marks the spot where she was buried, m. (2)
2 CONT May 31, 1711, Dorothy Root of Eifield, Ct. He d. Feb. 20, 1712,
2 CONT and was buried by the side of his first wife Abilenah. Their remains
2 CONT and the stones whi'ch mark their resting places have (probably) been
2 CONT removed to the new cemetery in Springfield.
2 CONT Japhet probably resided for a time in Milford, Connecticut-
2 CONT As the Worshipful Capt. John Pyncheon of Springfield conveyed to
2 CONT Japhet Chapin of Milford, in Connecticut Colony, a small strip of
2 CONT land near Connecticut River in Springfield, Bounds east on Deacon
2 CONT Chapin's land. Deed dated 16th of November, 1669.
2 CONT By Deed bearing date March 9th, 1666, John Pyncheon conveyed
2 CONT to Samuel Chapin the greater part of the land lying in the Valley
2 CONT between Chicopee River and Willimansett Bro(^. And by Deed
2 CONT bearing date April 16th, 1673, Samuel conveye^the same premises
2 CONT to his son Japhet Chapin. The said Japhet built him a house at
2 CONT the upper end of Chicopee street, north-westerly of where the
2 CONT dwelling house of Henry Sherman now (1862) stands.
2 CONT 4 SECOND GENERATION.
2 CONT Japhet was at the great fight at Turner's fulls, May iSth, 1676,
2 CONT and on the outside leaf of an old account book belonging to said
2 CONT Japhet, I find the following, supposed to be in his hand-writing.
2 CONT " I went out Volenteare against ingens the 17th of May, 1676 and
2 CONT we ingaged batel the 19th of May in the moaning before sunrise
2 CONT and made great Spoil upon the enemy and came off the same day
2 CONT with the, Los of 37 men and the*Captin Turner, and came home the
2 CONT 20th^ of May." Tliomas Cbapin, son of Japhet, was one of the
2 CONT original grantees of the large tract of land which was granted to
2 CONT the officers and soldiers and their descendants in the Falls fight.
2 CONT And on another leaf of the same book, I find the following,
2 CONT " my
2 CONT father was taken out of this troubelsom world the 11 day of November
2 CONT about eleven of the clock in the eve, 1675." After the death of
2 CONT Japhet Cbapin, the Rev. Mr. Williams of Deerfield wrote a lengthy
2 CONT letter to his children, instructing them concerning the improvement
2 CONT which they should make of his death, and speaking of him as having
2 CONT been a man of great piety. This letter is now (1859) probably in
2 CONT the care of Mr. Dormer Chapin.
1 NOTE Deacon of the Congrgational Church.  Probably for time resided in Milford, Conn.  John Pyncho n deeded land in Springfield to him 1669.  He was engaged in the battle with the Indains at Turners Falls in 1676, and was a deacon in t
2 CONC he church in Springfield.
2 CONT Chapin link:  <http://worldconnect.rootsweb.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?db=deannedriscoll>
2 CONT Note: Japhet was born 1642.  Was in the "great Falls Fight at Turner's Falls, Mass.  May 16 1676 where the Indians surprised and in conflict they owned that they lost in killed and drowned 300 warriors, while the English had only 
2 CONC one soldier killed. (From the Descendants of the Chapins Who Fought to Free America from British Rule.)
