1 NOTE From Ancestral File (TM), data as of 2 January 1996.
1 NOTE He was killed by the Indians.  Samuel Chapin set out two pear trees which bore fruit for the first time the year he died, 1730.
2 CONT Samuel was a farmer.  Mr. Samuel Chapin had been at his daily labor on the land which he owned on the west side of Conn. river and had just entered a boat on his return home at night, when he was fired upon by some Indians (wounde
2 CONC d by not dangerously) lying in ambush among the willows which grew near the water.  (from the Samuel Chapin Genealogy by Rev. Samuel Chapin.
1 NOTE Samuel CnAPm, son of Japhet and Abilene, b. July 4,
2 CONT 1665 ; m. Dec. 24, 1690, to Hannah Sheldon. He d. Oct. 19, 1729.
2 CONT His place of residence was at the upper end of Chicopee street,
2 CONT on the west side and near the residence of his father Japhet. His
2 CONT house stood not far from the place where Ephraim Chapin in after
2 CONT years built a house and where he resided and where his grandson
2 CONT Briant Chapin now (1862) resides. It was afterwards owned and
2 CONT occupied by his son Elisha, and after he was killed by the Indians,
2 CONT it passed into the possession of Abel, son of Thomas, and afterwards
2 CONT to Ephraim, son of Benjamin, who married Jemima, daughter of Abel,
2 CONT and after Ephraim's death to his son Frederick, and after his death
2 CONT to his son Briant. Samuel Chapin set out two pear trees on this
2 CONT place which bore fruit for the first time the year he died, 1730. One
2 CONT of said trees is still alive, (1859) and never failed to bear fruit,
2 CONT except one year, up to 1834, and has occasionally borne fruit since
2 CONT that time. The above pear tree bore a sweet and most delicious
2 CONT fruit, as the compiler of this work can testify, having ate of the fruit
2 CONT more than sixty years since, and often since that time. Said tree
2 CONT having become much decayed was felled with the woodman's axe,
2 CONT December, 1859.
2 CONT Mr. Samuel Chapin had been at his daily labor on land which he
2 CONT owned on the west side of Conn, river and had just entered a boat
2 CONT on his return home at night, when he was fired upon by some Indians
2 CONT (and wounded though not dangerously) lying in ambush among the
2 CONT willows which grew near the water.
2 CONT At the marriage of Samuel Chapin's daughter Abilene, there
2 CONT being a large number of guests and a short supply of seats, the
2 CONT young Misses were seated upon the sills of the house. The sills in
2 CONT those days were very large and placed on the top of the floors, or
2 CONT the bottom of the sills were as high as the top of the floors. When
2 CONT the dressmaker who made and fitted the bride's dress entered the
2 CONT room, and perceiving the large array of young Misses, exclaimed I
2 CONT wonder if all these little girls will ever become Brides, which remark
2 CONT caused quite a tittering among the juvenile portion of the females.
