1 NOTE !BIOGRAPHY: http://1704.deerfield.history.museum/people/short_bios.jsp
2 CONT Hannah Chapin Sheldon
2 CONT Hannah, the daughter of Japhet Chapin of Springfield, MA, married John Sheldon Jr. on December 3, 1703. They jumped from their chamber window during the assault. Hannah injured her ankle and sent her husband off to Hatfield to enl
2 CONC ist help. She was taken captive and lived with the French in Montreal until her father-in-law, Ensign John Sheldon, negotiated her release in May, 1705. Hannah returned to Deerfield, where she and John, Jr. had four children befor
2 CONC e her husband died in 1713. Hannah married Lieutenant Timothy Childs November 26, 1719. She lived to be eighty-five, dying on September 30, 1765.
2 CONT
2 CONT !BIOGRAPHY: http://www.memorialhall.mass.edu/collection
2 CONT "History of the Connecticut Valley in Massachusetts"
2 CONT printer J. B. Lippincott [Printer] 	
2 CONT author Louis Everts 	
2 CONT date 1879 	
2 CONT location Philadelphia, Pennsylvania 	
2 CONT height 12.0" 	
2 CONT width 9.0" 	
2 CONT process/materials printed paper, ink 	
2 CONT item type Books/Book 	
2 CONT accession # #L98.040 	
2 CONT Page 617
2 CONT Capt. Timothy Childs was born in 1686, and married, in 1719, Hannah (Chapin), widow of John Sheldon. He was an active scout in Queen Anne’s war, serving under the noted Capt. Benjamin Wright. He was fired upon and slightly wounded
2 CONC , July 10, 1724, while in the meadow at work, near Pine Hill. September, 1724, he was a lieutenant under Capt. Kellogg, in command of the forces at Deerfield and Sunderland. He died in 1766. His son Timothy settled in Gill, was a 
2 CONC captain in the Revolutionary war, and died Dec. 12, 1781, leaving a son, Timothy, who was a celebrated doctor in Pittsfield.
1 NOTE The town of Deerfield was attacked by Indians about three months after her marriage, and sh e was taken captive with many others, and marched to Canada and after about two years amny o f the captives were redeemed and returned hom
2 CONC e.  Her husband might have gone to Canada and ob tained her release before the others.  She could have been born in Milford, New Haven, Ct.
2 CONT The Chapin Book states " Hannah Chapin "the plunky daughter of Japhet," m. Dec 3 1703 to Joh n Shelton who built at Deerfield the Old Indian House.  In 1704, during the attack on Deerfie ld while the enemy was cutting through the 
2 CONC door of the house, John and Hannah jumped from th e second-story window.  In making the jump, she sprained her ankle.  Notwithstanding her inju ryshe urged her husband to leave her and hasten to Hatfield to give the alarm.  He, be
2 CONC ing bar efoot, tore strips from a blanket and bound them on his feet to keep them from freezing whil e on the way to Harfield.  Hannah was taken to Canada and was there two years.  She was redee med by her father-in-law, John Shel
2 CONC ton.  Esther Williams. also a captive in Canada, writes th at he was surprised that Hannah escaped death, so great were her sufferings on the March to C anada.  (From Maria N. Greene, Troy, N.Y.)
1 NOTE *Hannah was married Dec. 3, 1703, to John Sheldon of Deerfield.
2 CONT The town of Deerfield was attacked by the Indians about
2 CONT three months after her marriage, and she was taken captive with
2 CONT many others, and marched to Canada, and after about two years
2 CONT many of the captives were redeemed and, returned home. I have
2 CONT reasons for believing that Hannah's husband went to Canada and
2 CONT obtained her release before the release of the other prisoners. John,
2 CONT son of John and Hannah Sheldon of Deerfield, born in Springfield,
2 CONT April 12, 1706.
