1 TITL Ensign
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} A Patriot of the American Revolution for CONNECTICUT with the rank of Ensign.  [http://services.dar.org/Public/DAR_Research/search_adb/?action=full&p_id=A020792  DAR Ancestor # A020792]
2 CONT
2 CONT Nathaniel Chapin was born on 31 December 1738 to Nathaniel Chapin (1711-1745) and Sarah Abbe (1718-1785) in Springfield, Hampden, Massachusetts. He married Sibyl Terry (1740-1775) on 10 December 1761 in Enfield, Hartford, Connecti
2 CONC cut. They had seven children together; most of whom survived childhood. Sibyl passed away on 26 June 1775 in the early months of the war. Nathaniel married Zerviah Parsons (1740-1822) shortly after Sibyl’s death in 1775. Nathaniel
2 CONC  and Zerviah had no children together. During the American Revolutionary War, Chapin served as Ensign in Col. Comfort Sage’s Regiment; Wadsworth’s Brigade of the Connecticut State Levies. Chapin was made Ensign of 2d Company, 3d B
2 CONC attalion, by vote of the assembly. Ensign Chapin’s military career began on 6 June 1776. His battalion was sent to the aid of General George Washington at New York in 1776. Ensign Chapin was caught in the retreat from Long Island 
2 CONC in 1776 and he subsequently fought in the Battle of White Plains on Chatterton Hill on 28 October 1776. He left the service upon the completion of his six-month term in December 1776. Ensign Chapin survived the war and passed away
2 CONC  on 12 February 1831, in Enfield, Hartford, Connecticut at the age of 92. Ensign Nathaniel Chapin was buried at Enfield Street Cemetery in Enfield, Hartford, Connecticut.  
2 CONT
