1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ---- '''Benjamin Parsons'''
2 CONT * Christened:	1 May 1625, St. Mary's, Beaminster, Dorset, England to Williami Parsons 
2 CONT * Died: 24 Aug 1689,  Springfield, Hampden County, Massachusetts
2 CONT * Parents: William Parsons and Margaret Hoskins, married 15 Nov. 1602 
2 CONT * Wives: Sarah Vore, Sarah Heald
2 CONT
2 CONT Came to this country & was among the early settlers of Springfield, MA. Chief instrument in the formation of the Springfield church. Deacon & prominent citizen of exemplary moral character.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Biography==
2 CONT
2 CONT He came to New England about 1650, met and married Sarah at Windsor, Massachusetts 6 October 1653. She was probably born in Dorchester, Massachusetts about 1635, daughter of Richard and Ann Vore.
2 CONT
2 CONT Benjamin was still a resident of Beaminster in March 1649/50, when he surrendered his cottage there. He apparently left soon after, for he first appeared in Springfield, Massachusetts on 4 November 1651, when he was appointed a fe
2 CONC nce viewer.
2 CONT
2 CONT Benjamin Parsons took an active role in town affairs in Springfield, serving as surveyor of highways, fence viewer, juror, constable and selectman. He was active in establishing the First Congregational Church there in 1679 and be
2 CONC came a deacon. When he was chosen a selectman in February 1678/9, his name was not prefixed with a title, but at a meeting of the selectmen on 2 June 1679, he was designated as "Deacon” Benja Parsons. He owned land in Springfield,
2 CONC  Suffield, and Enfield; the latter two towns then in Massachusetts but now in Connecticut.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is one of six settlers that are named on the Enfield Plantation deed. That deed was negated with the Indians on 16 March 16 80. He never lived in Enfield, but was given more than one parcel of land. In 1691, B. Parsons, S. Terr
2 CONC y & I. Morgan were given a tract of land to build a sawmill. This was the first building built East of Enfield. Two of Deacon Benjamin’s sons, Benjamin II and Samuel settled in Enfield.
2 CONT
2 CONT The will of "Deacon Benja Parsons deceased - of Springfd," dated 20 February 1687/8 and probated 30 March 1690, named his sons: Benjamin, Samuel, Joseph, Ebenezer and Hezekiah; daughters: Mary "who is as yet unmarried," Sarah Dorc
2 CONC hester and Abigail Richards; and grandsons: John Parsons, John Dorchester, James Dorchester, Benjamin Mun, John Mun and John Richards. His sons Benjamin and Joseph were to be executors
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Origins
2 CONT
2 CONT Benjamin, an early settler of Springfield, Massachusetts, was baptized May 1, 1625 at St. Mary's Church in Beaminster, Dorset, England, son of William Parsons and Margaret Hoskins.
2 CONT
2 CONT Benjamin sailed with his brother Joseph  and others of the family from Gravesend, England, in the bark "Transport," bound for America in 1635.  (Comment: also seen as immigrated in 1644, and as 1650).
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born in Great Torrington, near Exeter, Devonshire, England (sic)  and is supposed to have descended from Sir Thomas Parsons, of Milton Royal, who was knighted by Charles II, 1634.  The record states that 
2 CONT
2 CONT >"Benjamin Parsons was a grandson of Thomas Parsons, of Great Milton, Oxfordshire, England, who died in 1597." (2)
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: This was a different Benjamin Parsons (son of https://www.geni.com/people/Hugh-Parsons-of-Great-Milton/6000000006519122520)
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
