1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Abigail Parsons was born in 1663 in Springfield, Hampshire County, Massachusetts Bay Colony. She was the daughter of Deacon Benjamin Parsons (born before May 1, 1625, in Beaminster, Dorset, England; died August 24, 1689, in Spring
2 CONC field, Massachusetts) and hie first wife Sarah (Vore) Parsons (born about 1635, died January 1, 1676, in Springfield, Massachusetts). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Her death date and location remain unknown. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Research Note on Death Date:'''  While some sources mistakenly cite June 27, 1689, in Springfield, this date is a known conflation with Abigail Colton. Abigail (Parsons) Richards is documented as living well after 1694; she sur
2 CONC vived the 1704 Deerfield Massacre and participated in property transactions in Deerfield as late as 1709. While family accounts and the movement of her husband John Richards suggest a relocation to Newark, New Jersey, between 1709
2 CONC  and 1713, primary documentation confirming Abigail's presence in New Jersey is absent. It is possible she passed away in New England prior to the move, or in Newark between 1709 and her husband’s death in 1718.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Marriages
2 CONT
2 CONT Abigail married first to John Munn on December 23, 1680. John Munn (born 1652, son of Benjamin Munn) settled in Westfield, Massachusetts, and was injured in the 1676 Falls Fight during King Philip’s War; he died on September 16, 1
2 CONC 684, of a "wasting sickness" contracted during that conflict. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Following his death, Abigail married John Richards, Jr. (schoolmaster) on October 7, 1686 in Hartford, Connecticut. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Abigail’s life was defined by the frequent moves and frontier challenges faced by her husband, John Richards, who served as a schoolmaster in Enfield, Connecticut (1697), and later in Deerfield, Massachusetts (1698). In Deerfield,
2 CONC  where John served as a Tax Assessor (1700/1) and Selectman (1702).  They were victims of the French and Indian [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raid_on_Deerfield Raid on Deerfield] of February 29, 1704.  Their house was burned, and
2 CONC  their daughter Jemima (aged 9 or 10) was captured and never returned, presumed a casualty of the event. Two weeks later, the family retreated to Enfield. By 1707, they were in Westfield, and by 1709, John had rebuilt their Deerfi
2 CONC eld home, which he sold to his stepson, Benjamin Munn. Sometime in the 1709–1713 period, the family moved from New England to Newark, New Jersey.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT === Family
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Parents'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * Father: Deacon Benjamin Parsons (1625 – 1689)
2 CONT * Mother: Sarah (Vore) Parsons (c. 1635 – 1676)
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Spouses'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * John Munn (1652 – 1684): Married December 23, 1680.
2 CONT * John Richards, Jr. (c. 1662 – 1718): Married October 7, 1686.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Children'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * From marriage to John Munn:
2 CONT * John Munn (born March 16, 1681; participated in the 1704 Meadow Fight; died 1730 in New Jersey)
2 CONT * James Munn (born circa 1683, Westfield, Massachusetts)
2 CONT * Benjamin Munn (born 1683; married Thankful Nims, daughter of Godfrey Nims, on January 5, 1702/3; died July 11, 1746)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT * From marriage to John Richards, Jr.:
2 CONT * Thomas Richards (born November 7, 1681; died May 1, 1730; married Mary)
2 CONT * John Richards, III (1687 – 1748)
2 CONT * Abigail (Richards) Bigelow (born January 13, 1688, in Westfield; married John Bigelow)
2 CONT * Jemima Richards (presumed casualty of the 1704 Deerfield Massacre)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT === Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT * Allen, Francis. ''The History of Enfield, Connecticut''.  Lancaster, PA: Wickersham Printing Co., 1900.
2 CONT * Bigelow Society. ''John (3) Bigelow and Abigail Richards Family''.[http://bigelowsociety.com/rod/richards.htm] No grave for John Richards' wife Abigail can be found in the cemetery today, nor was one reported in 1894 when all th
2 CONC e tombstone inscriptions in the cemetery were transcribed and published by F. D. Halsey and W. 0. Wheeler. This suggests that Abigail probably died before her husband, John, their daughter, Abigail and their son-in-law, John 3 Big
2 CONC elow, moved on to Whippany. 
2 CONT * Crane, Ellery Bicknell. ''Genealogy of the Crane Family'', Vol. 2, p. 308. Worcester, Mass.: Press of Charles Hamilton, 1895. [https://archive.org/details/genealogyofcrane02cran/page/n335/mode/2up]
2 CONT * Sheldon, George. ''A History of Deerfield, Massachusetts'', Vol. 2, p. 243. Deerfield, Mass.: E.A. Hall & Company, 1896. [https://archive.org/details/historyofdeerfie02shel/page/243/mode/1up]
2 CONT * Torrey, Clarence.  ''New England Marriages to 1700''. AmericanAncestors.org, New England Historic Genealogical Society, 2008.
2 CONT * https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raid_on_Deerfield
