1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT 1996 - Beverly Branning Green, 4406 Island Drive, North Topsail Beach, NC 28460      910-328-0939       olsalte@aol.com
2 CONT
2 CONT Darbytown - takes its name from N.S. Darby, who ran a tannery there for many years.  The business was commenced by Charles and Webb Horton and George Knapp, who built the tannery in 1851-52, and ran it under the style of Horton & 
2 CONC Co. For a few years. This firm also ran a store in connection with their business. They afterwards sold out to Mr. Darby, who operated the tannery until 1876, when the supply of bark in the neighborhood was about exhausted.  There
2 CONC  is nothing done in the place at present.
2 CONT
2 CONT From Forts of Pennsylvania - In the Wyoming Valley. pg 455 The settlements along the Delaware in what is now Pike county had built "forts" as they were called, but in reality their preparations for defence consisted in simple stoc
2 CONC kades around several of the stronger dwelling houses.  One of these was at Dingman's;  one at Capt. Johannes Van Etten's, three or four miles above Dingman's; amother, "fort Decker," about three miles below Dingman's, on Hornbeck 
2 CONC creek; and still another was "Fort Brink," where Jahn and Garrett Brink lived, two or three miles above bushkill. Emanuel Gunsalus [Gonzales] lived at the Bushkill, and his house, too, was placed in as good condition for defence a
2 CONC s was possible. As a matter of interest it is mentioned, that the "Manor of Wallenpaupack" was surveyed October 14, 1751, upon warrant dated November 25th, 1748, "for the use of the  proprietaries of Pennsylvania," and as if in ma
2 CONC rked defiance of the proprietaries, this body of men elected it for their settlement.  It was within the limits of the Delaware company's purchase and founded under the authority of that company, though its communications and asso
2 CONC ciations were always more with Wyoming; and when the county of Westmoreland was erected the "Manor" was included and brought within its jurisdiction. On the banks of the Delaware river at a point near the mouth of a stream known a
2 CONC s Caulkins creek, in Damascus township, Wayne county, a blockhouse was built by Simeon Caulkins, Moses Thomas and others, in the year 1755. The site is on tract No. 178 in Damascus, surveyed to John Lande.  It was the pioneer sett
2 CONC lement made by Connecticut  people in Pennsyhlvania, and was made by the Delaware Company, and by them named Cushutunk, after an Indian village lately existing at that placse.  In 1763 it contained about thirty log houses, a grist
2 CONC  mill and a saw mill besides the blockhouse.  In November of that year it was attacked by Indians from Wyoming, and of the three men present, one was killed and another wounded, before they could reach the fort, but it was success
2 CONC fully defended by the third, named Witters, who with
2 CONT
2 CONT An Iris Fields [an unreliable collector of names] also lists children : Cyrus Thomas 1752 Stafford Ct. Mary Thomas 1758 Stafford CT Rhoda Thomas 1760 Stafford CT
