2 CONT 61. William Dodge Quartus3, 5 was born in 1665 in Beverly, Essex, Massachusetts and died4 before 20 Dec 1708 in Beverly, Essex, Massachusetts.
2 CONT
2 CONT     10 Oct 1702: Captain John DODGE conveyed his property to his son, often named in the records as William DODGE, Quartus.
2 CONT
2 CONT     (2) 1703: Capt. John DODGE deeded to his son William some estate, and William covenants t o him 5 lbs. annually during John's life and provides for his wife Elizabeth during her life.
2 CONT
2 CONT     (3) 1708, 20 Dec: Ebenezer WOODBURY of Salem, miller, Nathaniel WALDRON of Wenham, bricklayer, William DODGE Jr. of Beverly, malster, and Jonathan RAYMENT of Beverly, yeoman, all administrators of the estate of William DODGE o
2 CONC f Beverly, deceased, lease to Jonathan DODGE of Salem for 7 years after 1 April next, the home living which was formerly Capt. John DODGE's, now belonging to the children of said William DODGE, deceased, partly in Beverly and in S
2 CONC alem , containing 54 acres, with house, barn and mills, being all set out in a deed of gift from Captain John DODGE, to said William DODGE. Also 4 acres of fresh meadow and 3 acres land in Proctor's Island, Chebacco. He died intes
2 CONC tate, and his administrators, among whom was his wife's brother Ebenezer WOODBERY, leased the land and mills his father had deeded to him, including John WOODBURY's homestead, to Jonathan DODGE, William's brother. The lease was to
2 CONC  extend 7 years from 1 Apr 1709.
2 CONT
2 CONT     (4) 1710, 7 Jun: Ebenezer WOODBURY deposed that he was present Oct 1702 at Capt. John DODGE 's house and was witnessed to the transcation between said John DODGE and his son William and set his hand as witness to the deed, and
2 CONC  said William gave a deed back to his father or his brother Jonathan, as his father desired 11 acres in Beverly and also surrendered a dwelling house to said father or brother Jonathan for him which he had built on a piece his fat
2 CONC her h ad given him by word of mouth.
2 CONT
2 CONT     (5) 1720/1, 21 Mar: William DODGE's real estate was divided. The saw mill became the proper ty of William DODGE's son-in-law, Samuel WOODBERY, another son-in-law Joseph CREESY, and a minor daughter, Abigail. Away, 1 1/2 rods w
2 CONC ide, was reserved for the use of the heirs, who also had the privilege of raising the water when it was needed at the mill and of flowing the land from Oct 31 to Apr 10 so long as the mill was "Repareable." Not only Capt. John DOD
2 CONC GE's property lying west of the head of Bass River, but also that east of the river and the brook, the land which had been John WOODBERY's, passed through Capt. John DODGE's son William DODGE to the latter's son-in-law, Samuel WOO
2 CONC DBERY.
2 CONT
2 CONT     William married. 
2 CONT
2 CONT     William married Hannah Woodbury, daughter of Unknown and Elizabeth. (Hannah Woodbury was baptized on 22 May 1670 in , Essex Co., Massachusetts.) 
2 CONT
2 CONT     Their children were: 
2 CONT
2 CONT        110 F    i. Hannah Dodge .
2 CONT
2 CONT         21 Mar 1720/1: William DODGE's real estate was divided. The saw mill became the property of William DODGE's son-in-law, Samuel WOODBERY, another son-in-law Joseph CREESY, and a minor daughter, Abigail. Away, 1 1/2 rods wid
2 CONC e, was reserved for the use of the heirs, who also had the privilege of raising the water when it was needed at the mill and of flowing the land from Oct 31 to Apr 10 so long as the mill was "Repareable."
2 CONT
2 CONT         Hannah married Samuel Woodbury, son of Thomas Woodbury and Hannah Dodge. (Samuel Woodbury was born in 1665-1666 in Beverly, Essex Co., Massachusetts and died on 18 Apr 1689 in Beverly, Essex Co., Massachusetts.) 
