1 NOTE {geni:about_me} GARVIS  GILBERT -- THE  GILBERTS OF HARFORD COUNTY MARYLAND:  Garvis Gilbert, our emigrant ancestor, was born 15 April 1680 in Leeds, Yorkshire, England.  He married twice and surnames of both his wives are unknown
2 CONC .  His first wife was Margaret and his second wife was Mary.  The first child of Garvis was born 1707 in Baltimore County, Maryland (now Harford County).  From this record we know that he was in this country in early 1700.  His na
2 CONC me first appears in Baltimore County in 1699 in the Middle River Hundred, 
2 CONT
2 CONT Garvis  Gilbert (sometimes Gervais, Jarvis, Garvies) may have been the son of Michael  Gilbert and Dorothy Hargrave.  If so he was born 15 April 1680 at Leeds, Yorkshire, England.  While there has been no primary proof that Garvis
2 CONC  who came to Maryland was their son, so much circumstantial evidence shows this to be true, that after analysis and research by many different parties this is considered [by some to be] a reasonable conclusion. (Marcum, “The Way i
2 CONC t Was with our Ancestors.")
2 CONT
2 CONT The name Garvis  Gilbert (sometimes spelled Jarvis or Gervais) first appears in Baltimore (now Harford) County, Maryland, in 1699 in the Middle River Hundred.  (From Taxables in Baltimore County, 1699, Maryland Historical Magazine
2 CONC , Vol 8, page 64).  (Henry C Peden Jr (707 Bedford Rd, Bel Air, Maryland, 21014),  Gilbert Records in Harford County, Maryland, Bel Air Copy Center, Bel Air, Maryland, ©1986:
2 CONT
2 CONT Baltimore County, Maryland Tax Lists (from LDS records) show the following: 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1702-Jervis Gilbard, South Gunpowder Hd; 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1703-Gervais  Gilbert, South Gunpowder Hd; 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1704-Jervis Gilbert, North Gunpowder Hd;
2 CONT
2 CONT 1705-Garvies Gilbert, North Gunpowder Hd; 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1706-Jarvis  Gilbert, North Gunpowder Hundred.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4 Nov 1707 - Garvis  Gilbert purchased a tract of land located on the head of the middle branch of the Bush River in North Gunpowder Hundred, Baltimore County, Maryland, from William Lowe and his wife Rebecca (Marcum citing Baltim
2 CONC ore County, Maryland, land deed records (Liber HW, No. B, folio 49).  According to Marcum this was the homestead of Garvis throughout his life. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Garvis Gilbert paid 5,000 pounds of tobacco to William Lough (Lowe) for the property and it was laid out as 100 acres, more or less, and contained plantation houses, edifices, buildings, fences, orchards, gardens, pastures and woo
2 CONC ds, became known as Lowe's Lot and lay near the line of the land called The Agreement.  (According to Davis (Baltimore County Land Records Vol RM:561-562),
2 CONT
2 CONT 22 Jan 1722 until 11 Apr 1726, Vestry records of Spesutia Church, St George's Parish show that Garvis Gilbert was a Vestryman at the Spesutia Church.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1727 - "In 1727 he (Garvis  Gilbert) bought Hall's Pipe, 145 acres on Swan Creek for 50 pounds Sterling.  He also bought Clark's Tobacco, 71 acres on Swan Creek (Wilbur E Davis, The Gilbert Family).
2 CONT
2 CONT 1735 - Garvis  Gilbert, "for the natural love and affection which I have and do bear unto my son Michael  Gilbert," gave Michael land to be released to his brothers at the time of his (Garvis') death:  Clark's Tobacco, 60 acres; H
2 CONC all's Pipe, 145 acres;  Gilbert's Chance, 40 acres. (Marcum citing Baltimore County, Maryland, land deed records (Lib. HWS, No. M, folio 302).
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 Jun 1739/17 Jun 1739 - The will of Gravis Gilbert of Baltimore County, planter, was dated June 2, 1739 and proved June 17, 1739. 
2 CONT
2 CONT To his son Samuel he gave a tract called  Gilberts Addition and part of  Gilbert's Outlet, joining to Edward Hall's Road; 
2 CONT
2 CONT To his son Garvis he gave Bow's (Lowe's) Lot and remainder of Outlet, joining; 
2 CONT
2 CONT To his son Charles he gave dwelling plantation Clark's Tobacco,  Gilbert's Chance, and Negro Man, Oliver, after demise of wife; 
2 CONT
2 CONT To his son Aquila he gave The Agreement and Negro woman, Charity, after demise of wife; 
2 CONT
2 CONT To his son Benjamin he gave ten pounds of money when he became of age; 
2 CONT
2 CONT to his son Daniel he gave ten pounds of money when he became of age; 
2 CONT
2 CONT To his daughter Hannah he gave a cow, one feather bed and furniture, and Negro child, Oliver, after demise of wife; 
2 CONT
2 CONT To his daughters, Mary and Martha, he gave each ten pounds of money when they became age 16 or at their marriage. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The executors of this will were his wife Mary and Michael  Gilbert. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Wit: Obd Pritchard, James Pritchard, and James Canell (Camell,Camill).). 
2 CONT
2 CONT (Will dated 2 Jun 1739 and filed 10 Jun 1739 by Obadiah Pritchard and James Pritchard. Administration bond posted 7 Nov 1739 by the executors with John Durbin and Samuel Pritchard securities;  estate administered 12 Aug 1740 by ex
2 CONC ecs). 
2 CONT
2 CONT The date of death of Garvis Gilbert has been determined to be 5/6 Jun 1739. (Marcum citing Baltimore County, Maryland, Perogative Court Wills 22, page 79).
2 CONT
2 CONT The property of Gravis Gilbert, dec'd, distributed to his sons by executor Michael Gilbert follow:
2 CONT
2 CONT •1740 (HWS, LS, 420) Samuel Gilbert, Clark's Tobacco, 60 acres. 
2 CONT
2 CONT •1740 (HWS, IA, 413) Jarvis Gilbert, Lowe's Lot, 100 acres, part of  Gilbert's Outlet, 125 acres. 
2 CONT
2 CONT •1742 (TB, C, 204) Charles Gilbert, Clarke's Tobacco, 40 acres; Union, 60 acres;  Gilbert's Chance. 
2 CONT
2 CONT •1753 (ABB, J, 17) Aquilla Gilbert, Hall's Mill Run, 200 acres. 
2 CONT
2 CONT (Marcum citing Baltimore County MD land deed records)
2 CONT
2 CONT Even though Michael Gilbert is not named in Garvis' will as his son, it is proven in a gift deed in the Hall of Records in Annapolis, Maryland, that Garvis Gilbert  "for the natural love and affection which I have and do bear unto
2 CONC  my son Michael  Gilbert." he gave land to Michael to be released to his brothers at the time of his (Garvis) death. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Church Register shows the death of Charles Gilbert son of Gervase and Mary, and also the death of their daughter, Mary. 
2 CONT
2 CONT It is surmised that after the death of these children, Gervase and Mary had other children of the same name, as they are mentioned in his will of 1739. It was a familiar custom that when one child was named and died as an infant, 
2 CONC another one would be named the same name. Garvis Gilbert fathered fifteen children and his line established the  Gilbert family in Harford County, Maryland. The microfilm records of St. John's and St. George's parishes are full of
2 CONC  birth, death, and marriage records on the  Gilbert's of Harford County Maryland.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1727 - Wilbur E Davis, The Gilbert Family: "In 1727 he (Garvis Gilbert) bought Hall's Pipe, 145 acres on Swan Creek for 50 pounds Sterling.  He also bought Clark's Tobacco, 71 acres on Swan Creek.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1735 - Marcum citing Baltimore County, Maryland land deed records (Lib. HWS, No. M, folio 302): Garvis  Gilbert, "for the natural love and affection which I have and do bear unto my son Michael Gilbert," gave Michael land to be re
2 CONC leased to his brothers at the time of his (Garvis') death: Clark's Tobacco, 60 acres; Hall's Pipe, 145 acres;  Gilbert's Chance, 40 acres.
2 CONT
2 CONT From about 1748 until the late 1750's five of Garvis Gilbert's sons, Samuel, Garvis, Aquilla, Benjamin and Daniel moved to Bedford (formerly Lunenburg and now Campbell) County, Virginia.  Two of his sons, Michael and Charles, rema
2 CONC ined in Baltimore County, MD; however, some of their children later moved to Virginia.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1773 the northeastern end of Baltimore County, Maryland was carved off to establish the new county of Harford, with its county seat at Bel Air. The coastal portion of Hartford County contains the towns of Havre de Grace of Aber
2 CONC deen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Most of Garvis' property lay west of the town of Havre De Grace and to the south and east of Aberdeen, including the northern portion of the present Aberdeen Proving Grounds. Descedants of Garvis Gilbert's sons, Michael and Charle
2 CONC s Gilbert, inherited large portions of the original Garvis Gilbert estate.
