1 NAME Edward /Morris/ Sr.
2 GIVN Edward
2 SURN Morris
2 NSFX Sr.
2 _MARNM Morris
2 NICK Dea. Lieut. Edward Morris IV   Morice
2 NICK Morrison
2 NICK Edward Morris
2 NICK Edward Cartwright Boston mariner
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC Woodstock Hill Cemetery
2 ADDR
3 CITY Woodstock
3 STAE Connecticut
3 CTRY United States
1 BIRT
2 DATE 22 JAN 1630
2 ADDR
3 CITY Great Amwell
3 STAE England
1 DEAT
2 DATE 14 SEP 1689
2 ADDR
3 CITY Woodstock
3 STAE Connecticut Colony
1 OCCU Lt. and Deacon
1 TITL Lieut.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Lieut. Edward Morris=
2 CONT * BIRTH 22 Jan 1632 Great Amwell, East Hertfordshire District, Hertfordshire, England
2 CONT * DEATH 14 Sep 1690 (aged 58) Woodstock, Windham County, Connecticut, USA
2 CONT * BURIAL Woodstock Hill Cemetery, Woodstock, Windham County, Connecticut, USA Latitude: 41.9489784, Longitude: -71.9735565
2 CONT * MEMORIAL ID  11446909 Pictures added by Sarah Beane & lwag
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Biography==
2 CONT Edward was the son of Edward and Prudence Heath Morrison;[1] he was baptised at Great Amwell, Hertfordshire on January 22, 1631/2.[2] His father died before his first birthday and nothing is known of his life prior to arriving at 
2 CONC New England in the early 1650's.  He and his descendants adopted the "Morris" surname. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward is first noticed in February of 1652 when granted land in Roxbury (which he later forfeited.)[3]  On November 29, 1655 he married Grace Bett/Bell(?) at Boston[4] and they had at least eight children.[1]  All were born in Ro
2 CONC xbury[3] where Edward became a constable, a selectman, and Deputy to the General Court.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT As the population continued to grow, a group of Roxbury residents including Edward Morris formed a plan to create a new plantation, originally named "New Roxbury." Edward gained the title of Lieutenant of the "Soulgers of New Roxb
2 CONC ury" after this petition was approved:
2 CONT
2 CONT " New Roxbury July ye 8 1689.
2 CONT The Inhabitants of New Roxbury being mett together In ye sence of our great hazard and danger and our uncapacitie to defend ourselves, having no man Impowerd to order ye Souldiers for our defence... therefore ye Souldiers on ye af
2 CONC oresaid day make choyce of Edward Morris for Leftenant and William Bartholomew Jr Ensign if authority approve them."[6]
2 CONT In March of 1690, New Roxbury was renamed Woodstock (later to become Woodstock, Windham, Connecticut); at a town meeting in May, Edward was chosen to be one of three selectmen,[7] but he lived only a few months; Edward died on Sep
2 CONC tember 14, 1690.[8] His estate was settled
2 CONT
2 CONT "At a County Court holden in Boston January 27,1690-1 Power of administration to all and singular the Goods. Chattels, rights, or debts of Edward Morris late of Roxbury, Dec'd intestate, is granted unto Isaac Morris his Eldest Son
2 CONC ..."[9]
2 CONT --------------
2 CONT Was in New England by February of 1652/3.  His father died before he was born.  Although his father used the name Morrison, he changed it to Morris. Representative to the General Court 1678-1686.  A pioneer proprietor of Woodstock
2 CONC  CT, town's first military officer (Lieutenant).  A leader among the settlers.  His parents were Edward Morrison and Prudence Heath.  Prudence was sister to John Johnson's wife, so Edward Morris was closely connected to the Johnso
2 CONC n family of Roxbury and Woodstock.  This is one of many examples of how kinship influenced where immigrants settled. In 1683 the Massachusetts General Court approved the settlement of Woodstock from Roxbury.  In 1684 Edward Morris
2 CONC , along with John Ruggles and John Curtis, was sent to "view the wilderness and find a convenient place."  The town was originally called New Roxbury.  Judge Samuel Sewall proposed the name Woodstock.  Woodstock did not become par
2 CONC t of Connecticut until 1794 .[30]
2 CONT ---------------
2 CONT Edward was born in Essex county in England and both his parents died shortly afterwards.  He may have been raised by members of his mother's Heath relatives until he was old enough to travel to America. He might have done that sim
2 CONC ilar to the way his sister Elizabeth traveled as a servant when she was just 11 years old.
2 CONT
2 CONT He and his sister might have lived with either of two Heath uncles or an aunt in Roxbury, MA.  The first record of him is when he was granted land in Roxbury on February 3rd, 1652. In 1661 he was made chairman of a committee to su
2 CONC rvey the common land of the town, and was later paid to maintain it.  He was chosen one of the constables of the town in 1664.  He married in November of 1655 and had the first of 8 children in September of 1656.  He was admitted 
2 CONC to the church in 1658 and all of his children were baptised by the famous Reverend John Eliot.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward was chosen as a selectman of the town in 1674 and two years later as a trustee for the high school in Jamaica Plain.  In 1678 he was chosen as deputy to the General Court and served there for may years.  The town of Roxbury
2 CONC  gradually ran out of space for an expanding population and in 1682 Edward was involved in the purchase of a large plot of land from the Nipmuck indians in northern Connecticut. After the land was examined by a small group which i
2 CONC ncluded his oldest son Isaac , Edward's was one of the 40 families who decide to settle the town of New Roxbury in Connecticut in 1686.  The town was later renamed Woodstock.
2 CONT
2 CONT As one of the elders of the new town Edward was active in laying it out and was chosen as one of the first selectmen.  In 1689 when there was rising concern over troubles with indians Edward was named a Lieutenant, though there is
2 CONC  no record of any military engagement.  He became the first person to die in Woodstock in 1690 at the age of 57. [31]
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Chronology== [14]
2 CONT 2//23/1652/3 Immigrated to America
2 CONT 1653 Settled in Roxbury, MA. & was the 
2 CONT 1654 Elected Constable 
2 CONT 11/20/1655 Married Grace Bett at Boston, MA
2 CONT 1674-1687 Selectman of Roxbury 
2 CONT 1677-1687 Deputy to the General Court & colonial auditor, was one of the leading first settlers to Woodstock
2 CONT 1688',89',90' Selectman of Woodstock, A leader of the settlers of Woodstock and their first military officer.
2 CONT 1676 he was appointed one of three trustees for the High School at Jamaica Plain, which was instituted that year by a gift of land for its support by Hugh Thomas and his wife. 
2 CONT 1678 he was chosen deputy from Roxbury to the General Court, and was thereafter annually chosen until the vacation of the Colonial Charter
2 CONT 1686, a period of nine years, during which, with the exception of seven brief sessions of the court, he was the sole representative of the town.
2 CONT 9/14/1690 Woodstock Hill Cemtery 'Here lies buried the body of Lieut. Edward Morris. Deceas'd September 1690....
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Parents'''
2 CONT Edward Morris/Morrison 1595–1632
2 CONT Prudence Heath Morris  1597 – unknown
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Siblings'''
2 CONT Elizabeth (Morris) Cartwright
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Spouse''' 
2 CONT Grace Bett Morris 1634–1705 m. 11/20/1655 in Boston, MA
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Children'''
2 CONT Isaac Morris 9/16/1656- m Hannah Mayo
2 CONT Susannah Morris Clayton 1654–1710
2 CONT Dea. Edward Morris Jr. 3/10/1658–1727 m Elizabeth Bowen
2 CONT Grace Morris Child 2/7/1661–1723 m Benjamin Child
2 CONT Lieut Ebenezer Morris 4/14/1664–1718 m Sarah Davis
2 CONT Elizabeth Morris Child 3/25/1666–1752 m Joshua Child
2 CONT Margaret Morris Johnson 9/25/1668–1750 m Dea John Johnson
2 CONT Samuel Morris 3/19/1671–1745 m Mehitable Mayo
2 CONT Martha Philbrick Morris Lyon 1/30/1675–1756 m Dea William Lyon
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Notes==
2 CONT Many older publications state that Edward was the son of Thomas Morris and Grissie Hewsone. This was disproved in 1992 by Douglas Richardson.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT ==References==
2 CONT 1. https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Morris-2750
2 CONT
