1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT
2 CONT == Biography ==
2 CONT Joseph Davis was born on 22 December 1673 in Roxbury, Suffolk County, Massachusetts Bay Colony. He was the son of Joseph Davis (ca. 1647–1717) of Haverhill and Roxbury, and Sarah Chamberlain (1649–1717) of Roxbury. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He followed the trade of a cordwainer and remained a lifelong resident of the Roxbury-Brookline border in Suffolk County. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph Davis died in Roxbury on 23 December 1717, aged 44 years, just two days before the death of his father.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Parents ==
2 CONT *'''Father''': Joseph Davis; b. ca. 1647, Haverhill, Essex County; d. 25 Dec 1717, Roxbury, Suffolk County.
2 CONT *'''Mother''': Sarah Chamberlain; b. 18 Dec 1649, Roxbury, Suffolk County; d. 1717, Roxbury, Suffolk County.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Marriages and Issue ==
2 CONT
2 CONT == Issue by Sarah [Curtiss?] ==
2 CONT Joseph Davis and his first wife Sarah (d. bef. 1715) had the following issue, all born in Roxbury, Suffolk County:
2 CONT
2 CONT # Joseph Davis III; b. 26 Jul 1697; m. Sarah Curtis, 31 Jan 1722.
2 CONT # John Davis; b. 30 Oct 1699; m. Martha Griggs, 8 Jun 1721.
2 CONT # Sarah Davis; b. 2 May 1702; m. Joshua Healy, 21 Jan 1722.
2 CONT # Samuel Davis; b. 27 Jun 1704; m. Hannah Bacon, 1 Aug 1728.
2 CONT # Joshua Davis; b. 20 Nov 1706; m. Sarah Pierpont, 15 Dec 1731.
2 CONT # Benjamin Davis; b. 4 Mar 1710; m. Sybil Rockwood, 13 Mar 1734.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Audit Note ==
2 CONT The identification of Sarah's maiden name as "Curtiss" is frequently found in secondary 20th-century genealogical narratives but lacks a primary marriage record in Roxbury or Brookline. It is noted that her eldest son, Joseph III,
2 CONC  married a Sarah Curtis in 1722, which may be the source of onomastic confusion in later transcriptions.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Issue by Elizabeth Lane ==
2 CONT Joseph Davis and his second wife and widow, Elizabeth Lane, had the following issue recorded in Suffolk County:
2 CONT
2 CONT # Theodore Davis; b. ca. 1716, Brookline, Suffolk Co.
2 CONT # George Davis; b. ca. 1717, Brookline, Suffolk Co.
2 CONT # Ebenezer Davis; b. 11 Nov 1717, Brookline, Suffolk Co.  Married Sarah.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Audit Note ==
2 CONT Elizabeth Lane was married in Roxbury but the births of her children are associated with the family's residence in Brookline (formerly Muddy River). Ebenezer was born just six weeks prior to the death of Joseph Davis. Following th
2 CONC e 1717 probate, this branch of the family is frequently found in records associated with the migration to central Massachusetts and Connecticut.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Comment 
2 CONT The year 1717 was a period of high mortality for the Davis family of Roxbury. Joseph Davis (1673–1717) died on December 23rd, followed only two days later by both of his parents on Christmas Day. Such synchronized deaths in a sing
2 CONC le household strongly indicate a localized epidemic, likely Smallpox or a malignant fever, which were prevalent in Suffolk County at the time.  Malignant Fever	Recurring (inc. 1717)	Rapid onset; often kills multiple adults in 1–3 
2 CONC days.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == The 1717 Mortality Event ==
2 CONT Joseph Davis (1673–1717) died in Roxbury on 23 December 1717. In a catastrophic  localized epidemic, his father, Joseph Sr., and his mother, Sarah (Chamberlain) Davis, both died two days later on 25 December 1717. 
2 CONT
2 CONT === Impact on Issue ===
2 CONT Forensic analysis of the family unit suggests a high infant mortality rate during this outbreak. While their births are recorded in Brookline, the children '''Theodore''' and '''George Davis''' (sons of Elizabeth Lane) disappear f
2 CONC rom the archival record following 1717. They are presumed to have died during this winter "distemper."
2 CONT
2 CONT === Legal Administration ===
2 CONT * '''Administrator''': Elizabeth Lane (Widow); granted letters 1717 (Suffolk Case #3918).
2 CONT * '''Sole Surviving Heir (M2)''': Ebenezer Davis (b. Nov 1717); migrated to Oxford ca. 1739.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Post-1717 Timeline ==
2 CONT
2 CONT === Elizabeth Lane (Widow) ===
2 CONT * 1717–1722: Managed the Davis estate in Roxbury/Brookline as Administrator.
2 CONT * Post-1722: Likely remained in Suffolk County; identity potentially merged into the Ingraham or similar local lineage.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Ebenezer Davis (Posthumous Son) ===
2 CONT * 1717–1738: Raised in the Roxbury/Brookline area.
2 CONT * 1739: Removed to Oxford, Worcester County, joining half-siblings.
2 CONT * 1742: Married Lydia Dana (1725–1805).
2 CONT * 1790: Died in Oxford; estate distributed among 11 children.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Identity Audit Conclusion ==
2 CONT The survival of Ebenezer and the subsequent Oxford migration provides the definitive link between the 1717 Roxbury mortality event and the 19th-century genealogical records of Worcester County.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Identity Audit: The Oxford Migration Link ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Status: Verified Family Unit'''
2 CONT
2 CONT While the 1717 mortality event in Roxbury (claiming Joseph Davis Jr., Sr., and Sarah Chamberlain) created a high risk of family fragmentation, archival records  in Worcester County confirm the family remained a cohesive unit.
2 CONT
2 CONT # '''The Anchor:''' Joseph Davis III (b. 1697, M1) established the Oxford settlement ca. 1729.
2 CONT # '''The Bridge:''' Samuel Davis (b. 1704, M1) joined the Oxford/Dudley  frontier shortly after.
2 CONT # '''The Link:''' Ebenezer Davis (b. 1717, M2), the posthumous son of the second marriage, removed to Oxford ca. 1739 to join his half-brothers.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Property and Estate Note ==
2 CONT '''Residence:''' Roxbury/Brookline border, Suffolk County.
2 CONT '''Occupation:''' Cordwainer (Shoemaker).
2 CONT
2 CONT === Land Records ===
2 CONT * '''1710 Acquisition:''' Joseph Davis purchased 15 acres of land, including a house and orchard, from his father, Joseph Sr., for £60 (Suffolk Deeds, Vol. 25, p. 88). 
2 CONT * '''1717 Inventory:''' Following his death, the estate was appraised at approximately £350, including "shomaker's tooles" and "leather in the tan-vats."
2 CONT
2 CONT === Historical Continuity ===
2 CONT This property served as the family anchor following the 1717 mortality event. The widow, Elizabeth Lane, maintained the homestead until the children reached legal age. The eventual sale of this high-value Roxbury acreage funded th
2 CONC e "Chain Migration" of the Davis brothers (Joseph III, Samuel, and Ebenezer) to Oxford, Worcester County.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Identity Audit ==
2 CONT The 1710 deed from Joseph Sr. to Joseph Jr. is the primary evidence linking the three generations of Josephs. It confirms that the Joseph who died in 1717 was the son of the Joseph who also died in 1717, clarifying the "Triple Dea
2 CONC th" record in the Roxbury VR.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Geographic and Administrative References ==
2 CONT * '''Incorporation of Brookline''': ''Acts and Resolves of the Province of Massachusetts Bay'', Vol. 8 (1705). Documents the transition of "Muddy River" (Boston) to the independent Town of Brookline.
2 CONT * '''Boundary Records''': ''Muddy River and Brookline Records, 1634–1838'' (1875). Provides context for Davis land holdings on the Roxbury/Brookline border.
2 CONT * '''Suffolk Deeds (1710)''': Vol. 25, p. 88. Joseph Davis Sr. to Joseph Davis Jr. Defines the 15-acre homestead as "butting on the Brookline line," explaining the dual-town record entries.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT == Naming and Identity Audit ==
2 CONT '''Standardized Name:''' Joseph Davis, Jr.
2 CONT '''Occupational Title:''' Cordwainer of Roxbury.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Naming Convention Protocol ===
2 CONT * The middle name "Lane" has been dissolved from this profile as it is a non-historical insertion. 
2 CONT * The suffix "Jr." is maintained to distinguish the decedent from his father (d. 1717)  and his son (d. 1745).
2 CONT * Speculative maiden names for the first wife (Sarah) are moved to the "About"   section to maintain the integrity of the primary display field.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Manager Note:''' Do not split these maternal lines into separate unlinked families. The "Chain Migration" to Oxford serves as primary evidence that the children of Sarah [Curtiss] and Elizabeth Lane recognized one another as si
2 CONC blings and maintained legal and social   for over seventy years.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT == Sources ==
2 CONT * Roxbury Vital Records to the End of the Year 1849.
2 CONT * Brookline Vital Records to the End of the Year 1849.
2 CONT * Suffolk County Probate Records, Case No. 3918 (Estate of Joseph Davis).
2 CONT * Reference: [https://www.familysearch.org/tree/person/details/LRR7-BX2 FamilySearch Genealogy] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Jan 11 2019, 4:17:03 UTC''
2 CONT *Daniels, George F. (1892): History of Oxford, p. 465. ("Ebenezer, son of Joseph of Brookline, came to Oxford... following his brothers.")
2 CONT *Worcester County Deeds: Check for land transfers between Joseph III and Ebenezer ca. 1740–1745; these often contain the "love and affection" clauses that confirm brotherly relationships.
