2 CONT
2 CONT Robert may have married an unidentified woman about 1585. The Great Baddow parish register marriages are extant for the 1580s and that marriage isn't there. Robert married a woman named Margaret by the time he wrote his will in 16
2 CONC 23. He mentions her goods she brought to the marriage being separate from his, inferring that she had been previously married and had a dower from that marriage. In her will, she refers to her Pease children and grandchildren, so 
2 CONC it's very likely she was their biological mother. Margaret Rodam married a man named Robert Pease in Chelmsford, Essex, England, on 8 October 1598.[1] Chelmsford parish is next to Great Baddow parish and are about two miles apart.
2 CONC  Robert Charles Anderson referred to her as Margaret _____ in the Great Migration series in 1995.[2][3] He evidently didn't know of the Chelmsford record or else he would have mentioned it. The record wasn't found published anywhe
2 CONC re to date. Children of Robert of Great Baddow begin to appear in baptism records there in 1587. There is a gap in the children's baptisms between 1593 and 1600. This allows for the death of Robert's first wife and his remarriage 
2 CONC to Margaret. No record of a marriage was found that might be her first marriage or a likely burial of a man in that region named Rodam or it's variants.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Robert Pease and probably an unidentified first wife (Much Baddow records):
2 CONT
2 CONT daughter, bap. 10 Dec. 1587 (page is damaged, name missing)[4]
2 CONT son, bap. 28 Sep. 1589 (page is damaged, name missing)[5]
2 CONT John, bap. 24 May 1593[6] bur. 10 Jan. 1599/1600[7]
2 CONT Children of Robert Pease and perhaps Margaret (perhaps Rodam) :
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary, bap. 10 Jan. 1599/1600[8]
2 CONT Elizabeth, bap. 12 Sep. 1602[9] She may have been the Elizabeth Pease who had banns read in Much Baddow to marry Jacob Page in 1654[10]
2 CONT Mary, bap. 22 Sep. 1605[11] Abraham Page is named as a son-in-law in Robert Pease's will, as well as a daughter Mary. She was surely the "Marie" Pease who m. Abraham Page in Great Burstead, Essex, on 12 Nov. 1622[12] Abraham Page 
2 CONC was bap. in Much Baddow in 1623, the son of Abraham and Mary.[13] Mary was buried in Much Baddow on 29 September 1628, referred to as the wife of Abraham Page.[14]
2 CONT Robert, b. abt. 1607?, perhaps 1597. He is said to have been 27 when he was enrolled on a ship's passenger list on 30 April 1634. This is likely either a mistake by the person taking the information or a mistranscription. His brot
2 CONC her John was also a passenger with the correct age of 27 (he would have turned 28 in November 1634). The Great Baddow parish register is complete between 1605 and 1608 and Robert isn't there. The register continues uninterrupted a
2 CONC fter this, but John's is the last of Robert's children's baptisms recorded there. Given that Robert married by 1630, he probably wasn't born much after 1608. He may have been the oldest child. There no obvious marriage record foun
2 CONC d for Robert (see Research Notes), so that marriage and Robert Jr.'s baptism may have been in another parish for which the register isn't extant for this period.
2 CONT daughter, recorded as not being bap., bur. 24 July 1608[15] There are other burials around the same time of such children, but it isn't evident from the register why these children weren't baptized unless they were still born, but
2 CONC  that conflicts with John's baptism below.
2 CONT John, bap. 20 Nov. 1608[16]
2 CONT Death and Estate
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Pease was buried in Much Baddow on 16 May 1623.[17][18] His will was written on 10 May 1623[19] and proved on 12 June 1623.[20] [21]
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Pease of Much Baddowe, Co. of Essex, locksmith, being sick in body.
2 CONT His wife Margaret was to have all his "customarye lands & tenents" in Much Baddowe, the profits of which were to pay his debts and the will's legacies. The lease he had with his son-in-law Abraham Page was to remain in place.
2 CONT Should his wife remarry, the lands were to go to his son Robert
2 CONT To his son John, his eighteen acres of "customarye tenents & lands" called "Nayers" and "Mayes" that he bought from William Courtman and occupied by the said Abraham Page after the death of his wife
2 CONT To his daughter Elizabeth forty pounds to be paid by his executrix from the profits or sale of land he bought from Robert Bond and occupied by Henry Drane, the same land to be sold to pay debts and legacies only if no other means 
2 CONC is possible
2 CONT "all the tools, instruments and impliments of his trade
2 CONT To the poor of Much Baddow thirty shillings to be distrubuted at his burial
2 CONT To his wife, her choice of goods in their house no otherwise bequeathed
2 CONT He made arrangements in case Robert or John died before they reach 20 years or had children for legacies to be paid to his daughters Elizabeth and Mary.
2 CONT Wife Margaret to be executrix
2 CONT Whatever remains of his goods after his wife died were to be divided equally among his children except what his wife brought to the marriage
2 CONT Overseers were to be his cousin Thomas Stamer and his brother-in-law Francis King
2 CONT Witnesses Robert (Podare?), William Archebold, Richard Sweet[er?]
2 CONT Signed by Robert Pease (his name, not his mark)
2 CONT Francis King has been thought by some to have married Robert's wife Margaret without considering that he may have married Robert's sister. The latter appears to be the case. He was undoubtedly the Francis King who married "Eedy Pe
2 CONC se" in Sandon, Essex, England, on 13 March 1615/6.[22][23] Sandon parish is next to Great Baddow. Edy, Ede, etc., was a common form of Edith. This suggests she was one of the last children born to her and Robert's parents.
2 CONT
