1 NOTE {geni:about_me} .
2 CONT Margaret (Unknown) Pease migrated to New England during the Puritan Great Migration (1621-1640).
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret is seen as Margaret King, daughter of https://www.geni.com/people/Francis-King/6000000074071752018?through=6000000007680189221, without supporting evidence.  Also seen as daughter of https://www.geni.com/people/Nicholas-H
2 CONC ardman/6000000084767963455?through=6000000084769074970 & https://www.geni.com/people/Sara-Hardman/6000000084771427083?through=6000000084769074970, again, no supporting evidence that she was the wife of Robert Pease.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret (Unknown) Pease* (abt. 1580 - bef. 1645) Margaret was married to Frances Weston; presumably a second marriage for both. Prior to her marriage to Frances, she is thought by some to have been married to a gentleman by the s
2 CONC urname of Reeves*, who would have been the father of Margaret's daughter, Lucy. In 1630, she, Frances, and her daughter, Lucy emigrated to the America with the Winthrop fleet. Frances Weston was an early leader of the colony, a de
2 CONC puty of the court, and a land owner, but soon the Weston's, who were followers of Samuel Gorton, found themselves in conflict with religious authorities. …
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Margaret (Unknown) Pease (abt. 1580 - bef. 1645)
2 CONT * Born about 1580 [uncertain] in England 
2 CONT * Died before 1 Jan 1645 before about age 65 in Salem, Essex, Massachusetts Bay Colony  [uncertain]]
2 CONT * Daughter of [father unknown] and [mother unknown]
2 CONT * Wife of Robert Pease — married about 1598 in Essex, England  [uncertain] at age 18
2 CONT * Wife of Francis Weston — married [date unknown] [location unknown
2 CONT * Mother of Robert Pease and John Pease
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Unknown-248859
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret may have been born about 1580 if she was the mother of the children of Robert Pease's children, the first known having been baptized in 1600. She refers to her Pease children and grandchildren in her will (see below). Ass
2 CONC uming this is the case, and assuming they didn't have earlier children baptized outside of Much Baddow (now known as Great Baddow), she married Robert about 1598-9.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although her sons Robert and John sailed on the ship Francis from Ipswich, England, to Boston, Massachusetts Bay Colony, in 1637, she wasn't with them.[1] Robert is listed alone and John is listed on two different lists, one (the 
2 CONC same one as Robert) alone and another that accounts for accompanying children. As an adult, Margaret should have had her own listing if she was on the ship. An alternative is that she married Francis Weston (see below) before she 
2 CONC emigrated and they came on a ship for which there is no extant passenger list. Anderson estimates his immigration year at 1632 based on the first records of him in Salem in 1633. The claim that Weston came in 1630 in the Winthop F
2 CONC leet isn't supported by credible evidence.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriages and Children
2 CONT
2 CONT She may have been the Margaret Rodam who married Robert Pease in Chelmsford on 8 October 1598.[2][3] Chelmsford parish is next to Great Baddow parish and are about two miles apart. Robert Charles Anderson referred to her as Margar
2 CONC et _____ in the Great Migration series in 1995.[4][5] He evidently didn't know of the Chelmsford record or else he would have mentioned it. The record wasn't found published anywhere to date.
2 CONT
2 CONT Evidence is clear that Margaret married Francis Weston. At the 3 November 1635 session of the Massachusetts Bay Colony Court of Assistants it was "Ordered that John Pease shalbe whipt & bound to his good behavr for strikeing his m
2 CONC other Mrs. Weston, & derydeing of her & for dyvers other misdemeanrs & other evill carriages."[6] Francis and Margaret were charged with defamation at the 3 October 1637 session of the Salem Quarterly Court.[7] At the same session
2 CONC  she was charged because she "challenged three of the jurymen of Salem." Also, "Francis Westons wife was censured to bee set 2 houres in the bilboes hear & 2 houres at Salem vpon a lecture day."[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT Francis and Margaret moved to Rhode Island Colony, where he died about 1644-5. Margaret moved back to Salem and evidently reverted back to the surname Pease, perhaps to lessen the stigma of being among the Nonconformists who settl
2 CONC ed Rhode Island Colony. (more about their association with the Nonconformists should be added, also the same for John and his wife Lucy Pease)
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret died probably in Salem, Essex, Massachusetts Bay Colony, between 1 September 1644, when she wrote her will, and 1 January 1644/5, when it was probated .[9] (more about her will should be added)
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Robert and probably Margaret (perhaps Rodam) Pease (Much Baddow records):
2 CONT
2 CONT #Mary, bap. 10 Jan. 1599/1600[10]
2 CONT #Elizabeth, bap. 12 Sep. 1602.[11] She may have been the Elizabeth Pease who had banns read in Much Baddow to marry Jacob Page in 1654[12]
2 CONT #Mary[13] Abraham Page is named as a son-in-law in Robert Pease's will, as well as a daughter Mary. She was surely the "Marie" Pease who m. Abraham Page in Great Burstead, Essex, on 12 Nov. 1622[14] Abraham Page was bap. in Much B
2 CONC addow in 1623, the son of Abraham and Mary.[15] Abraham Page was bap. in Much Baddow in 1623, the son of Abraham and Mary.[16] Mary was buried in Much Baddow on 29 September 1628, referred to as the wife of Abraham Page.[17]
2 CONT #Robert, b. abt. 1607?, said to have been 27 when he was enrolled on a ship's passenger list on 30 April 1634. This is likely either a mistake by the person taking the information or a mistranscription. His brother John was also a
2 CONC  passenger with the correct age of 27 (he would have turned 28 in November 1634). The Great Baddow parish register is complete between 1605 and 1608 and Robert isn't there. The register continues uninterrupted after this, but John
2 CONC 's is the last of Robert's children's baptisms recorded there. Given that Robert married by 1630, he probably wasn't born much after 1608. He may have been the oldest child. There no obvious marriage record found for Robert (see R
2 CONC esearch Notes), so that marriage and Robert Jr.'s baptism may have been in another parish for which the register isn't extant for this period.
2 CONT #daughter, recorded as not being bap., bur. 24 July 1608.[18] There are other burials around the same time of such children, but it isn't evident from the register why these children weren't baptized unless they were still born, b
2 CONC ut that conflicts with John's baptism below.
2 CONT #John, bap. 20 Nov. 1608[19]
2 CONT
2 CONT Research Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT Francis King is referred to as Robert Pease's brother-in-law in his will. For this reason, some researchers have assumed he was the brother of Robert's wife without considering that he married Robert's sister. Francis was undoubte
2 CONC dly the man who married "Eedy Pese" in Sandon, Essex, England, on 13 March 1615/6.[20][21] Sandon parish is next to Great Baddow.
2 CONT
2 CONT These Great Migration sources can be added to "Sources" below if they aren't redundant.[22] [23] and John Pease[24]; likewise, her second husband, Francis Weston.[25]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Named in her husband Robert Pease’s will, proved 12 June 1623, two months after he died.
2 CONT
2 CONT August 1636, Great Baddow: Witness to a bond given by William Vincent to be paid to Abraham Page (orphaned grandson by daugher Elizabeth) when he comes of age.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1639, Salem: admitted as a member of the church in Salem. She evidently travelled to Massachusetts in 1639, probably accompanied by her son John who returned to Essex to get her.
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret (_____) Pease presumably came to New England with one of her sons John & Robert Pease. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret Pease, widow and mother of Robert and John, also emigrated to America, and died Sep 01, 1644 in Salem. In her will dated Sep 01, 1642, proved Jan 01, 1645, she mentions a grandchild, John, son of Robert.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==notes==
2 CONT
2 CONT - No proof of parentage.
2 CONT - death date also seen as Sept 1644, which contradicts a 2nd marriage in 1651.
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Great Migration Project as posted by Linda Mac on FindAGrave:
2 CONT
2 CONT ''(comment: this bio seems to be a mixup with John Pease's "mother in law" '''Margaret''', widow of Francis Weston & mother of his wife)''
2 CONT
2 CONT There are two competing versions of her later history, each of which has its problems. We prefer the first of these two stories. On 3 November 1635 it was ordered "that John Pease shall be whipped, & bound to his good behavior, fo
2 CONC r striking his mother, Mrs. Weston, & deriding of her, & for diverse other misdemeanors, & other evil carriages." On 1 September 1644 "Margit Pease" of Salem made a will in which the principal beneficiaries were the children of he
2 CONC r son Robert Pease; this will was proved on 1 January 1644/5. For this to be the widow of Francis Weston, we would have to assume that he had died before 1 September 1644 (and there is nothing to contradict this), and that after h
2 CONC is death she had returned to Salem and reassumed the name of an earlier husband. Richard Harcourt, who later held some of the land of Francis Weston, noted that Margaret had held a life interest in these lands, so there should not
2 CONC  have been a competing claim from her Pease heirs. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The second story is that she married (2) by early 1651 [http://www.geni.com/people/Adam-Goodwin/6000000021138033640?through=6000000007680189221 Adam Goodwin,] was supported by the town because of her distracted condition, and died
2 CONC  on 2 May 1651. This later history for Margaret is dependent on a letter of Roger Williams in which in early 1651 he mistakenly called her "Mrs. Weston" when she was already married to Adam Goodwin. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Links
2 CONT * http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=65262161
2 CONT * http://homepages.rootsweb.ancestry.com/~bakhelvy/html/fam00135.htm
2 CONT * “Married Robet King 1586 in Great Badow, Essex, England” ???
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://minerdescent.com/2010/08/25/robert-pease-sr-great-baddow/
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret King was born in 1573-1574 in Great Baddow, Essex, England.  Her parents were  Francis KING and [__?__].
2 CONT The widow Margaret continued to live in Great Baddow for ten years.  She immigrated to America in the years just prior to her death.  Margaret died on 1 Sep 1644 in Salem, Essex, MA at age 70.
2 CONT
