1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Inventor of New England clam chowder
2 CONT
2 CONT A “Red Coat and Clam Chowder,” The Vineyard Gazette (1927)
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://iconicnorwich.org/founders-6/
2 CONT
2 CONT FAMILY
2 CONT
2 CONT John Pease Sr. married Lucy Reeves Pease (1611-1643)  in 1635. Their two children were :
2 CONT
2 CONT 1) James Pease (1637-1719)
2 CONT 2) John Pease Jr. (1639-1711) : He inherited his father’s Norwich home lot and lived in Norwich until his death in 1711.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Pease Sr. married Mary Creber Pease (1625-1695) in 1648. They had eight children. Their names were : 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1) Mary Pease (1649-1674)
2 CONT 2) David Pease (1651-1689) : Married but wife’s name is unknown.
2 CONT 3) Abigail Pease (1653-1674) : Had a daughter named Bathsheba Sliffe
2 CONT 4) Capt. Samuel Pease (1655-1689) Husband of Mary Whittemore
2 CONT 5) Sgt. Thomas Pease (1657-1748) : Husband of Bathsheba Merry
2 CONT 6) Rebecca Pease (1659-1674)
2 CONT 7) Sarah Pease Daggett Capon (1661-1735) : Wife of Deacon John Daggett and later Banfield Capon
2 CONT 8) Jonathan Pease (1670-1736) : Husband of Sarah Jones and Mary Newcomb
2 CONT
2 CONT John Pease Sr. died in 1677 and his final resting place is unknown. His first wife, Lucy, died in October 1643 in Salem Massachusetts. His second wife, Mary, died after 1695. Her burial site is unknown.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT John Pease Sr. (1608-1677)
2 CONT
2 CONT John Pease, one of the original 35 proprietors of Norwich, is credited with being the first to add potatoes to clam chowder, thus creating the first New England Clam Chowder recipe.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The primary difference between the traditional clam chowder of Europe and of early New England clam chowder is the substitution of the biscuit (a.k.a. cracker or bread) with potatoes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Before settling in Norwich John Pease lived in Edgartown on Martha’s Vineyard. While there he learned of the healthy benefits of eating clams, especially when they were made into chowder. At that point-in-time all chowder recipes 
2 CONC included bread, which is a grain based food. During one of the growing seasons flocks of wild pigeons darkened the skies and ruined the grain crops on the Vineyard. Since it was not possible to add bread to clam chowder, Pease, wh
2 CONC o had learned about the taste and benefits of potatoes in Virginia, came up with the idea of adding potatoes in place of the bread.
2 CONT
2 CONT Trading vessels regularly traveled along the coast from Virginia to Cape Cod and whenever they reached the Cape would stand over to the Vineyard to exchange news with the Edgartown colonists. Potatoes were carried as a part of the
2 CONC  stores on these vessels and it was from one of these ships that John Pease obtained his first supply.
2 CONT
2 CONT One pot of chowder made with potatoes was enough to convince the family that bread or biscuits were inferior as a “thickening” for the dish. Pease obtained more potatoes, and besides introducing them as a staple article of food on
2 CONC  the Island, it is said that he was the first to plant them in Edgartown.
2 CONT ----
