2 CONT  The Puritan Great Migration.
2 CONT John Pease migrated to New England during the Puritan Great Migration (1621-1640). (See The Great Migration (Series 2), by R. C. Anderson, vol. 5, p. 414)
2 CONT Join: Puritan Great Migration Project
2 CONT Discuss: PGM
2 CONT Contents
2 CONT
2 CONT [HIDE]
2 CONT 1 Biography
2 CONT 2 Research Notes
2 CONT 3 Sources
2 CONT 4 Acknowledgments
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT John Pease was baptized 20 November 1608 at St. Mary the Virgin church, Great Baddow, Essex, England[1][2] the son of Robert Pease.
2 CONT
2 CONT John, age 27, and his brother Robert, also aged 27, are recorded on 31 April 1637 as passengers on the ship Francis, bound from London to New England.[3] John is on an additional list that accounts for accompanying children. They 
2 CONC were Faythe Clearke, age 15, Robert Pease, age 3, and Darcas Greene, age 15. It has been proposed that Robert was his nephew, son of Robert, assuming he was younger than 7, although he has a baptism in Great Baddow in 1630. Also, 
2 CONC it would be odd that he would be listed as accompanying John and not Robert, and Robert's son John isn't listed even though he also immigrated.
2 CONT
2 CONT Great Baddow in Essex County was about 30 miles NE of London on the road to Ipswich, making it the most convenient place for him to embark on a trip to America.
2 CONT
2 CONT Their reasons for leaving England are uncertain, but this quote from "Calendars of State Papers, American and Colonial Series, 1574-1660," says: "On 4 February 1634 Henry Dade wrote from Ipswich, England, to the Archbishop of Cant
2 CONC erbury that the ships FRANCIS and ELIZABETH with 60 men in each intend to sail for New England on about 10 March and he supposes they are debtors or persons disaffected with the established church. About 600 such men will go over 
2 CONC shortly and he questions the effects of allowing such swarms to go. Mr. Ward of Ipswich has preached against the Book of Common Prayer thus causing this giddiness and desire to go to New England."[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT The charismatic Puritan minister, Thomas Hooker, had regularly preached in Essex County, including Great Baddow, in the 1620s and early 1630s. "It seems that he was actively preaching in Essex sometime before the end of 1626, and 
2 CONC entered into his lectureship [at St. Mary's in Chelmsford] toward the end of that year, for his daughter Anne was baptized at Great Baddow, a village near Chelmsford, on January 5, 1627. He moved his family to Chelmsford sometime 
2 CONC in the following year..."[5] Chelmsford and and Great Baddow, then called Much Baddow, are neighboring parishes, being about two miles from church to church.
2 CONT
2 CONT New England Puritan preacher Cotton Mather wrote of his time at Chelmsford: "Hereby there was a great reformation wrought, not only in the town [Chelmsford,] but in the adjacent country, from all parts whereof they came to 'hear t
2 CONC he wisdom of the Lord Jesus Christ,' in his gospel, by this worthy man dispensed; and some of great quality among the rest, would often resort from far to his assembly...." [6]
2 CONT
2 CONT Later, when Bishop Laud removed him from his lectureship at St. Mary's in Chelmsford for not conforming to church teachings, Hooker moved to the nearby village of Little Baddow (just a few miles from Great Baddow) and set up a sch
2 CONC ool there for children and another for ministers of the area who wanted to learn more from him of the Puritan way of the church. Therefore, the Pease family surely knew of Thomas Hooker and maybe were his followers. The Pease brot
2 CONC hers came to Boston just a few months after Hooker. A large number of people from around Chelmsford left their home in England and came to New England.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Peases were in Salem, Massachusetts Bay Colony, by 1637.
2 CONT
2 CONT John married about 1635 (probably in Salem) to Lucy
2 CONT
2 CONT John was involved in the maritime business of trading, from which he earned the title 'captain', and was often gone from home. (note: citation needed) He may have returned to England in 1639 to bring his mother Margaret and his ne
2 CONC phew Abraham Page back to Salem. (note: Abraham Page was his brother-in-law and didn't emigrate from Essex)
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of John and Lucy:
2 CONT
2 CONT James Pease was born March 15, 1637/8 in Salem, Mass. He married (1st) Elizabeth Norton (1664, daughter of Nicholas Norton). He married (2nd) Abigail (Travis) Covell on April 22, 1706.
2 CONT John Pease was born about 1640. From his father's will he received land at New London and lived there in 1672. He probably was unmarried. He died about 1710 in Norwich, New London County, CT
2 CONT He was a land-owner, both at Salem and at Edgartown with the occupation as that of a husbandman/farmer. At Edgartown he held the office of constable on November 7, 1653.
2 CONT
2 CONT June 18, 1644 he sold 1 house and 75 acres and he left Salem and moved with his wife and 2 sons James and John to Edgartown, on Martha's Vineyard Island (then a Dutch colony).
2 CONT
2 CONT At the 3 November 1635 session of the Massachusetts Bay Colony Court of Assistants it was "Ordered that John Pease shalbe whipt & bound to his good behavr for strikeing his mother Mrs. Weston, & derydeing of her & for dyvers other
2 CONC  misdemeanrs & other evill carriages."[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT From the "History of Martha's Vineyard", chapter: "Annals of Edgartown":
2 CONT
2 CONT "...Francis Weston was an early settler at Salem, originally a friend and supporter of Roger Williams, whom he followed in exile to Rhode Island. He was unfortunate, however, in his second marriage, as Mrs. Margaret Weston was aff
2 CONC licted with one of the religious whimsies of the period, and incurred the opposition of the authorities, not then famed for charity and tolerance, and she was made to sit in the bilboes for her schismatic 'notions.' The particular
2 CONC  doctrines she imbibed were those promulgated by Samuel Gorton, for which he and others suffered persecution and were driven from Salem to seek an asylum in Rhode Island.
2 CONT "...In a few years Weston himself became a disciple of Gorton, and his step-daughter, Lucy Pease [wife of John], likewise joined the sect, all of which was doubtless to the disadvantage of John Pease, socially and commercially, in
2 CONC  orthodox Salem. In addition to this Mrs. Weston was undoubtedly unbalanced mentally, and later became of hopelessly unsound mind. We are thus enabled to see the environment of John Pease, and considering the stress of the times a
2 CONC nd the religious intolerance of the period may not harshly judge his unlawful chastisement of his mother-in-law. Doubtless she deserved forcible repression, and invited it by her actions. "Weston was banished in March, 1638, from 
2 CONC the jurisdiction of Massachusetts for promulgating the tabooed Gortonian doctrines, and took up his residence at Shawomet, Rhode Island, whence he continued to spread them by whatever means he could employ."[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT When John Pease traveled to warn his father-in-law of his impending arrest, he probably took his ship around Massachusetts and Cape Cod and up Narragansett Bay to Shawomet since the trip overland would have been a difficult 70 mil
2 CONC es: south to Boston, SW to Providence, and south to Shawomet/Warwick. It is not probable that John Pease ever met Roger Williams because Williams had already left Salem when John arrived, and by 1640 there were 16,000 people in Ma
2 CONC ssachusetts. Also in 1644 Roger Williams was in England trying to secure a charter for Rhode Island to free them of the dangers of Massachusetts authority. That same year the Massachusetts General Court passed a law that anyone ho
2 CONC lding to Baptist doctrines should be banished.
2 CONT
2 CONT John's mother and brother Robert died in 1644, which along with his wife's troubles with the religious authories, surely contributed to his timing in leaving Salem and moving to Martha's Vineyard Island. On June 18, 1644, John Pea
2 CONC se sold to Richard Ingersoll of Salem "one house and 75 acres of land adjoyning to the fearme wheron the said Richard dwelleth." This was a fortunate move for the family since they escaped the Salem witch trials of the 1690's; how
2 CONC ever one Sarah Pease (daughter-in-law of his brother, Robert) was prosecuted for witchcraft at Salem in 1692 and imprisoned, but not executed.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1643 the settlements in Connecticut and Massachusetts joined to form the New England Confederation. The name of the union was "The United Colonies of New England" and though it lost much of its power in a dispute in 1653 over t
2 CONC he amount of power Massachusetts would have, it continued on until 1684 when Charles II revoked its charter. By 1643 there were 14,000 to 16,000 people in Massachusetts, probably as much as all the rest of British America put toge
2 CONC ther.
2 CONT
2 CONT There is an interesting account of the family's move to Martha's Vineyard which was printed in the Nantucket Gazette and the Albany Journal in 1838. This account has no historical proof, and the dates are surely wrong, but being a
2 CONC  family story, it may have some truth to it: "In the fall of 1632, or a year or two later, a vessel bound from England to South Virginia, fell in with the south shoal of Nantucket, came up through the Vineyard sound and anchored o
2 CONC ff Cape Poge on account of a distemper which, like a plague, raged among the passengers and crew, twenty-five of whom died. "
2 CONT
2 CONT Or according to another account, scarcity of provisions was the cause. Four men with their families, requested to be put on shore, preferring to take their chance with the natives, than to pursue the voyage under such distressing 
2 CONC circumstances. "They landed at the spot since called Pease's Point or Edgartown. Their names were John Pease, Thomas Vincent, ___ Trapp and ___ Browning or Norton. A red coat, presented on landing, by Pease to the Chief or Sachem,
2 CONC  secured at once the good offices of the tribe; and they were treated with hospitality. In order to shelter themselves from the approaching winter, Pease and his company made excavations in the side of a hill near the water, whenc
2 CONC e they could command a full view of the harbor and adjacent bay...."
2 CONT
2 CONT Local tradition says there were families living there by the name of Pease, Vincent, Trapp, and Stone before 1640. The story goes that these settlers were on their way to join the Jamestown colony , but were driven into Edgartown 
2 CONC harbor for shelter. They remained here after spending their first winter in a dugout at "Green Hollow," near what is now known as Tower Hill.
2 CONT
2 CONT On Martha's Vineyard "John Pease lived at first at Mattakeset and later, when the home lots were distributed, he drew the first in present-day Edgartown situated at the north end of the town of Great Harbor. John owned a house lot
2 CONC  of ten acres of upland and two acres of meadow at the north end at a place now known as Pease's Point, and a street to the south of this land is Pease Point Way.
2 CONT
2 CONT On Mar 23, 1647 John Pease sold 'a parcell of land about 10 acres & two acres of meadow' at Mattakeeset to Mr. John Bland and moved to Norwich, CT. Here he purchased land that he retained until his death, bequeathing it to John Pe
2 CONC ase Jr.: "all that was given to me at Mohegin, with that frame of a house." [from his will]
2 CONT
2 CONT John's wife Lucy died about 1648 (or maybe as early as 1646)--about the time he moved to Connecticut: "... About 1645-50 the new settlements at Saybrook, New London, & New Haven [Connecticut] were being founded and were attracting
2 CONC  hundreds from the old towns in Massachusetts and probably John Pease was prospecting and trying his fortune on the mainland...."
2 CONT
2 CONT It was probably on Martha's Vineyard that he married his second wife, Mary _____ [maybe Browning, about 1648. If so, Mary was much younger than John, probably born about 1630. She may have been the daughter of Malachi Browning (b 
2 CONC abt 1600 in Essex County, England; d Nov 27, 1653 at the home of his friend, Robert Scott in Boston, MA) & Mary (Collier) Browning (b bfr 1604 in Ewell, Surrey, England). They were married 22 May 1628 in London, England. The timin
2 CONC g of their emigration is uncertain.]
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of John and Mary
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary, b Oct 15, 1649; d May 29, 1737, Enfield, CT, age 87
2 CONT David, b 1651, Martha's Vineyard; m Elizabeth?; d Oct 12, 1689, age 38
2 CONT Abigail, b 1653; d before 1711
2 CONT Capt. Samuel, b abt 1655; m Mary ?; was a ship's captain; d Oct 12, 1689, age 34
2 CONT Thomas, b 1657.; m abt 1684 Bathsheba Merry, daughter of Joseph Merry. Thomas was a schoolmaster. Lived in Edgartown.
2 CONT Rebecca, b abt 1659
2 CONT Sarah, b 1661; m John Doggett abt 1686.
2 CONT Jonathan, b abt 1667; m Mary Newcomb, June 13, 1728, Edgartown, MA; moved to Windham, CT; d abt 1727.
2 CONT In 1650 John was still in New London, Ct. but before March 5, 1653 he had moved back to the Island, when he was involved in a land suit. He was elected Constable Nov. 7, 1653, the only office he is known to have held in the town. 
2 CONC His nephew, Robert Pease, moved to Edgartown by 1656 to be the town weaver, but had moved away by 1660. In 1673 he and 19 others of the leading residents of the Vineyard signed the celebrated appeal to Massachusetts for annexation
2 CONC , and joined in the 'Dutch Rebellion' of that and the following year. He was one of the first to be attacked in reprisal, 5 days after the petition was dated:
2 CONT
2 CONT "John Peas being by the Govournour by his officer warned to appear to answer his misdemeanour for committing a riott at Edgartown the Marshall returneth answer that the warrant was by the said Peas his wife taken him and therefore
2 CONC  he cannot return his warrant; the said Peas appearing before the Govournour is both person and estate bound to answer at the next sessions of triall held upon the Duke his highness province and Territories for the said riott comm
2 CONC itted and his wife for forcibly taking the warrant of the marshalls hands." [#2] This incident probably had something to do with the second Dutch War of 1672. (The first Dutch War was in 1664 when the English took over New Netherl
2 CONC and and it became New York.)
2 CONT
2 CONT After this trouble with the governor, John may have left Martha's Vineyard for another place, maybe Portsmouth, NH. It's not know for sure when or where he died. He died sometime between Sept. 1677 and June 3, 1689. He was on a ju
2 CONC ry in Sept 1677 in Martha’s Vineyard, and his wife had remarried by 1689, so sometime between those 2 dates he died.[9][10][11][12][13]
2 CONT Find A Grave: Memorial #37316691
2 CONT
2 CONT (By June 3, 1689 his widow had remarried to ? Creber [probably Captain Thomas Creber of Portsmouth, N.H., master of the ketch "John & Mary," engaged in coastwise trading. The name of this boat seems to imply that it had belonged t
2 CONC o and was named by John Pease]. Mary outlived this husband also and was a widow again in 1695.
2 CONT
2 CONT His will, dated March 4, 1674/5 bequeaths inheritance to his two sons James and John by his first wife, his 2nd wife Mary and children Thomas, Jonathan, Samuell, David, Abigail, Mary, Rebecca and Sarah
2 CONT
2 CONT Will of John Pease[14]
2 CONT
2 CONT March the 4th, 1674. The last will and testament of me, John Peas,
2 CONT husbandman and inhabitant uppon Martins Vineyard in the Town called
2 CONT Edgartown, I John Peas having upon good consideration and being now
2 CONT in some measure in good health and perfect understanding and memory
2 CONT though I am striken in years and Crasy in respect of what formerly, I having
2 CONT had two wives one formerly deceased and by her have yet two sons surv-
2 CONT iving, James Peas, & John Peas and these two sonnes James the Elder,
2 CONT God hath been pleased to bless him in his labours & indeavours and I
2 CONT have been helpfull to him so that he is verry well to pass in his Estate farre
2 CONT beyond myself: I do therefore in this my last will and testament give
2 CONT to my eldest Son James Peas twelve pence; My second son John Peas
2 CONT I have alreddy given unto and do hereby give unto him all that was given
2 CONT unto me at Mohegin, with that frame of a house I set up uppon some part
2 CONT of that land; I say I give it unto my son John Peas & his heirs forever:
2 CONT I having by my now living wife Mary Peas, four sonnes & four Daughters,
2 CONT my Sonnes Thomas Peas Jonathan, Samuell, and David and my Daughters
2 CONT Abygaill Peas, Mary, Rebecca, and Sarah Peas unto these my four Sonnes
2 CONT and four Daughters I doe give all my landes and houseing that I have
2 CONT upon this Iland Martins Vineyard to be either equally devided or valued
2 CONT or sold or exchanged and the price thereof Equally devided to everyone
2 CONT of them alike and this to be performed at convenient age of them: or as
2 CONT my now living wife Mary shall see meet whome I make my full and whole
2 CONT Executrix to performe all this my last will and testament; and I give unto
2 CONT whatsoever I have more or less for her use and comfort and helpfulness
2 CONT in bringing up my children at her disposing for Ever.
2 CONT In witness hereunto my hand and seal.
2 CONT JOHN PEAS.
2 CONT Further I give and bequeath unto
2 CONT my second son John Peas twelve pence.
2 CONT Wee whose names are underwritten
2 CONT are witnesses to this will and testament.
2 CONT Thomas Birchard,
2 CONT Kathrin Birchard,
2 CONT Thomas Trappe,
2 CONT Research Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT The following information has been refuted
2 CONT
2 CONT Lucy (maybe Reeves, she was probably the daughter of Margaret Reeves? Weston and step-daughter of Francis Weston, who had all come over with Wintrop in 1630 and were in Salem, Mass. by 1635). Lucy's mother and step-father were ban
2 CONC ished from Mass. in 1638 for religious differences: they were followers of Anne Hutchison and Thomas Gorton. They took up residence in a settlement of Rhode Island, and John Pease helped them escape from Massachusetts authorities.
2 CONC  Another author, in his book published in 1911, said that John Pease, the Settler, had married Lucy Weston on Martha's Vineyard,[15] however, that information was found not be be accurate by Anderson, in his Great Migration serrie
2 CONC s.
2 CONT
