1 OCCU accused witch
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} During the 1692 witch trials, '''Sarah Pease''', and her of '''Robert Pease''', the weaver, were both accused of witchcraft.  Sarah's husband, Robert, emigrated from England in 1634 at the age of about 4 years old 
2 CONC with his father Robert and his uncle John Pease. Her family name and origin is unknown until the time of her marriage to Robert in 1658. Of her trial, the following is excerpted from the article: She was accused on Monday, 23 May 
2 CONC 1692 of  "sundry acts of Witchcraft committed on the bodys of Mary Warren, Abigaile Williams and Eliz Hubbard." She was accused along with Benjamin Procter and Mary Derich. A warrant for her arrest was issued and she was arrested 
2 CONC that day. (*Sarah and Robert were both imprisoned in 1692.) The following day was set aside for examinations and the proceedings were recorded by Nathaniel Cary of Charlestown. He and Mrs. Cary had come to observe and to face Mrs.
2 CONC  Cary's accuser, Abigail Williams. He writes of the prisoners, one of whom surely was Sarah Pease:  
2 CONT
2 CONT >The Prisoners were called in one by one, and as they came in were cried out of, etc. The prisoner was placed about 7 or 8 foot from the Justices, and the Accusers between the Justices and them;  the Prisoner was ordered to stand 
2 CONC right before the Justices, with an Officer appointed to hold each hand, least they should therewith afflict them, and the Prisoners Eyes must be constantly on the Justices; for if they look'd on the afflicted, they would either fa
2 CONC ll into their Fits, or cry out of being hurt by them;  after Examination of the Prisoners, who it was afflicted these Girls, etc., they were put upon saying the Lords Prayer, as a tryal of their guilt; after the afflicted seem'd t
2 CONC o be out of their Fits, they would look steadfastly on some one person, and frequently not speak; and then the Justices said they were struck dumb, and after a little time would speak again;  then the Justices said to the Accusers
2 CONC , "which of you will go and touch the Prisoner at the Bar?" then the most couragious would adventure, but before they made three steps would ordinarily fall down as in a Fit;    the Justices ordered that they should be taken up an
2 CONC d carried to the Prisoner, that she might touch them; and as soon as they were touched by the accused, the Justices would say, they are well, before I could discern any alteration..."
2 CONT
2 CONT Sufficient evidence must have been found against Sarah because she was sent to Salem jail on 25 May 1692. Although testimony was brought against her again on August 5th, Sarah Pease escaped the condemnation of the judges, who sent
2 CONC enced 15 people to the gallows in September. By the late fall of that year the tide of hysteria had abated, and sympathy was turning from the "victims" to the accused. Sarah survived the winter and was released in May of 1693, aft
2 CONC er suffering a year in jail. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Parentage==
2 CONT According to Elaine K. Pease, Sarah [Sedgwick?] was born about 1630, perhaps in Salem, Massachusetts. Her parents were probably not  Major General Robert Sedgwick and Joanna Blake because Robert’s daughter Sarah married Governor J
2 CONC ohn Leverettt about 1644 in Charlestown, Suffolk, Massachusetts. Some sources state her parents were Richard Goodale and Catherine Killim, but their Sarah married someone else. Sarah Pease was living in 1704. Her surname can also 
2 CONC be seen as Goodall.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Sources==
2 CONT *The Early History of the Pease Family History by Austin Spencer Pease   Samuel Bowles & Co. printers Springfield, MA 1869  pp. 16-17
2 CONT
2 CONT * Paul Boyer and Stephen Nissenbaum, Salem Possessed (New York, DaCapo, 1977), p 639, 655-5.
2 CONT
2 CONT * George L. Burr., ed., Narratives of the Witchcraft Cases, 1648-1706 (New York: C. Scribner's Sons, 1914; reprind ed., New York: Barnes and Noble, 1946), p. 350. 
2 CONT
2 CONT * Pease, Elaine K. "Goody Pease of Salem Town". Essex Genealogist, August 1994.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Sarah Pease was convicted of witchcraft in Salem, Massachusetts, and was jailed for approximately one year.
2 CONT
2 CONT  [https://salemwitchmuseum.com/locations/robert-and-sarah-pease-home-site-of/]
