1 NOTE {geni:about_me} WILLIAM BEMENT was born in Enfield, Connecticut but the spirit of emigration had long been rife in the little community, and sometime before 1732 the desire for a wider outlook had taken him to Windham, from which 
2 CONC he removed to Wethersfield, Connecticut before 1749 where he purchased some land. Later, he was of Great Barrington, Massachusetts and for many years was the innholder of the town, and the warden of the county prison.  He was the 
2 CONC innkeeper in the opening years of the Revolution. News of Lexington reached the inn on 30 Apr 1775, and at sunrise on the following day the southern regiment of the Berkshire Militia was on its way to Boston, completely equipped a
2 CONC s to arms and generally so as to uniform. Two of his sons marched with the Minute Men and were commissioned staff officers; William Bement, Jr., armorer, and Ebenezer Bement, adjutant. They served through the year 1775, with their
2 CONC  regiment in the fortifications at Roxbury, and subsequently in other commands. His remaining sons, Asa and Samuel, likewise saw service in the struggle for independence, while he at home substantially aided in advancing the commo
2 CONC n cause. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After the end of the Revolution in 1783 the Berkshire County area went through a period of unrest known as Shay's Rebellion. William continued at Great Barrington for some years following this period of unrest, held in much esteem
2 CONC  by his fellow town men for his unswerving loyalty to the principles of true liberality, law and order. His declining days were spent at Stockbridge, Massachusetts where his estate was administered upon by his son, Asa Bement, 16 
2 CONC Feb 1798. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Chronicles of the Bement Family in America; 1928, pp. 66-75 
2 CONT
