1 CONT ROBERT GOODALE
1 CONT
1 CONT ORIGIN: Unknown (but see COMMENTS below).
1 CONT MIGRATION: 1634 on the Elizabeth 30 April 1634, "Robert Goodall", aged 30, and "Kathern, his wife," aged 28, were enrolled at for passage to New England on the Elizabeth[Hotten 280];on 30 April 1634, "Mary Goodale", aged 4, "Abrah
1 CONC am Goodale", aged 2, and "Isaacke Goodale", aged "half a year," children of "Rob[er]t Goodale", were enrolled at as passengers for New England on the Elizabeth[Hotten 282].
1 CONT FIRST RESIDENCE: Salem.
1 CONT OCCUPATION:Planter[EQC 2:61].
1 CONT EDUCATION:Signed his deeds.
1 CONT ESTATE:In the 1636 division of land "Rob[er]t Goodell" received twenty acres (in the non-freeman's part of the list)[STR 1:23].In the 25 December 1637 division of marsh and meadow in , "Rob[er]t Goodell" received one acre, for a h
1 CONC ousehold of seven[STR 1:102].On 21 January 1638/9, "Robert Goodale" was granted "20 acres more to be added to the 20 already granted in all 40 acres to be laid out by the town"[STR 1:78].On 11 February 1638/9, "Robert Goodall desi
1 CONC reth an enlargement of land"[STR 1:83].
1 CONT On 31 August 1649, there was "granted to Frances Skerie a little [spot] of ground between the sea and his field which was formerly Goodel's"[STR 1:159].
1 CONT On 13 February 1651/2, "Robert Goodell having 40 acres of land granted long since by the town and he having bought land of several others that had the land granted to them viz: Joseph Grafton 30 acres, John Sanders 40 acres, Hener
1 CONC ie Herick 40 acres, William Bound 40 acres, Robert Pease & his brother 30 acres, Robert Cotta 30 acres, William Walcott 30 acres, Edmund Marshall 20 acres, Thomas Antrum 20 acres, Michall Shaflin 20 acres, Mr. Venor 40 acres, John
1 CONC  Barber thirty acres, Philemon Dickenson 20 acres, Mr. Goose 50 acres, in the whole 480, it is ordered that the said Robert Goodell shall enjoy the said 480 [acres] of land being part of the eleven hundred acres, he discharging th
1 CONC e town of the abovesaid grant and he is allowed to said 480 acres of upland 24 acres of meadow provided that the meadow laid out within his upland be a part of it"[STR 1:171].
1 CONT On 31 March 1652, "Rob[er]t Goodhall of , planter," sold to "David Carwithen three acres of salt marsh on Royall Side[ELR 1:12].On 4 April 1653, "Rob[er]t Goodell of , planter," sold to "Francis Skerry ... four acres of land in Sa
1 CONC lem Neck"[ELR 1:17].On 7 July 1656, "Robert Goodall of ..., husbandman," sold to "David Carwithen of the same place 3 acres of salt marsh on Riall Side"[ELR 1:34].
1 CONT On 26 October 1653, "John Barbour, late of , carpenter," sold to "Rob[er]t Goodhall of the same, husbandman, thirty acres of land in Salem[ELR 1:21].On 28 February 1653[/4], "Thomas Antrop of " sold to "Rob[er]t Goodhall forty acr
1 CONC es of land lying near the sad Rob[er]t's land within the bounds of [ELR 1:22].
1 CONT On 31 October 1653, "John Blackleich of , merchant," sold to "Rob[er]t Goodhall of fifty acres of land lying between Mr. Higgesson's and Mr. Alderman's lands"[ELR 1:20].On 15 March 1659/60, "Robert Goodell of , ... husbandman," so
1 CONC ld to "Giles Coree of Salem ... fifty acres of land, which was a fifty acre lot formerly given by the town of to E[d]ward Giles, & sold by him to Mr. Blackleech, of whom the said Rob[er]t Goodell lately bought the said land"[ELR 2
1 CONC :68, 26:172].
1 CONT On 20 December 1662, "Robert Goodell sometime of , planter," sold to "Mr. George Corwin of the same town, merchant, a piece of salt marsh containing by estimation two acres" in " aforesaid, at the upper end of a place called the G
1 CONC reat Cove..., having a spring of water in it, with a small parcel of meadow"[ELR 2:61].On 6 January 1662[/3?], "Robert Goodell of Salem ..., farmer," sold to "Tho[mas] Flint of the same place, farmer, ... a certain parcel of land 
1 CONC containing fifty acres"[ELR 2:76].On 14 August 1678, "Robert Goodale of ..., husbandman," sold to "Thomas Flint, carpenter, of the same town, three acres & fifty-eight pole of meadow & upland ... lying near the now dwelling house 
1 CONC of the said " [ELR 11:16]
1 CONT On 7 June 1697, "Nathaniel Felton Senior aged about eighty-two years & John Massey Senior aged about sixty-six years both of both testifieth & saith that the field commonly called the East Field in lying between the great pasture 
1 CONC belonging to Col. Gedney & the field belonging to John Cornwall now in the possession of John Higginson Junior was formerly two houselots of about two acres each lot & that there was erected and standing on each of said lots a dwe
1 CONC lling house which were inhabited by Robert Goodale & Peter Wolfe before the year of our Lord one-thousand six-hundred sixty & one"[ELR 11:253].
1 CONT On 7 June 1667, "Rob[er]t Goodell of ..., husbandman," sold to "Mr. Nicholas Manning of the same town twenty acres of upland with some meadow contained in it ... in a place called the North Field"[ELR 4:176].
1 CONT On 29 June 1672, "Robert Goodall of ... and Margaret his wife" sold to "John Buxton of the aforesaid four & forty acres of swamp & upland ... it is to be understood, the forty-four acres of land before mentioned is part of the six
1 CONC  hundred acres granted by the town of in small parcels to several men, many years ago, & purchased by the said Goodal"[ELR 3:177].On 29 December 1674, "Robert Goodell of ... & Margaret my wife" sold to "John Buxton of aforesaid on
1 CONC e & twenty acres of land ... near the great river commonly called Ipswich River"[ELR 4:132].
1 CONT On 21 July 1658, "Robert Goodell of " exchanged land with "John Smith my son-in-law," Smith receiving "ten acres of upland ... with an old house upon the same ... near the great cove in a neck of land commonly called the north nec
1 CONC k," and Goodale receiving "fifty acres of land ... adjoining to a farm of the said Robert"[ELR 1:39-40].
1 CONT On 20 September 1665, "Robert Goodell of " deeded to "my daughter Sara Bacheler the wife of John Bacheler of ... thirty-two acres of upland & two parcels of meadow about eight acres, situate & being in the limits of aforesaid, bei
1 CONC ng part of the eleven hundred acres granted by the town of to several proprietors on the south side of the river commonly called Ipswich River"[ELR 6:28].On 16 November 1666, "John Bacheler of " sold this same land to "Lott Killum
1 CONC  of the same place"; "Robert Goodell" acknowledged "the abovesaid upland & meadow to be a free gift unto John Bacheler with his wife Sarah my daughter at her marriage"[ELR 6:54].
1 CONT On 26 October 1665, "Robert Goodell of " deeded to "my son Zachariah Goodell of aforesaid sixty acres of land ... being part of the eleven hundred acres of land granted by the town of for small lots, lying near my house"; "it is t
1 CONC o be understood that 50 acres were given by the said Robert Goodell, as a legacy for his portion, & the other ten acres were sold to him for a valuable sum"[ELR 6:71].On 1 February 1667/8, "Robert Goodell of " deeded to "my son Za
1 CONC chariah Goodell of the said ten acres of fresh meadow ... near the river commonly called the great river"[ELR 6:71].
1 CONT On 10 February 1667/8, "Robert Goodell of " deeded to "my son Isaack Goodell of the place aforesaid one hundred acres of upland ... near the river commonly called the Great River ... & seven acres of fresh meadow lying almost in a
1 CONC  triangle"[ELR 4:207].
1 CONT On 3 November 1668, "Robert Goodelle of , farmer," sold to "his daughter Hanah Killum of 35 acres of land in , 10 acres of which adjoined the home field of said Robert ...; the other parcel of 25 acres adjoins 40 acres of Lot, hus
1 CONC band of Hanah Killum"; "said Killum promised not to sell this land without his wife's consent"[EQC 7:294].
1 CONT On 14 August 1678, "Robert Goodell of ..., husbandman," for a consideration provided by "my daughter Elizabeth & her former husband John Smith deceased," deeded to "the said Elizabeth, now the wife of Henry Bennett ... a certain p
1 CONC arcel of land called ten acres, yet containing fifteen acres ... & is part of that land of mine not far from my now dwelling ..., also two acres of meadow lying at the great river so-called"; "Margarett Goodell" joined in this dee
1 CONC d and made her mark[ELR 6:66].
1 CONT In his will, dated 12 October 1682 and proved 27 June 1683, "Robert Goodell being now aged & weak in body, as also my wife and my daughter Elizabeth Bennett hath taken care of me," bequeathed to "my daughter Elizabeth Bennett, & m
1 CONC y grandchild John Smith my house & the orchard & all the meadows that I now possess with the pasture, which is about eight acres of upland"[EPR 302:46].
1 CONT On 7 November 1682, "John Pease Senior, aged about 53 years, saith that in his knowledge, the land that my father-in-law Goodell sold to his son-in-law John Smith was laid out by my father Goodell's order & appointment several yea
1 CONC rs before the land that my father gave to his son Isaac Goodell"[ELR 6:80].
1 CONT The inventory of the estate of "the late deceased Robert Goodell," taken 10 March 1682/3, totalled 79lb. 16s., of which 71lb. was real estate: "the house, upland, orchard & meadow," 71lb.[EPR 302:46].
1 CONT On 23 February 1684[/5], "Margarett Goodale of " sold to "the aforesaid town of my whole orchard with six acres of planting ground upon which the orchard stands, & four acres of meadow ground near unto the said orchard & two acres
1 CONC  of ground for a pasture near unto the said orchard & also one cow, all which was given me by my husband Rob[er]t Goodale deceased, for my comfortable subsistence after his decease, as by a deed of gift bearing date the thirteenth
1 CONC  of August one-thousand six-hundred & sixty-nine"[ELR 7:19].
1 CONT BIRTH:About 1604.
1 CONT DEATH:Between 12 October 1682 (date of will) and 10 March 1682/3 (date of inventory).
1 CONT MARRIAGE: (1) By about 1630 Katherine _____. She is not seen in New England (unless she is one of the household of seven on 25 December 1637 [STR 1:102]).
1 CONT (2) About 30 August 1669 Margaret Lazenby (on 30 August 1669, "Robert Goodell have made choice of & by God's permission do fully intend to take to my wife Margaret Lazenby, late of Exeter in New England (& the which being done), I
1 CONC  the said Robert do hereby covenant & promise to & with the said Margaret, that in case she outlives me, to give & bequeath unto her for her comfortable maintenance ... a new dwelling house which I do intend God willing shortly to
1 CONC  build, with what household stuff therein God shall please to continue unto me until my death; item: two cows & a horse or mare fit for her to ride on, also my whole orchard upon my farm near my dwelling house at Bald Hill, with s
1 CONC ix acres of the planting ground upon which the said orchard stands, the best of the said ground & four acres of meadow ground near to my said orchard with a pasture plot of about two acres fenced in near to my said house & orchard
1 CONC  & also that she shall have a competency of fire wood & timber for her use ... & after her decease ... shall fall to my son Jacob Goodell ... [and] in case she outlive my said son Jacob that then she shall have & enjoy for her use
1 CONC  & benefit that part of my estate which I shall & do intend to leave unto my said son Jacob, during her life" [ELR 6:9]). She received support from the town from 1683 to 1689, and on 25 March 1689 the town paid "for digging of gra
1 CONC ves" for four people, including "Goody Goodall" [STR 3:96, 102, 131, 138, 142, 158, 173, 174, 179, 200, 204].
1 CONT CHILDREN:
1 CONT With first wife:
1 CONT i MARY GOODALE, b. about 1630 (aged 4 in 1634 [Hotten 282]); m. by 1654 John Pease (eldest known child b. Salem 20 May 1654).
1 CONT
1 CONT ii ABRAHAM GOODALE, b. about 1632 (aged 2 in 1634 [Hotten 282]); sailed for New England in 1634; no further record.
1 CONT
1 CONT iii ISAAC GOODALE, b. in late 1633 (aged "half a year" on 30 April 1634 [Hotten 282]); m. Salem 25 January 1668[/9?] Patience Cooke.
1 CONT
1 CONT iv ELIZABETH GOODALE, b. say 1636; m. (1) by 1658 John Smith [ELR 1:39-40]; m. (2) Salem [blank] March 1674/5 Henry Bennett (this marriage record as published from the court copy gives the groom's name as "William," but in deeds h
1 CONC e is called "Henry," and these seem to be the more reliable records [ELR 6:66, 9:64]).
1 CONT
1 CONT v SARAH GOODALE, b. say 1638; m. Wenham 4 May 1666 John Batchelder [EQC 3:384].
1 CONT
1 CONT vi ZACHARIAH GOODALE, bp. Salem 31 May 1640 [SChR 17]; m. Salem 31 December 1666 Elizabeth Beacham. (The baptismal record associated with this son does not include a given name, but, with one exception, ages given in depositions c
1 CONC oincide with this date (deposed on 18 July 1676 "aged about thirty years" [EQC 6:191]; deposed 25 November 1679 "aged about forty years" [EQC 7:295]; deposed on 26 March 1685 "aged about forty-five years" [EQC 9:439]).)
1 CONT
1 CONT vii JACOB GOODALE, bp. Salem 9 January 1640/1 [SChR 18]; on 28 April 1669, "Jacob Goodale was ordered to pay 5s. to the constable of Andover for bringing home his son" [EQC 4:142]; on 28 June 1676, "Lot Killum" deposed regarding w
1 CONC hat "Jacob Goodall told him upon his death bed" [EQC 6:191]; on 18 July 1676, "Giles Cory presented upon suspicion of abusing the body of Jacob Goodell by beating, was fined and ordered to pay the witnesses" [EQC 6:190]. (Davis su
1 CONC ggests convincingly that the record of 28 April 1669 incorporates a scribal error, and that the order should apply to Robert Goodale, and that the son named in the record was Jacob; Davis also comments that "Some of the evidence g
1 CONC iven at the time of his death in 1676 points to the conclusion that he [Jacob] was not of entirely normal mentality" [Lydia Harmon Anc 36].)
1 CONT
1 CONT viii HANNAH GOODALE, bp. Salem 6 August 1645; m. Wenham 21 May 1666 Lot Killam [EQC 3:384].
1 CONT
1 CONT COMMENTS:On 27 December 1642, "Robert Goodell" was one of twelve men "presented for breach of order of court, keeping their cattle in the common corn fields" and fined for this infraction[EQC 1:49].On 26 December 1643, "Robert Goo
1 CONC dell" was one of eleven men "fined for putting their cattle into the north corn fields"[EQC 1:56].On 27 August 1644, "Robert Goodell [was] fined for suffering his goat to go in his neighbor's cornfield"[EQC 1:69].On 18 February 16
1 CONC 45/6, "Robert Goodell was accused by Myghel Sollis (also Sallow) of stealing four goats. Sollis had lost one old and three young goats and finding such, with his mark on them, in custody of Goodall, the latter promised to return t
1 CONC hem. When Sollis went for them, Goodall said that he had killed one and the rest were lost. Quit for lack of testimony"[EQC 1:91].
1 CONT On 11 July 1644, "Robert Goodell complained of Ruben Guppy for rent and burning fence"[EQC 1:68].On 27 June 1671, "Rob[er]t Goodell" sued "Gartrid Pope for withholding or molesting, mowing and carrying away the hay on a parcel of 
1 CONC meadow. Withdrawn"[EQC 4:384].
1 CONT In Elizabeth (Beacham) Goodale, wife of Zachariah Goodale, accused her brother-in-law John Smith of sexual assault; this case brought forth many illuminating depositions from family members and neighbors[EQC 5:43, 52-55].
1 CONT In her will of 1 March 160[1/]2, "Elizabeth Goodale" of , mentioned three sons, including Robert, and "Henry Kilham and Alice his wife, my daughter"[Waters 2:1403, citing Archdeaconry of Suffolk 38:478]. This latter couple were th
1 CONC e parents of AUSTIN KILLAM {1637, } and this may be what led George E. Williams to claim that the first wife of Robert Goodale was "Catherine Kilham"[ 5]. While there is no evidence for this identification, the fact that one of th
1 CONC e daughters of Robert Goodale married a son of Austin Killam suggests that there may be some relationship.
1 CONT Williams also claimed that this immigrant was baptized at on 16 August 1601, son of "Robert and Joan (Artys) Goodell"[ 5]. Examination of the Dennington register confirms this baptism, but there is also the baptism of another Robe
1 CONC rt on 17 March 1611, son of Thomas, and we have already seen that the widow Elizabeth also had a son Robert. Although we do not agree with the conclusions made by Williams, further investigation of the records of and vicinity shou
1 CONC ld reveal the correct identity of this immigrant.
1 CONT Robert Goodale was an interesting character, who did not fully participate in his community, but who, throughout his half century of residence in Salem, pursued a single goal with great determination. So far as we can tell he was 
1 CONC never a church member, never a freeman and never an officeholder. The vast majority of the records in which he appears, even his few minor criminal transgressions, involve his dealing with land. He devoted much effort to the amass
1 CONC ing of land, and the provision of competent estates for his children[ 33].
1 CONT BIBLIOGRAPHIC NOTE:In Mary Lovering Holman prepared a very brief account of this immigrant and his family[ 210]. In 1924 Walter Goodwin Davis published a more complete account of this immigrant and some of his descendants[ 33-44].
1 CONC  In 1984 George E. Williams compiled a genealogy of the descendants of Robert Goodale, which in the immigrant generation relies overmuch on tradition and speculation[ 5-7].
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert Charles Anderson, THE GREAT MIGRATION: Immigrants to New England 1634-1635, Volume III, G-H.  Boston: New England Historic Genealogical Society, 2003
0 @N6000000007150036846@ NOTE BIOGRAPHY
1 CONT William and Elizabeth Goodell, the grandparents of Robert were
1 CONT probably members of the Godelle or Goodelle family, French Huguenots
1 CONT who emigrated to London in the 1530's. Huguenots were Protestant
1 CONT followers of John Calvin during the 1500's and 1600's. It's members
1 CONT were persecuted in France and suffered greatly. In all, eight wars
1 CONT were fought between the Huguenots and French Catholics between 1562
1 CONT and 1598 in their struggle for survival. Although the protestants had
1 CONT the support of many French leaders and nobility, the queen mother,
1 CONT Catherine de Médicis made a compact with the Duke of Guise which
1 CONT resulted in the Massacre of Saint Bartholomew's Day on August 24,
1 CONT 1572, when thousands of Huguenots were slaughtered. During this
1 CONT period of per-secution more than two million of the Huguenots left
1 CONT France to seek a more friendly atmosphere. The Goodells were among
1 CONT that number who emigrated to England. The name appears as Goodell,
1 CONT Goodale, Goodall, or according to the late Rev. Isaac Goodell, as
1 CONT "some other of the 18 variations". We find the historian Bowen and
1 CONT other genealogists call him Goodell, while the historian Perly calls
1 CONT him Goodale.
1 CONT Although his birth year was given as 1604 in his oath of allegiance
1 CONT when sailing for America, one of Robert's descendants has verified the
1 CONT church baptisimal record as 1601. The "Elizabeth" left Ipswich,
1 CONT England on April 30, 1634 and landed at Boston in July. At that time,
1 CONT Robert Goodell was 30, his wife Katherine was 28, their daughter Mary
1 CONT 4 years old, their son Abraham was 2 and their baby, Isaac was 6
1 CONT months old. The first record of the Goodells in America indicated
1 CONT that they settled just north of Salem at Great Cove in the North
1 CONT Fields.
1 CONT The Spring, so often mentioned in the early records, in what was
1 CONT later called Liberty Hill Park, was on Robert Goodell's farm and was
1 CONT known for the first half century of Salem's history as Goodell's
1 CONT Spring. It is not recorded how he acquired this first farm. However,
1 CONT in 1636 and in 1638 he received grants of twenty acres each in that
1 CONT portion of the town which became known as Salem Village, and he
1 CONT gradually acquired by purchase similar grants made to other early
1 CONT settlers, until by 1651, he was the owner of a tract of land at Bald
1 CONT Hill comprising four hundred and eighty acres, which was con-firmed to
1 CONT him by a town grant on November 7, 1651. He probably moved to this
1 CONT new property at about that time, and as opportunity offered, disposed
1 CONT of his Great Cove farm, deeding the house and a portion of the land to
1 CONT his son-in-law, John Smith in 1658. Another lot was conveyed to
1 CONT Nicholas Manning in 1667, and a third conveyance was probably made to
1 CONT John Orne, who owned a part of the property, hitherto unconveyed in
1 CONT 1684.
1 CONT Robert Goodell seems to have devoted his life to the developement of
1 CONT his large grant for the benefit of his sons and daughters. As they
1 CONT married, he gave them a generous acreage as a wedding gift, thus
1 CONT creating a family settlement about his own homestead. Although his
1 CONT clear firm handwriting would seem to indicate a good education, he
1 CONT took no part in local or broader govern-mental affairs and his name
1 CONT seldom appears in the records in any active capacity except as
1 CONT plaintiff or defendant in suits based on the ownership of his stock or
1 CONT land.
1 CONT His will reads as follows, "I, Robert Goodell being now aged & weake
1 CONT in body, as alsoe my wife and my daughter Elizabeth Bennett, hath
1 CONT taken care of me and therefore my will & desire is & I doe will &
1 CONT bequeath unto my daughter Elizabeth Bennett & my grand child, John
1 CONT Smith, my house & the orchard & all the meadowes that I now possess
1 CONT with the pasture which is about eight acres of upland be it more or
1 CONT lesse, all which house, land & meadows my daughter Elizabeth Bennett,
1 CONT and my Grand child John Smith, shall enjoy after the lease, or terme,
1 CONT that it is now let for, is expired, they or either of them paying as
1 CONT much rent, yearly as the wife of the abovesaid Robert Goodell hath let
1 CONT it for which is to the value of twenty shillings in current pay, dated
1 CONT the twelfth of October one thousand six hundred eighty two; & after my
1 CONT daughter Elizabeth's decease, the whole lands shall be my grand
1 CONT child's John Smith. In witness I have hereunto set my hand & seal.
1 CONT Francis Scerry Robert Goodell
1 CONT John Massey his X marke & seale
1 CONT The said Francis Scerry & John Massey, whoe are entered as witnesses
1 CONT to the above written, made oath in court in Salem that in their
1 CONT presence the said Robert did signe & seale to the above written as his
1 CONT act & deed & last will & testament: In Court as Salem; 27 June 83.
1 CONT Attesr. Hilliard Verne, Clerk
1 CONT "We whose names are under written being desired by the widdow Margaret
1 CONT Goodell wife of the late deceased Robert Goodell, do appr'se the
1 CONT estate of the deceased Robert Goodell, aprizes as followeth,
1 CONT £ OE d
1 CONT the house uplands orchard & meadow 71: 00: 00
1 CONT 2 cowes 5 lbs, wearing apparell
1 CONT bedding & other lumber 3 £ 1 OE _08:___16:___00_
1 CONT 79: 16: 00
1 CONT Salem the 10 Day of March 1682/3
1 CONT Nathaniel Putnam
1 CONT Job Swinerton
