0 @N6000000004548007998@ NOTE From: The Munson Record: "Abel's father (Joseph) and his father-in-law (Atwater Cook) were friends who migrated together and settled in the same neighborhood."
1 CONT
1 CONT From the History of Herkimer County, NY., CHAPTER XXIX.
1 CONT
1 CONT HON. EZRA GRAVES Was of early New England ancestry. He was born at Russia in this county December 1, 1803, and was the son of Hon. John Graves, who came to that town in 1795, clearing a farm in the "wild woods" and encountering al
1 CONC l the hardships of frontier life, till he rose in position to be member of Assembly and then Sheriff, and living to the age of seventy-six. His son Ezra at an early age worked at the trade of tanner and currier, having been employ
1 CONC ed as such by Abel Munson and Colonel Amos Griswold in Salisbury, and by other residents of that and the neighboring towns. At twenty-three years of age he was married at Newport to Miss Maria Card. In the year 1827 he with others
1 CONC  founded the village of Florence; but soon afterwards he determined to commence the study of the law. He pursued his studies in the law office of General Charles Gray from 1832 to 1835, when he was admitted to the bar and immediat
1 CONC ely began practice. His first office was that of under sheriff, while his father was sheriff; his second was that of commissioner of deeds; his third that of supervisor of Herkimer, to which position he was elected in 1840 and re-
1 CONC elected in 1841. 
0 @N6000000004548139606@ NOTE Benton's History of Herkimer County:
1 CONT County Clerk of Herkimer County starting in 1841 for six years, Undersheriff for 3 years, Supervisor for one year, Justice of the Peace for 12 years, until the time of his death.
0 @N6000000000337374676@ NOTE Source# 1:  David Atwater,  "A GENEALOGICAL REGISTER OF THE DESCENDANTS IN THE MALE LINE OF DAVID ATWATER, ONE OF THE ORIGINAL PLANTERS OF NEW HAVEN, CT., TO THE SIXTH GENERATION"
1 CONT NEW HAVEN, CT: PRINTED BY TUTTLE, MOREHOUSE & TAYLOR (1873)
1 CONT
1 CONT 1. David Atwater was one of the first planters of New Haven, and in the first division of lands among the settlers a farm was assigned him in the "Neck," as the tract between Mill and Quinnipiac Rivers was called, upon which it is
1 CONC  believed he lived till his death, which occurred Oct. 5th, 1692. His children were-- 
1 CONT
1 CONT    2. Mercy, born Feb. 29, 1647; married John Austin. 
1 CONT    3. Damaris, b. Nov. 12, 1648; m. John Punderson. 
1 CONT    4. David, b. July 13, 1650. 
1 CONT    5. Joshua, b. Jan. 11, 1652. 
1 CONT    6. John, b. Nov. 1, 1654. 
1 CONT    7. Jonathan, b. July 12, 1656. 
1 CONT    8. Abigail, b. March 3, 1660; m. Nathaniel Jones. 
1 CONT    9. Mary, b. March 31, 1662; m. Ichabod Stow. 
1 CONT   10. Samuel, b. Sept. 17, 1664. 
1 CONT   11. Ebenezer, b. Jan. 13, 1666. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Source #2:  Francis Atwater:  "Atwater History and Genealogy" (The Journal Publiching Company. Meriden, CT 1907)
1 CONT
1 CONT David Atwater, son of Thomas and Susan Narsen, baptized in Lenham church October 8, 1615. He was born in Royton in Lenham, England, and in the month in which he became twenty-one years of age, October, 1636, his father died, and h
1 CONC is mother died scarcely more than two months later,--in January, 1637. In less than six months from the latter event, June 26, 1637, the brothers, Joshua and David, with their sister Ann, arrived in Boston. It cannot be doubted th
1 CONC at their arrangements for removal, so hastily made at that time of general discontent and apprehension in church and state affairs, involved large pecuniary sacrifices. 
1 CONT
1 CONT It is seen that David was in his twenty-second year when he came to New England in 1637. If he was one of those who accompanied Mr. Eaton to Quinnipiack in the autumn of that year, he returned to Boston, for only seven of the comp
1 CONC any, of whom Joshua was one, remained at Quinnipiack. It may be believed that David and his sister Ann, remaining in Boston that winter, sailed with the company for their new home in the spring of 1638. He signed the plantation co
1 CONC venant June 4, 1639, the day of the meeting of the constituent assembly in Mr. Newman's barn, which was the commencement or foundation of the Colony of New Haven. 
1 CONT
1 CONT He was unmarried previous to 1643, when he appears alone on the list of planters, with a valuation upon his estate of œ500, "according to which he will pay his proportion in all Rates and Public charges from time to time to be ass
1 CONC essed for civil uses, and expect Lands in all divisions which shall generally be made to the planters." He was one of twenty-nine whose estates were on the list at œ500 or more. 
1 CONT
1 CONT He married Damaris Sayre, daughter of Thomas Sayre, of Southampton, L. I., before March 10, 1646-7, the date of the General Court, when the name of "David Atwater's wife" was read among those seated in the meeting-house. She died 
1 CONC April 7, 1691. Upon the union of the New Haven and Connecticut Colonies, consummated at a General Court held at Hartford, May 11, 1665, David Atwater was the first of the New Haven Colony who was sworn a freeman of the united colo
1 CONC ny. He died October 5, 1692. 
0 @N6000000003783906387@ NOTE In the year 1637 brothers Josua and David Atwater along with their siser
1 CONT Ann Atwater came to America. They were accompanied by Messrs. Eaton and
1 CONT Davenport as well as others.  They were founders of the new colony, New
1 CONT Haven.  There was much religious unrest in the Mother Country, England.
1 CONT This may have been part of the reason the Atwater young people camt to
1 CONT America.
1 CONT
1 CONT Credit is given to David Atwater as being the first signer of the planters
1 CONT agreement which was the start of the New Haven Colony. Brother Joshua had
1 CONT been one of the 7 pioneers to visit and spend the winter there as David
1 CONT and Ann had stayed in New York, where they had docked at.
0 @N6000000001657816569@ NOTE
1 CONT David Atwater was baptized in Royton in Lenham, Kent, England, on Sunday, October 8, 1615, and died in Cedar Hill, New Haven, Connecticut, on October 5, 1692. Damaris Sayre was born in Leighton Buzzard, Bedfordshire, England, in—s
1 CONC ay—1625, and died in New Haven, Connecticut, on April 1, 1691. He is the son of John and Susan (Narsin) Atwater. She is the daughter of Thomas and Margaret (Aldrich) Sayre. They had ten children.
1 CONT
1 CONT Also: http://aleph0.clarku.edu/~djoyce/gen/conn/rr01/rr01_393.html#P8340
1 CONT On 27 Nov 1718 Mary second married John Atwater, son of David Atwater (ca 1615-5 Oct 1692) & Damaris Sayre (-1 Apr 1691), in Wallingford, CT.14 Born on 1 Nov 1654 in New Haven, CT.12 John was baptized in First Congregational Socie
1 CONC ty, New Haven on 1 Nov 1654.1 John died in 1748.1
1 CONT
