0 @N6000000002247908409@ NOTE About the following:
1 CONT Amos H. Powers incorrectly thought that the girl was Mary Powers because he did not know that Trial Shepard had a younger sister, Mary Shepard.  Most likely the story below is this Mary.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT The following was copied from: The Powers Family by Amos H. Powers
1 CONT                                                    Printed in 1884
1 CONT
1 CONT Mary Power and Lieut. Joseph Wheeler, who was probably a son a Lieut. Joseph Wheeler, later, Capt Wheeler, were the grandparnets of Capt. Glazier Wheeler, noted in the history of New Hampshire.  May she not be the "little girl" re
1 CONC ferred to by S. G. Drake, and copied by the writer in 1853 from his manuscript account?  February 12, 1676, Jacob and Isaac Seppard were killed near Nashobe, in Concord Village, as they were threshing in their barn. Being afraid o
1 CONC f a surprise, they had set a little girl, their sister Mary, about 15 years old, on an eminence to look out; but the Indians stole upon and took her captive, and then proceeded to the barn, and killed her brothers.  She was carrie
1 CONC d away to their town or encampment, and not long after made her heroic escape.  As her captors were asleep in the night, she took one of the horses they had stolen not long before from Lancaster, and mounting upon it, with a saddl
1 CONC e which she got from under the head of one of the sleeping Indians, rode off; coming to the Nashua River, she dashed through it and thence through the woods to her own home."  The question may fairly be raised if the Sheppards wer
1 CONC e not threshing in the barn of their brother-in-law, and if the little girl "Mary" was not their niece, who was just the age described at that time.  We have no record that the Sheppards had a barn in Concord Village, or a sister
0 @N6000000006348677387@ NOTE "Abby married Lyman Leonard of Springfield, and with him espoused the Mormon faith, following the fortunes of that sect to Nauvoo and Salt Lake; had one son and a daughter."
0 @N6000000006348486542@ NOTE From a correspondent:
1 CONT Margaret was daughter of Andrew Toshach and Katherine Campbell of
1 CONT Monzievaird. I think she was born around 1650. Her parents married in 1641
1 CONT and had David, Margaret and Duncan. There were probably other children but
1 CONT I'm sure of these 3. Margaret had her last child in 1687 and her first about
1 CONT 1670. I estimate the order of the children as;
1 CONT Hugh [an adult in 1691] bc 1670
1 CONT Patrick [had wife and son by 1697] bc 1672
1 CONT Katherine [m 1694] bc 1674
1 CONT Jane m a Lawrence
1 CONT John b Jan. 1687
1 CONT Since there is no son Andrew, the naming pattern may not be useful here
1 CONT although they may have had an Andrew who died.
1 CONT As for Margaret, the last reference I have for her is dated 1714.
0 @N6000000006348486536@ NOTE
1 CONT
1 CONT MacGregorie may have been of the MacGregor Clan.  He lived during a time when it had become illegal to use the name "MacGregor."  The following are excepted from the Electric Scotland's website history of the Clan MacGregor.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the reign of David II [1329-1371], the Campbells managed to procure a legal title to the lands of Glenorchy; nevertheless, the Macgregors maintained, for a long time, the actual possession of them by the strong hand. They knew 
1 CONC no other right than that of the sword, but ultimately that was found unavailing, and at last, expelled from their own territory they became an outlawed, lawless and landless clan.
1 CONT
1 CONT John Macgregor of Glenorchy, who died in 1390, is said to have had three sons; Patrick, his successor’ John Dow, ancestor of the family of Glenstrae, who became the chief of the clan; and Greogor, ancestor of the Macgregors of Ror
1 CONC o. Patrick’s son, Malcolm, was compelled by the Campbells to sell the lands of Auchinrevach in Strathfillan to Campbell of Glenorchy, who thus obtained the first footing in Breadalbane, which afterwards gave the title of earl to h
1 CONC is family.
1 CONT
1 CONT The principle families of the Macgregors, in process of time, except that of Glenstrae, who held that estate as vassals of the Earl of Argyll, found themselves reduced to the position of tenants on the lands of Campbell of Glenorc
1 CONC hy and other powerful barons. It being the policy of the latter to get rid of them altogether, the unfortunate clan was driven, by a continuous system of oppression and annoyance, to acts of rapine and violence, which brought upon
1 CONC  them the vengeance of the government. The clan had no other means of subsistence than the plunder of their neighbours’ property, and as they naturally directed their attacks chiefly against those who had wrested from them their o
1 CONC wn lands, it became still more the interest of their oppressors to represent to the king that nothing could put a stop to their lawless conduct, "save the cutting off the tribe of Macgregor root and branch". In 1488, soon after th
1 CONC e youthful James IV had ascended the throne which the murder of his father had rendered vacant, an act was passed "for staunching of thiftreif and other enormities throw all the realme"; evidently designed against the Macgregors, 
1 CONC for among the barons to whom power was given for enforcing it, were Duncan Campbell of Glenorchy, Neil Stewart of Fortingall, and Ewin Campbell of Strachur. At this time the Macgregors were still a numerous clan. Besides those in 
1 CONC Glenorchy, they were settled in great numbers in the districts of Breadalbane and Athol, and they all acknowledged Macgregor of Glenstrae, who bore the title of captain of the clan, as their chief.
1 CONT
1 CONT  In 1589 they [MacGregors] seized and murdered John Drummond of Drummond Ernoch, a forester of the royal forest of Glenartney, an act which forms the foundation of the incident detailed in Sir Walter Scott’s "Legend of Montrose".
1 CONT
1 CONT Among other severe measures passed against this doomed clan was one which deprived them of their very name. By an act of the privy council, dated 3d April 1603, all of the name of Macgregor were compelled, on pain of death, to ado
1 CONC pt another surname, and all who had been engaged at the battle of Glenfruin, and other marauding expeditions detailed in the act, were prohibited, also under pain of death, from carrying any weapon but a knife without a point to c
1 CONC ut their victuals. They were also forbidden, under the same penalty of death, to meet in greater numbers that four at a time.
1 CONT
1 CONT The favourite names assumed by the clan while compelled to relinquish their own, were Campbell, Graham, Stewart, and Drummond. Their unity as a clan remained unbroken, and they even seemed to increase in numbers, notwithstanding a
1 CONC ll the oppresive proceedings directed against them. These did not cease with the reign of James VI, for under Charles I all the enactments against them were renewed, and yet in 1644, when the Marquis of Montrose set up the king’s 
1 CONC standard in the Highlands, the clan Gregor, to the number of 1000 fighting men, joined him, under the command of Patrick Macgregor of Glenstrae, their chief. In reward for their loyalty, at the Restoration the various statutes aga
1 CONC inst them were annulled, when the clan men were enabled to resume their own name. In the reign of William III however, the penal enactments against them were renewed in their full force. The clan were again proscribed, and compell
1 CONC ed once more to take other names.
1 CONT
1 CONT From a correspondent:
1 CONT 1. Patrick's service in France.
1 CONT As far as I know the only source for this is a letter written by Gov. Thomas
1 CONT Dongan of the New York Province in Feb. 1687. He says Patrick "served
1 CONT formerly in France". He doesn't actually say he knew him but it seems a good
1 CONT assumption. Dongan was an Irish Catholic nobleman whose family fled to France
1 CONT when it seemed sure that Cromwell would win in England. He joined other
1 CONT British royalists in the French army and served for 30 years rising to the
1 CONT rank of Colonel [1648-1678]. In 1678 Charles II ordered British troops to
1 CONT return home. [He became king in 1660.] So I believe Patrick was in France
1 CONT between 1660 and 1678 and probably late in this period and certainly under
1 CONT Charles II not Charles I. It is impossible to be sure but I put Patrick's
1 CONT birth around 1640 or later.
1 CONT
1 CONT Patrick Mc Gregory
1 CONT and the Macgregorie Expedition!
1 CONT
1 CONT by
1 CONT Stefan Bielinski
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT During the 1680s, New York sought to push English claims westward and northward into lands also claimed by France. One manifestation of this initiative can be seen in Governor Thomas Dongan's issuing of trading licenses to private
1 CONC  (but officially sanctioned) trading groups. One of these groups was led by Patrick Mc Gregory.
1 CONT
1 CONT Patrick Mc Gregory/Magregorie was a Scottish opportunist who landed a group of people in Maryland in 1684. Tradition tells of a military background possibly explaining why he was called "Major" and "Colonel." Moving north through 
1 CONC New Jersey, he sought land on Staten Island but instead settled on the Hudson Highlands where he attempted to patent an estate.
1 CONT
1 CONT He appears to have been connected to Governor Thomas Dongan and his enterprises. In 1686, Dongan appointed "Major Magregorie" mustermaster general of the New York militia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Learning native languages, he entered the Indian trade. With Dongan's permission, in 1687 he led a party to trade with the Ottawas at Michilimakinac. In his group were a number of ambitious/daring young men from Albany including J
1 CONC ohannes Bleecker, Jr. and Arnout C. Viele. That spring, the adventurers were captured by the French and Indians and Mc Gregory was sent as a prisoner to Montreal. Freed by the French governor as a goodwill gesture, a year later he
1 CONC  served Governor Edmund Andros in command of a company of soldiers against the Indians in Maine.
1 CONT
1 CONT He returned to New York City where he was caught up in the turmoil surrounding the insurgency of Jacob Leisler. He was killed in March 1691 while trying to take the fort from Leisler's forces.
1 CONT
1 CONT At the time of his death, he was in the process of securing title to land in present-day Orange County.
1 CONT
1 CONT Colonel Mc Gregory left several sons and daughters who became residents of New York. Young Patrick Mc Gregory came with his father from Scotland and married the widow Zeytie Hooghteeling Marselis in 1697. By 1704, four of their ch
1 CONC ildren had been baptized in the Albany Dutch church. He was a soldier in one of the garrison companies stationed at Albany and later was named the city carman or porter and also was a "freeman" of Albany.
1 CONT
1 CONT Source:  http://www.nysm.nysed.gov/albany/bios/m/pamcgreg.html
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Janet Hunter
1 CONT Patrick MacGregory/Macgreiger Scotland>USA 1684
1 CONT Sat Dec 30 19:34:52 2000
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Hello MacGregor Cousins:
1 CONT
1 CONT I do believe I may have my second line of MacGregor ancestors. Do any of you folks in Scotland know where the fellow described in this "broadside" referred to below might fit in. I have information on his family in New York, and m
1 CONC y branch moved to North Carolina after lands were confiscated ca 1691 in New York (confiscation of lands must be a family curse without geographical boundaries).
1 CONT
1 CONT Any thoughts on this Patrick MacGregory (many spellings) are much appreciated. He is the Captain referred to below.
1 CONT
1 CONT "In a broadside advertisement circulated in Scotland in 1683/84 urging settlers to sign up for East Jersey it states "in the shire of Pearth(sic) let them apply themselves to David Toshach of Monyvard and Captain Macgreiger....etc
1 CONC ." After a harrowing crossing, during which one of their vessels was nearly wrecked in a hurricane off the Capes of Virginia they limped into port
1 CONT in the Chesapeake at the Bohemia River (near the upper end of Chesapeake Bay),and then they had to make their way from there to East Jersey. 
1 CONT
1 CONT They eventually ended up in New Windsor, Orange Co NY, with the Toshachs and a number of Palatine families from Germany. Captain McGregory continued to lead troops, including an excursion ca 1687 into Ontario against the French (a
1 CONC nd Indians), where he was captured, but returned
1 CONT
1 CONT Best Regards,
1 CONT
1 CONT Janet Baugh Hunter...other line is Patrick MacGregor & Marian MacDonald>William MackGayhee (the brother of Iain who moved to Virginia according to the legend, see www.mcgeheezone.com)>Thomas MacGehee & Ann Baytop/Bastrop>Sarah McG
1 CONC ehee & Thomas Lipscomb
