0 @N6000000003177827119@ NOTE Daniel Brainerd was the progenitor of nearly all who bear the nameBrai nerd/Brainard. He was born in 1641. His name, originally wasBrainwood . He was stolen from his native town of Braintree, Essex,England, whe n he was about 8 ye
1 CONC ars old and brought to America. He wassold to Mr. W adsworth, a farmer in Hartford, for his passage:"To belearned to read and write and at the time of his freedom when twenty-oneyears old to b e given two suits of clothes." It is 
1 CONC difficult to see whatadvantage wa s gained from all this business by whoever it was that"stole" young Da niel if all he got for the boy was the recovery of theexpense of passa ge. Farmer Wadsworth, of course got thirtenn years ofs
1 CONC ervice from the lad; and the boy himself seems not to have done badly,besides getting an education of sorts and two suits of clothes. He livedwith the Wadsw orth family in Hartford and remaining there until 1662 whenhe turned 2 1 
1 CONC and was freed. Then, with others, he went to Haddam and tookup land and made the new town his permanent home, where there was stillunbroke n wilderness. He and the others had to fell trees for theirhouses. He and his wife, Hannah 
1 CONC Spencer, and their eight childrenworshipped in Mi ddletown, CT(about 8 miles away) before permission wasgranted to the c itizens of Haddam, by the General Court of CT, to build achurch. He be came a merchant in Haddam and built a 
1 CONC store out of logs. Hebecame a De acon of the Church, a Commisioner in 1669, and a Deputy to theGeneral COurt. Deacon Daniel Brainerd died April 1, 1715, at age 74. Heis buri ed in the ancient burying ground in Haddam, a few rods e
1 CONC ast of theCour thouse. His home is town plot No. 5 1/2 and bounded on the west byMai n Street, north by John Bailey, east by the river, and south by Joseph Stannard. Home lot was 4 acres. Daniel married 1st HannahSpencer(daugh ter
1 CONC  of Girrad SPencer and Hannah Hills) in 1663, 2ndElizabeth Wakeman on March 30, 1693 and 3rd Hannah Spencer(daughter ofThomas(Sargeant) S pencer and Sarah Bearding) on November 3, 1698
1 CONT
1 CONT Daniel Brainerd's children were baptized in Middletown, about eigghtmi les distance, before permisiion was granted to the citizens of Haddamb y the General Court to build a church and with a settled minister toma intain independen
1 CONC t worship, to baptize the children, to performmarriag e ceremony and to bury the dead. The journey thither was onhorseback, following the Indian trail, which became a bridle path, withthe wife s eated on a pillion, carrying the ch
1 CONC ild in her arms. Thechildren were b aptized as "children of ye church of Christ in Lyn(Lynn,Mass.) receive d ye initiatory seal of ye covenant baptism by virtue ofCommunion of C hurches."
1 CONT
1 CONT Daniel Brainerd held many public offices of that day. He was repeatedl yhonored by reappointment to the office of constable, surveyor, fencev iewer, town assessor, collector, and justice of the peace, and withoth ers was one appoi
1 CONC nted to stake out land and to lay out highways. Hewa s elected Commisioner by the General Court in Hartford, CT 1669. He wa sone if a committee to secure minister, Jan 1, 1682 and was elected de aconof the Congregational Church in
1 CONC  that place, serving in that capaci tythrough his life.
1 CONT
1 CONT WILL OF DANIEL BRAINERD
1 CONT
1 CONT Inventory and Agreement in CT Probate Records, Volume IX, 1710 to 1715 ,Page 37
1 CONT
1 CONT BRAINERD, DANIEL, HAddam inventory 834-10-03 Taken 19 April 1715, byNa thaniel Spencer, Gerrard Spencer and Jodeph Arnold. An agreement, date dthe 2nd of May, 1715 A distribution of the estate by an agreement amo ngthe children, D
1 CONC aniel, James, Joshua, William, Caleb, Elijah andHezek iah(sons), Thomas Gates and Hannah his wife(only daughter); Ourfather' s will not being so full and clears as we could wish, we have thought best to agree upon a distribution o
1 CONC f the estate of our honoredfather. And having first come to a loving agreement with our honoredmother in law, Hannah Brainerd, we have proceeded to make the followingdistribut ion: I, Thomas Gates, acting  in behalf of my wife Han
1 CONC nah, andwe, Dani el, James, Joshua, WIlliam, Caleb, Elijah and Hezekiah have fullyand f reely made up our honored father's Daniel Brainerd's his estate, andd o hereby promise and engage, for ourselves and heirs, each to take ups a
1 CONC tisfied of lands heretofore set down to us and to make no morechallen ges or demands of any more lands or rights, neither by virtue orunder color of any deed of gift received from our father in his lifetimeor u pon any other color
1 CONC  or pretense whatsoever, and that we do receivesd. lands and every parcel of them as or sd. father held them the day ofhi s death, each running the risk of the title our father had to them, an dbeing obliged to defend the same for
1 CONC  his own part if there(was)occais ion. Moreover we the sd Daniel, James, Joshua, William, Caleb andElija h, do hereby(declare) that we have received 8-01-00 apiece out ofthe p ersonal estate. I, Thomas Gates, do acknowledge that I
1 CONC  have received33 -01-00 and I Hezekiah Brainerd, have received 32-12-99 Moreover we doa ll and each of us for ourselves and heirs hereby covenant and promiset hat we shall amke no demands of estate, real or personal, that was our 
1 CONC father's and that we will in no wise disturb or annoy each other, bute ach of the eight aforementioned and their heirs may quietly enjoy hisp art aforementioned. Under the full ratification of all which premises wehave all and eac
1 CONC h of us hereunto set our hand and affixed seal this 2ndMay, 1715.
1 CONT Daniel Braynard, LS
1 CONT James Braynard, LS
1 CONT Joshua Braynard, LS
1 CONT William Braynard, LS
1 CONT Caleb Braynard, LS
1 CONT Elijah Braynard, LS
1 CONT Hezekiah Braybard, LS
1 CONT
1 CONT To Thomas Gates,acting on behslf of his wife Hannah(only daughter of o urhonored father) 50 acres of land bought of Mr. Blackleach, on the no rthside of Haddam bounds i COlchester bounds more, in personal or move ableestate. Total
1 CONC , 45-10-00 COurt Record, Page 253-14 April, 1715:Admi nistration granted to Daniel and Hezekiah Brainad jointly. Theyexhibit ed an inventory of sd. estate and also an agreement for divisionof th e estae, which was accepted by the 
1 CONC court.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sources:
1 CONT 1. Lucy Abigail Brainard. Genealogy of the Brainerd-Brainard Family iA merica 1649-1908. Hartford Press, The Case, Lockwood, Brainard Company ,1908. Page 41.
0 @N6000000006348250431@ NOTE Thomas Spencer was a sergeant of the militia in the Pequot War.  He settled in Hartford, CT in 1636.
1 CONT
1 CONT The following comes from: "The Great Migration Begins:  Immigrants to New England 1620-1633" by Robert Charles Anderson:
1 CONT
1 CONT SPENCER, THOMAS [1633, Cambridge]
1 CONT THOMAS SPENCER
1 CONT
1 CONT ORIGIN:  Stotfold, Bedfordshire
1 CONT MIGRATION:  1633
1 CONT FIRST RESIDENCE:  Cambridge
1 CONT REMOVES:  Hartford 1636
1 CONT
1 CONT CHURCH MEMBERSHIP:  Admission to Cambridge church prior to 14 May 1634 implied by freemanship.
1 CONT FREEMAN:  14 May 1634 (sixth in a sequence of nine Cambridge men) [MBCR 1:369].
1 CONT EDUCATION:  He made his mark to his will.
1 CONT OFFICES:  Petit jury, 7 March 1649/50, 15 May 1650, June 1651, 7 September 1652, 7 June 1655, 1 December 1659, 6 June 1661, 5 June 1662 [RPCC 77, 82, 101, 111, 143, 205, 234, 248].  Hartford constable, 4 March 1657[/8] [RPCC 185].
1 CONT ESTATE:  Granted one rood for a cowyard in Cambridge, 5 August 1633 [CaTR 5].  Granted a proportional share of one in the undivided meadow, 20 August 1635 [CaTR 13].  In the 8 February 1635/6 list of house, credited with one in to
1 CONC wn [CaTR 18].
1 CONT      In the Cambridge inventory of lands on 1 May 1635 "Thomas Spenser" held four parcels: "in the town one house with a garden plot and backside about one rood"; two acres in Smalllot Hill; one acre in the Neck; and one acre more
1 CONC  in the Neck [CaBOP 7].  The first two of these parcels were purchased by Edmund Angier [CaBOP 50].
1 CONT      In the Hartford land inventory in February 1639/40 "Thom[as] Spenser, sergeant at arms," held thirteen parcels (some of which he acquired after the date of the inventory): "one parcel on which his dwelling house now standeth 
1 CONC with yards & gardens therein being which was sequestered for Thom[as] Fisher & is now settled on Thom[as] Spenser part whereof he hath sold to Will[ia]m Spenser & now remaineth by estimation one acre"; one acre and two roods in th
1 CONC e West Field; three roods in the Soldier's Field; four acres in the North Meadow; one acre and two roods on the east side of the Great River; one acre and one rood on the east side of the Great River "which is a parcel of that whi
1 CONC ch was sequestered for Mr. Woolcott"; four acres on the east side of the Great River "which he bought of Will[ia]m Spenser for a parcel of land which lay in the Middle Oxpasture & contained seven acres two roods & was parcel of th
1 CONC e said Thom[as] Spenser's land"; three acres and two roods in the cowpasture; five acres and twenty perches in the cowpasture; five acres in the Little Oxpasture "which he bought of Nath[aniel] Yela for land belonging to the said 
1 CONC Thomas Spencer"; four acres & two roods in the Little Oxpasture "which he received of Richard Goodman for land belonging to the said Thomas Spencer"; two acres in the Little Oxpasture "which he bought of Ric[hard] Goodman" on 24 F
1 CONC ebruary 1653; and four acres on the east side of the Great River "which he bought of Joseph Eston" on 24 February 1653 [HaBOP 152-54].
1 CONT      In his will, dated 9 September 1686 and proved 26 October 1687, "Thomas Spencer Senior of Hartford, ... finding the shadows of the evening are stretching over me," bequeathed to "my eldest son Obadiah ... my housing and land 
1 CONC in Hartford, all my lands both meadow and upland on west side and east side of the Connecticut River" in Hartford (he paying certain legacies), along with some personal goods; to "my son Thomas" œ15, of which he hath œ5; to "my so
1 CONC n Samuel (he having received a good portion of his uncle) all my wearing apparell"; to "my son Jarrad my shop & tools & œ12, all of which are in his hands"; residue to "my five daughters Sarah, Elizabeth, Hannah, Mary and Martha, 
1 CONC to be equally divided to them by their brother my son Jarrad"; "my son Obadiah Spencer my executor"; "Capt. Allyn, Lieut. Caleb Standly and my cousin Samuel Spencer" overseers [Hartford PD Case #5095; Manwaring 1:365].
1 CONT      The "inventory of the estate of Sergeant Thomas Spencer who deceased the 11th of September 1687" totalled œ139 19s., including œ132 in real estate: "six acres of land on the east side Coneticutt River," œ60; "four acres of la
1 CONC nd in the long meadow," œ20; "four acres of woodland," œ2; and "his homelot and housing," œ50; after listing the few items of personal estate, the appraisers noted that "the other moveable estate was distributed by himself and del
1 CONC ivered to his daughters most of it in his lifetime and the remainder before the inventory was taken as his executors gave the account of said estate" [Hartford PD Case #5095].
1 CONT
1 CONT BIRTH:  Baptized Stotfold, Bedfordshire, 29 March 1607, son of Gerard and Alice (Whitbread) Spencer [TAG 27:162].
1 CONT DEATH:  Hartford 11 September 1687 (from inventory).
1 CONT MARRIAGE: (1) After 2 November 1634 Anne Dorryfall, sister of BARNABY DORRYFALL [NEHGR 105:197-99].  "Anne Dorifall, aged 24," sailed on the Elizabeth of Ipswich in 1634 [Hotten 281] and was admitted to Boston church 2 November 16
1 CONC 34 as "Ann Derifall, our brother Wm. Coddington's maid servant" [BChR 19].  She died by 1645.
1 CONT      (2) Hartford 11 September 1645 Sarah Bearding [HaVR 606], daughter of Nathaniel Bearding of Hartford.
1 CONT CHILDREN:
1 CONT      With first wife
1 CONT      i    OBADIAH ("eldest son"), b. say 1635; m. by about 1666 Mary Disborough (estimated b. of first child), daughter of Nicholas Disborough [TAG 27:166, 38:209].
1 CONT      ii   THOMAS, b. say 1637; m. by about 1666 Esther Andrews (estimated b. of first child), daughter of William Andrews [TAG 27:166-67, 35:57].
1 CONT      iii  SAMUEL, b. about 1639 (if he is the Samuel who d. Windham 8 January 1726/7 aged about 88 [NEHGR 105:198], and if that age at death is not exaggerated); m. by about 1684 Sarah _____ (estimated b. of first child [TAG 27:16
1 CONC 7]).
1 CONT      With second wife
1 CONT      iv   SARAH, b. say 1646; m. by 1667 Thomas Huxley (on 26 March 1667 John Winthrop Jr. treated "Huxlye, Sarah, above 20 years, wife of Tho[mas] Huxly of Hartford & daughter of Sergeant Tho[mas] [S]penc[er] of Hartford" [WMJ 71
1 CONC 0]).
1 CONT      v    ELIZABETH, bp. 26 March 1648 [HaVR 579]; m. by about 1668 Samuel Andrews, son of William Andrews [TAG 35:55-59].
1 CONT      vi   JARED, b. say 1650; m. Hartford 22 December 1680 Hannah Pratt, daughter of John [HaVR Barbour 301].
1 CONT      vii  HANNAH, b. Hartford 15 April 1653 [CTVR 31]; said to have married (1) George Sexton, (2) Daniel Brainerd and (3) Jonathan Chapman, but the secondary sources are vague and uncertain, and the evidence for these marriages h
1 CONC as not been found.
1 CONT      viii MARY, b. Hartford 18 May 1655 [CTVR 32]; m. Westfield 7 October 1675 Thomas Root [Pynchon VR 31].
1 CONT      ix   MARTHA, b. Hartford 19 March 1657[/8] [CTVR 31]; m. by 1677 Andrew Benton (b. of first child), son of Andrew Benton [TAG 27:163; NEHGR 60:301-2 (evidence for this marriage not presented in either source)].
1 CONT ASSOCIATIONS:  Thomas Spencer was brother of WILLIAM SPENCER of Cambridge and Hartford, and of Michael Spencer and Gerard Spencer of Lynn [TAG 27:79-87, 161-65].  In this article Jacobus makes the three sons by the first wife of T
1 CONC homas Spencer slightly younger than we do here.
1 CONT
1 CONT COMMENTS:  On 7 September 1652 Thomas Spencer undertook to pay the fine of Sarah Spencer [RPCC 112].
0 @N6000000000658950446@ NOTE From Ancestral File (TM), data as of 2 January 1996.
0 @N6000000004972818295@ NOTE From p.356 of "The First Century of the History of Springfield: The Official Records from 1636 to 1736, with an Historical Review and Biographical Mention of the Founders" by Springfield (Mass.), Henry Martyn Burt; Published by H.
1 CONC M. Burt, 1898
1 CONT
1 CONT Found at: http://books.google.com/books?id=MOAWAAAAYAAJ
1 CONT
1 CONT At a Meeting of ye Select Men Dec: 26 1666, vizt Ens: Cooper Robert Ashley John Dumbleton Benj: Parsons & Eli: Holyoke John Sackett & Ambrose ffowler veiwers of the fences of the feild at Worronoco have this Summer past presented 
1 CONC diverse defects in the fences of ye feild there vizt Henry Glovers fence defective 12 rod John Williams fence defective 24 rod & Walter Lee his fence defective 10 rod for which tho the fynes according to Order would rise high, the
1 CONC  Select Men doe assess the Said persons as followeth vizt Henry Glover at 20s, John Williams at 35, Walt: Lee at 18s, these fynes ye 
1 CONT Townes share & to be added to your rates to the Town this winter: And for ye veiwers share It is left to them to moderate as they See cause. 
1 CONT
1 CONT ****************************************
1 CONT
1 CONT A Genealogical Memoir of the Descendants of Ambrose Fowler of Windso
1 CONT
1 CONT PUBLICATION: NEHG Register, Vol 11, July 1857, page 247
1 CONT
1 CONT Ambrose Fowler is supposed to be a son of William Fowler, one of the first magistrates of the New Haven Colony, and a first settler at Milford but this has not been proven. He appeared at Windsor, CT as early as 1640, where he was
1 CONC  one of Rev. John Warham's church who came from Dorchester, MA, and located at Windsor in 1636. Ambrose was one of a committee in 1641, Hartford Colony, to settle the bounds of Uncoway and Poquonnuck. His name is often met with in
1 CONC  the records of that town. He sold land at Windsor in 1671, soon after which he removed and settled with his children at Westfield, MA. In 1675 there is a notice of the burning of "Mr. Ambrose Fowler's house and barn" at Windsor. 
1 CONC His will was made in 1692, and proved in 1706, in which he names seven children, all recorded at Windsor.
0 @N6000000004972818320@ NOTE Jane Alvord was a gr.-dau. of John White, who came over in the Mayflower
1 CONT
1 CONT **********************************
1 CONT
1 CONT The Burke and Alvord Memorial 1864 by John Alonzo Boutelle pg 92
1 CONT This page can be viewed at: 
1 CONT
1 CONT "Historians seem to agree that Alexander and Benedict of Windsor, Conn., were brothers, and that Alexander's sister, Joan Alvord, married May 26, 1646 Ambrose Fowler.
1 CONT
1 CONT We suppose, from what records we are in possession of, that Alexander and Benedict Alvord emigrated from the county of Somerset, England, and that Benedict after coming over and patriotically volunteering to assist in defending Co
1 CONC nnecticut colony from the Pequots, returned to England after his espoused, Joan Newton (Nurton)."
