0 @N6000000001182805801@ NOTE He was a Vestryman for the Spesutia Angelican Church of Perryman, Maryland in 1724.  
1 CONT His will is dated June 2, 1739 and is on record at the Hall of Records in Annapolis.
1 CONT He was the father of 15 children.
1 CONT
1 CONT From about 1748 until the late 1750's, five of Garvis Gilbert's sons, Samuel, Garvis, Aquila, Benjamin and Daniel moved to Lunedburg (later Bedford and now Campbell) County, Virginia.  Two of his son, Michael and Charles, remained
1 CONC  in Baltimore County, Maryland; however, some of their children lated moved to Virginia.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1733, the northeastern end of Baltimore County, Maryland was carved off to establish the new county of Harford, with its county seat at Bel Air.  The coastal portion of the county contains the towns of Havre de Grace and Aberde
1 CONC en.  
1 CONT Most of Garvis Gilbert's property lay to the west of the town of Harve de Grace and to the south and east of Aberdeen, including the northern portion of the present Aberdeen Proving Grounds.  Descendants of Michael and Charles Gil
1 CONC bert inherited large portions of the original Garvis Gilbert estate.
0 @N6000000004881454129@ NOTE also : Gervase ,  Garvase,  Jarvis.....various spellings
0 @N6000000001167434649@ NOTE He came to America before 1705.
1 CONT From the Descendants of Garvis Gilbert "Austin Reed Spainhour".
1 CONT
1 CONT Garvis Gilbert (sometimes Gervais/Jarvis/Garvies/Garvis) may have been a son of Michael Gilbert and Dorothy Hargrave. If so, he was born 15 Apr 1680 at Leeds, Yorkshire, England, from Marcum, "The Way it Was with our Ancestors". W
1 CONC hile there has been no primary proof that Garvais who came to Maryland was their son, so much circumstantial evidence shows this to be true, that after careful analysis and intensive research by many different parties this is the 
1 CONC only reasonable conclusion that can be drawn.”
1 CONT
1 CONT From Henry C Peden Jr (707 Bedford Rd, Bel Air MD 21014), Gilbert Records in Harford County MD, Bel Air Copy Center, Bel Air MD, ©1986: The name of Garvis Gilbert (sometimes spelled Jarvis or Gervais) appears first in Baltimore (n
1 CONC ow Harford) County MD in 1699 in the Middle River Hundred. From "Taxables in Baltimore County", 1699, Maryland Historical Magazine, Vol 8, page 64.
1 CONT
1 CONT Baltimore County, Maryland Tax Lists (from LDS records) show the following: 1702-Jervis Gilbard, South Gunpowder Hd; 1703-Gervais Gilbert, South Gunpowder Hd; 1704-Jervis Gilbert, North Gunpowder Hd; 1705-Garvies Gilbert, North Gu
1 CONC npowder Hd; 1706-Jarvis Gilbert, North Gunpowder Hundred.
1 CONT
1 CONT 4 Nov 1707 - Marcum citing Baltimore County MD land deed records (Liber HW, No. B, folio 49): Garvis Gilbert purchased a tract of land located on the head of the middle branch of the Bush River in North Gunpowder Hundred, Baltimor
1 CONC e County MD from William Lowe and his wife Rebecca. According to Marcum this was the homestead of Garvis throughout his life. According to Davis (Baltimore County Land Records Vol RM:561-562), Garvis Gilbert paid 5000 pounds of to
1 CONC bacco to William Lough (Lowe) for the property and it was laid out as 100 acres, more or less, and contained plantation houses, edifices, buildings, fences, orchards, gardens, pastures and woods, became known as Lowe’s Lot and lay
1 CONC  near the line of the land called The Agreement.
1 CONT
1 CONT 22 Jan 1722 until 11 Apr 1726, Vestry records of Spesutia Church, St George’s Parish show that Garvis Gilbert was a Vestryman at the Spesutia Church.
1 CONT
1 CONT 1727 -  From Wilbur E Davis, "The Gilbert Family",In 1727 he (Garvis Gilbert) bought Hall’s Pipe, 145 acres on Swan Creek for 50 pounds Sterling. He also bought Clark’s Tobacco, 71 acres on Swan Creek.
1 CONT
1 CONT 1735 - Marcum citing Baltimore County MD land deed records (Lib. HWS, No. M, folio 302): Garvis Gilbert, “for the natural love and affection which I have and do bear unto my son Michael Gilbert,” gave Michael land to be released t
1 CONC o his brothers at the time of his (Garvis’) death: Clark’s Tobacco, 60 acres; Hall’s Pipe, 145 acres; Gilbert’s Chance, 40 acres.
1 CONT
1 CONT 2 Jun 1739/17 Jun 1739 - Marcum citing Baltimore County MD Prerogative Court Wills 22, page 79: The will of Gravis Gilbert of Baltimore County, planter, was dated June 2, 1739 , 4 days  later her was dead. His will was proved June
1 CONC  17, 1739. To his son Samuel he gave a tract called Gilbert’s Addition and part of Gilbert’s Outlet, joining to Edward Hall’s Road; To his son Garvis he gave Bow’s (Lowe’s) Lot and remainder of Outlet, joining; To his son Charles 
1 CONC he gave dwelling plantation Clark’s Tobacco, Gilbert’s Chance, and Negro Man, Oliver, after demise of wife; To his son Aquila he gave The Agreement and Negro woman, Charity, after demise of wife; To his son Benjamin he gave ten po
1 CONC unds of money when he became of age; to his son Daniel he gave ten pounds of money when he became of age; To his daughter Hannah he gave a cow, one feather bed and furniture, and Negro child, Oliver, after demise of wife; To his d
1 CONC aughters, Mary and Martha, he gave each ten pounds of money when they became age 16 or at their marriage. The executors of this will were his wife Mary and Michael Gilbert. Wit: Obd Pritchard, James Pritchard, and James Canell. (W
1 CONC ill dated 2 Jun 1739 and filed 10 Jun 1739 by Obadiah Pritchard and James Pritchard. Administration bond posted 7 Nov 1739 by the executors with John Durbin and Samuel Pritchard securities; estate administered 12 Aug 1740 by execs
1 CONC ). The date of death of Garvis Gilbert has been determined to be 5/6 Jun 1739.
1 CONT
1 CONT From about 1748 until the late 1750's five of Garvis Gilbert's sons, Samuel, Garvis, Aquilla, Benjamin and Daniel moved to Bedford (formerly Lunenburg and now Campbell) County,  Virginia.  Two of his sons, Michael and Charles, rem
1 CONC ained in Baltimore County, MD; however, some of their children later moved to Virginia.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1773 the northeastern end of Baltimore County, Maryland was carved off to establish the new county of Harford, with its county seat at Bel Air. The coastal portion of Hartford County contains the towns of Havre de Grace of Aber
1 CONC deen.
1 CONT
1 CONT Most of Garvis' property lay west of the town of Havre De Grace and to the south and east of Aberdeen,  including the northern portion of the present Aberdeen Proving Grounds.  Descedants of Garvis Gilbert's sons, Michael and Char
1 CONC les Gilbert, inherited large portions of the original Garvis Gilbert estate.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marcum citing Baltimore County MD land deed records: The property of Gravis Gilbert, dec’d, distributed to his sons by executor Michael Gilbert follow:
1 CONT
1 CONT 1740 (HWS, LS, 420) Samuel Gilbert, Clark’s Tobacco, 60 acres.
1 CONT
1 CONT 1740 (HWS, IA, 413) Jarvis Gilbert, Lowe’s Lot, 100 acres, part of Gilbert’s Outlet, 125 acres.
1 CONT
1 CONT 1742 (TB, C, 204) Charles Gilbert, Clarke’s Tobacco, 40 acres; Union, 60 acres; Gilbert’s Chance.
1 CONT
1 CONT 1753 (ABB, J, 17) Acquilla Gilbert, Hall’s Mill Run, 200 acres.
0 @N6000000003249251674@ NOTE From the Descendants of Garvis Gilbert "Austin Reed Spainhour".
