1 CONT Genealogy of the Descendants of Thomas Lord
1 CONT
1 CONT Page 1
1 CONT
1 CONT THOMAS LORD I
1 CONT
1 CONT THOMAS LORD was born in 1585, son of Richard and Joan Lord of Towcester, County of Northton, England.
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard Lord's will, dated May 30, 1610, probated February 7, 1611, is filed in the Archives of North Hants, Second Series, Second Volume V, folio 38, and is as follows:
1 CONT
1 CONT In the name of God Amen--30th Daye of Maye in the Yeare of our Lord God 1610--I Richard Lorde of Towcester in Co. of Northton, husbandman of whole mynde doe make this my last will and testament--my soull vnto Allmight God and my b
1 CONC odie to be buried in the Churchyard of Towcester--
1 CONT
1 CONT 1st I give towards the repare of the said parish church of Towcester xij d.
1 CONT
1 CONT Item I give to Elizabeth my Daughter x li. of currat money of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT Item I give and bequeath to Ellen my Daughter xxx li. of currant money of England to be paid vnto her by my Executor hereafter named in manner and forme following--viz. the one half thereof att her daie of marriage and the other h
1 CONC alf within twelve moneths after her said daie of marrige Yf she shall then be livinge And yf it shall happen that she shall marry with one Robert Marriot of Calcot yeom then my will is that the saide sume of xxx li. be made vpp xl
1 CONC  li. and to be paid her at the said daies before married by equall porcons But if she happen not to marrye then my will is that she shall have xxx li. oneli for her porcon to be paid to her within three yeares after my Decease.
1 CONT
1 CONT Item I give and bequeath to Alice my Daughter 30 li. to be paid vnto her by my executor the one half thereof at her daye of marrige and the other half within twelve moneths after her said daie of marrige yf she shall then be livin
1 CONC ge But if she the said Alice happen not to marrye then my will is that her said Legacye be paid to her within five yeares next after my decease
1 CONT
1 CONT Item I give & bequeath to Joan my wife the one half of all my goods and chattels whatsoever moveable except the long Table in my hall and the seelinge and benches about my house and my will is that she shall haue & enjoye During h
1 CONC er naturall lyfe (yf so long she keepe herself my widdowe) the chamber ouer the kitchen where she and I due lodge and third pt of the apples & onle wch shall growe yearely in the orchard belonging to the house wherein I now dwell 
1 CONC in Towcester
1 CONT
1 CONT Item I give & bequeath moreour to my said wife during her natural lyfe (& yf so longe she keepe hereself my widowe) out of my Land & tenemts & hereditamts in Towcester aforesaid the Yearlie sume and annuitie of fyve pounds of curr
1 CONC ant money of England to be paide vnto her by my Executor hereafter named his heires or Assignes yearly quarterlie by equall and even porcons Provided allwaies that she my said wife shall not claym any Dower or thirde out of my sai
1 CONC d lands ten'ts or hereditenaments.
1 CONT
1 CONT Page 2
1 CONT
1 CONT Item I give and bequeath to Thomas my sonne and to his heires and assignes foreuer all my Lands ten'ts & hereditaments whatwoeuer in Towcester and within this Realm of England . . that he shall instly and trulie prforme this my la
1 CONC st will & testement without fraude or deceipt And all the rest of my goods and cattels my Detts and Legacies paid & my funerall expenses p??formed I give and bequeath to my said sonne Thomas whom I Doe make & ordaine my sole Execu
1 CONC tor of this my Last will and testement but vtterly Denye all other former wills heretofore by me made giuen or bequeathed Provyded allwaies that if anie one of my said children Ellen Alice or Thomas Doe Decease their lyves before 
1 CONC they are to receive their saide Legacies, That then the Legacie of the one of them soe deceasinge shall remayne & be Due to the other two of them onely then survivinge But if any two of them shall happen to Decease as aforesaide t
1 CONC hat then my saide Daughter Elizabeth shall haue fifteen pounde of their Legacies proportionablye to be paid unto her yf she the said Elizabeth shall then be livinge My Legacie guift bequest, thinge or things els whatsoeur herein e
1 CONC xpressed to the contraric hereof in any wise notwithstanding.
1 CONT
1 CONT And fynally Doe earnestlie Desyre my wellbelowed friends Mr Henry Peddler and Thomas Pedder of East Purye in the Counte of Northton gent's and Paul Boughton of the same Toune clerk to be my supervisors of this my last will and tes
1 CONC tament And I Doe give and bequeath to each of them for their paynes to be taken ij s. vj. d. of currant English money.
1 CONT
1 CONT In witness whereof I have herevnto put my hand & Seale the daye and yeare first aboue wrytten.
1 CONT
1 CONT Signed: Richard Lord
1 CONT Sealed & Subscribed in the
1 CONT p'sence of vs
1 CONT Thomas Pedder
1 CONT Paul Boughton
1 CONT Richard Abbot
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard Lord was buried at Towcester October 16, 1610; his wife was buried there September 22, 1610. He was born about 1555, and they married about 1582.
1 CONT
1 CONT Elizabeth, born about 1583.
1 CONT Thomas,      "   "    1585.
1 CONT Ellen,       "   "    1587.
1 CONT Alice,       "   "    1590, married May 20, 1611, Richard Morris.
1 CONT Richard Lord had a brother William, whose will is recorded in Northampton Probate Registry, pp. 1560-66, folio 72. It would seem that Richard and William descended from the Lord family of Yelvertoft, County Northampton, England.
1 CONT
1 CONT Thomas Lord (1) married February 23, 1610-11, Dorothy Bird, daughter of Robert and Amy Bird of Towcester, England.
1 CONT
1 CONT The records of the St. Laurence Church, Towcester, show that Dorothy Bird was baptized May 25, 1588.
1 CONT
1 CONT There is also a record on page 25 of the ancient Marriage License Book of Petersborough (near Towcester), England, of the marriage license issued to Thomas Lord and Dorothy Bird on February 20, 1610-11.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Towcester Registers, 1561-1633, give the following baptisms:
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard Lord, baptized Jan. 5, 1611-12.
1 CONT Anne Lord,       "     Sept. 18, 1614.
1 CONT Thomas Lord,     "     Nov. 15, 1616.
1 CONT William Lord,    "     Dec. 27, 1618.
1 CONT Robert Lord,     "     May 12, 1620.
1 CONT John Lord,       "     Jan. 21, 1623-24.
1 CONT Aymie Lord,      "     Nov. 30, 1626.
1 CONT Dorothy Lord,    "     July 1, 1629.
1 CONT ("The Founding of New England" by Ernest Flagg; "Lords of Towcester" by Garvin L. Payne; "Essex Institute," Vol. LIV).
1 CONT
1 CONT Thomas Lord was a man of means, position and influence, and in 1632 he sent his eldest son, Richard, then about twenty-one years of age, to America. He settled at Newtown, Mass., which afterwards became Cambridge. In 1633, Governo
1 CONC r Haynes and The Rev. Thomas Hooker, friends of Thomas Lord, sailed for America with about two hundred passengers important to the colony, and it is thought possible that Richard Lord went in advance in order to select a place to 
1 CONC settle.
1 CONT
1 CONT From Hotten's "Original Lists," we learn that "on the 29th of April 1635 were registered for transportation from the port of London to New England, in the ship 'Elizabeth and Ann,' of which Capt. Robert Cooper was master, Thomas L
1 CONC ord, aged fifty; his wife, Dorothy, aged forty-six; and their children Thomas, aged sixteen; Ann, aged fourteen; William, aged twelve; John, aged ten; Robert, aged nine; Aymie, aged six; and Dorothy, aged four." They landed in Bos
1 CONC ton and joined Richard Lord at Newtown. The ages of the children as given on the ship's register are probably only approximate, as they vary from the actual baptismal records.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1636, with his entire family, Thomas Lord joined the party of Rev. Mr. Hooker and Mr. Stone and one hundred men, women and children, which took its departure from Newtown to form a new settlement on the Connecticut River.
1 CONT
1 CONT "They traveled more than a hundred miles, through a hideous and trackless wilderness to Hartford. They had no guide but their compass; and made their way over mountains, through swamps, thickets and rivers, which were passable wit
1 CONC h great difficulty. They had no cover but the heavens, and no lodgings but such as nature afforded them. They drove with them one hundred and sixty head of cattle and subsisted by the way on the milk of their cows. Mrs. Hooker was
1 CONC  borne through the wilderness on a litter. The people generally carried their packs, arms and some utensils. They were nearly a fortnight on their journey. This adventure was the more remarkable as many of this company were person
1 CONC s of figure, who in England had lived in honor, affluence and delicacy, and were strangers to fatigue and danger. Gov. Haynes and some others did not appear in the colony until 1637." (Trumbull's Memorial History of Hartford.)
1 CONT
1 CONT It was early in June when they reached their journey's end. Their first labor was to prepare their dugouts in the hillside and provide shelter for their cattle. They had for some time been close friends and neighbors in Newtown an
1 CONC d were already organized as a church, had been members of townships and were familiar, therefore, with action as a body. They agreed to purchase territory jointly and afterwards parcel it out, and Mr. Samuel Stone and Mr. William 
1 CONC Goodwin were appointed, in behalf of the proprietors, to treat for land with the tribe of Suckiage Indians, of whom at this time Sequassen was the Chief Sachem. In this they were successful and soon
1 CONT
1 CONT purchased a large area. It is not known what they bartered--probably cloth, axes, knives, etc. That a consideration was given, that it was increased when Sequassen confirmed the grant and was enlarged again when his heirs and succ
1 CONC essors renewed it "to near the value the land was esteemed at before the English came into these parts" is apparent from the deed of renewal itself. As soon as acquired, the land was distributed to the new proprietors.
1 CONT
1 CONT Thomas Lord thus became an original proprietor and one of the first settlens of Hartford. He lived on the north side of the highway on the bank of the Little River (now Wells Street), a near neighbor of Gov. Haynes, Rev. Mr. Hooke
1 CONC r, Mr. Goodwin, Gov. Wyllys and others of the prominent inhabitants. His sons, Richard and Thomas, had the lots next to his. The Hartford settlers were largely people of some culture cast into raw conditions, and there was a mingl
1 CONC ing of high breeding and rough life.
1 CONT
1 CONT Neither the date of death nor the place of burial of Thomas Lord (1) is known. There is an entry on the Hartford Town Votes, under date of January 29, 1643-4. The Governor, Mr. Hooker, and several others, including Thomas Lord, Sr
1 CONC ., and his son Thomas, were freed from "Common worck in the hyway" for the next three years. As this is the last reference to Thomas Lord, Sr., in the volume, it might be conjectured that he died shortly after that time. ("The Fou
1 CONC nding of New England," by Ernest Flagg.) The land records of Hartford show that Dorothy Lord owned lands as early as May 29, 1651, and that on March 7, 1652, she sold some land to Richard Goodman, and according to the laws in effe
1 CONC ct at that time (see Epaphroditus Peck's "The Property Rights of Husband and Wife under the Laws of Connecticut") a wife could not make contracts while her husband was living. The graves of Dorothy Lord and quite a number of desce
1 CONC ndants are in the graveyard in the rear of the First Church of Hartford, and the names of Thomas Lord and his son Richard are inscribed on the granite monument as among the first settlers. Dorothy Lord died in 1675 at the age of 8
1 CONC 6, and her will, dated February 8, 1669, is now on file among the probate records in the Connecticut State Library at Hartford, and is as follows:
1 CONT
1 CONT "In the Name of God Amen, I Dorathy Lord of Hartford in the colony of Connecticutt
1 CONT in New England, Being stricken in yeares, & at present labouring under some
1 CONT bodyly weaknesses; Though through the mercy of God, I at present haue ye use of
1 CONT my understanding & memorye, yet I know not how suddenly the Lord may put an end
1 CONT unto my fewe dayes in this life, & therefore according to my duty I am willing soe
1 CONT to setle & disspose of that little estate the Lord hath lent me, that peace may be continued
1 CONT amongst my children when I am gathered to my fathers, & In order thereunto
1 CONT I doe declare this as followeth to be my last will & Testament. First that all my
1 CONT just debts be pd out of my estate.
1 CONT I doe giue & Bequeath my now dwelling house & Barne & my Home lott & my lower
1 CONT lott in the North meadow unto the children of my son Thomas Lord deceased, at the
1 CONT age of 18 years & if any decease before they attayne that age the suruiuor or suruiuors
1 CONT to possess it, & if they all dye then my son Wm or his children to possess what is
1 CONT giuen to them.
1 CONT
1 CONT "Itt: I giue unto my daughter Amy Gilbert & her children Three Acres of Meadow or
1 CONT Swamp in my upper lott in the Long meadow next to that Mrs Olcott hath now in
1 CONT possession,
1 CONT
1 CONT "Itt I giue unto my son Robt: Lord (If he live after my decease so long as to have
1 CONT
1 CONT Page 5
1 CONT
1 CONT Notice of this my will) Three Acres of my upper lott adjoyneing to that which I haue
1 CONT giuen my Daughter Gilbert.
1 CONT
1 CONT "Itt I giue unto my son Wm Lord & his heires foreuer Two Acres in my great lott in the
1 CONT long meadow next adjoyneing to that which I haue giuen my son Robert,
1 CONT
1 CONT "Itt I giue unto my son John Lord Tenn pounds in Currant pay of this country,
1 CONT
1 CONT "Itt Whereas my Grandson Richd: Lord hath disbursed seuerall sums of money or country
1 CONT pay for the Building my chimneys & shingling my house & repayres about it, I
1 CONT doe for the payment of him, giue grant & confirme unto him & his heires foreuer; all
1 CONT that my meadow lott in the long meadow which abutts upon the great Riuer east the
1 CONT little riuer west Mr. Westwoods land North & Barth Barnards land south.
1 CONT
1 CONT "I doe also giue & bequeath unto my sd Grandson Richard Lord & his heires foreuer
1 CONT all the remaynder of my upper lott in the long meadow, which I haue not given to
1 CONT my sons Robert & Son Wm: & my daughter Gilbert & her children, he payeing
1 CONT this legacie hereafter exprest, to my sonn John Tenn pounds. And in case my sonn
1 CONT Robt; shall depart this life before he hath notice of this my last will, Then that
1 CONT Three Acres of Land giuen to him shall be deuided Between my Son Wm. & my
1 CONT Grandson Richd Lord, I doe allso confirme unto my Grandson: Richard Lord & his
1 CONT heires all my wood land that is all ready layd out or to be layd unto me wth in the
1 CONT Bounds of Hartford.
1 CONT
1 CONT "I giue unto my Grandchild Hanna Ingersall my youngest cowe & my other oowe I
1 CONT giue unto my Grandchildren Dorathy & Margery Ingersall.
1 CONT
1 CONT "I giue my moueable estate & Cattell to my son Wm Lord my grandson Richd Lord
1 CONT my daughter Stanton my daughter Gilbert & the children of my daughter Ingersall,
1 CONT the whole to be divided into fiue partes, & my daughter Ingersalls children to haue
1 CONT one part, & the rest of them, each of them one part.
1 CONT
1 CONT "I giue unto the wife of Nicholas Clarke Tenn shillings.
1 CONT
1 CONT "I doe ordayn & constitute my son Wm. & my grandson Richd: my executors, &
1 CONT desire my loueing Freind Mr. John Allyn to be ouere seer of this my will, & for
1 CONT the confirmation hereof I have hereunto sett my hand this 8th of February: 1669:
1 CONT
1 CONT Signed in presence of us
1 CONT JOHN ALLYN DORATHY LORD
1 CONT "STEUEN HOPKINS                                                  her marke"
1 CONT
1 CONT After the general distribution by the Will, a supplementary disposal of special articles was ordered by Dorothy Lord, as follows, in abstract:
